window.COURSE = {"title":"CDAIO Learning Hub","programme":"IIMV CDAIO Batch 2 · Pillar 1: Strategic Leadership","modules":[{"id":"leadership","title":"Leading in the AI Era","blurb":"Mindset, moods and making sense of a BANI world","lessons":["c03","c04"],"color":"#E8833A"},{"id":"ai-foundations","title":"AI and ML Foundations","blurb":"From the AI stack to transformers, tokens and model evaluation","lessons":["c05","c06","c07","c08"],"color":"#2F6FDE"},{"id":"ai-value","title":"Automation and AI Use Cases","blurb":"Agents vs RPA, and how to prioritise an AI portfolio","lessons":["c09","c10"],"color":"#7A4FD6"},{"id":"strategy","title":"Digital and Business Strategy","blurb":"Digital economics, Porter, frameworks and enterprise architecture","lessons":["c11","c12","c13","c14"],"color":"#0E9F8E"},{"id":"transformation","title":"Digital Transformation and Business Models","blurb":"Technology as enabler, hype cycles, platforms and digital leadership","lessons":["c15","c16","c17"],"color":"#D9488B"},{"id":"product","title":"Digital Product Management","blurb":"Product thinking, PMF, MVPs, product-led growth and governance","lessons":["c18","c19","c20","c21","c22"],"color":"#C9861A"},{"id":"responsible-ai","title":"The CDAIO and Responsible AI","blurb":"The Chief AI Officer, bias, explainability and trust","lessons":["c23","c24","c25"],"color":"#3C8D3F"},{"id":"privacy","title":"Data Sovereignty and Privacy Law","blurb":"AI sovereignty, GDPR and India's DPDP Act","lessons":["c26","c27","c28"],"color":"#C2453B"},{"id":"governance","title":"AI Governance, Regulation and Data Strategy","blurb":"Organisational AI governance, the EU AI Act and enterprise data strategy","lessons":["c29","c30","c31"],"color":"#1F7A99"}],"lessons":[{"id":"c03","num":3,"module":"leadership","title":"Interpersonal Leadership in the AI Era","subtitle":"Mindset, Internal Dialogue, Emotions and Moods","date":"5 April 2026","faculty":"Dr. L. K. Jena (OB & HR)","workshop":false,"hook":"Leaders in an AI-enabled workplace must shift from directive management to human-centric orchestration, and that starts with knowing yourself: your mindset, what you tell yourself, and the emotions and moods you carry into every decision.","sections":[{"h":"Recap: styles and spots","blocks":[{"p":"Class used the HBR leadership-style self-assessment: each participant identified a <strong>primary</strong> and a <strong>supplementary</strong> style, plus <strong>blind spots</strong> (what diminishes performance) and <strong>sweet spots</strong> (what to cultivate). Most of the 40 participants came out as Collaborators, followed by Pilots and Forecasters."},{"p":"Purpose of the module: <strong>explore the self</strong> (intrapersonal) to expand functional capacity, which then drives interpersonal, organisational and societal benefit."},{"img":"assets/figures/c03-1.png","title":"THE 8 LEADERSHIP STYLES (HBR SELF-ASSESSMENT) USED IN CLASS","alt":"1Collaborator 2Energizer 3Pilot 4Provider Builds consensus and Mobilises people and Strong conviction and Supplies structure, partnerships; most common creates momentum vision; steers the resources and support primary style in the batch direction 5Harmonizer 6Forecaster 7Producer 8Composer Keeps relationships and Scans the future, Drives execution and Synthesises ideas into a the team in balance anticipates trends delivery of outcomes coherent whole"}]},{"h":"Lever 1: Mindset - fixed, growth, and flexible","blocks":[{"p":"Fixed mindset is not &#x27;bad&#x27;; it is useful where rules, compliance or data protection matter (e.g. manufacturing, sensitive data). Growth mindset is essential for transformation and innovation."},{"p":"Seniority tends to harden a fixed mindset (&#x27;success after success, so I must be right&#x27;). Antidotes shared by"},{"p":"participants: <strong>stop talking and actively listen</strong>, whiteboard sessions, sharing expectations openly, giving teams a 3-minute pitch and a free hand."},{"p":"Goal: a <strong>flexible mindset</strong> that is fixed on vision and goals and flexible on the path. Being agile does not mean always growth; being decisive does not mean always fixed."},{"img":"assets/figures/c03-2.png","title":"FIXED VS GROWTH MINDSET (CAROL DWECK)","alt":"Fixed mindset Growth mindset My way is the highway; claims credit Desire to learn and embrace change Avoids challenges and opportunities to learn Persists in the face of setbacks vs Discourages questions, so information stops flowing Invites divergent views, active listening Useful for compliance, data protection, Essential for transformation and innovation process-heavy work"}]},{"h":"From directive management to human-centric orchestration","blocks":[{"p":"Faculty research (198 HR and talent-acquisition professionals in Indian IT firms, 2023) found leaders must address <strong>AI</strong> <strong>anxiety</strong> (job displacement, role change, reduced autonomy), build <strong>psychological safety</strong>, and offer <strong>individualised</strong> <strong>coaching</strong>."},{"p":"Key enablers: <strong>radical transparency</strong>, <strong>communicating the &#x27;why&#x27;</strong> of AI adoption, active listening and dialogue, and <strong>democratising information</strong> to build trust across generations."},{"p":"Adopt a <strong>player-coach mindset</strong> (stay curious and &#x27;AI-literate&#x27; about both possibilities and limitations) and practise servant and empowering leadership."}]},{"h":"Five interpersonal skills for an AI-enabled workplace","blocks":[{"p":"AI automates routine tasks, so leaders must move teams from process ownership to strategic, high-value work. What AI lacks: genuine emotion, emotional nuance, contextual judgment."},{"p":"<strong>Hybrid decision-making:</strong> position AI as a decision partner, not a replacement. The leader becomes a <strong>decision architect</strong> who calibrates trust between human judgment and AI recommendations, with ethical oversight."},{"p":"Redefine emotional intelligence as <strong>me + technology + my people</strong>; blend rational and emotional needs; build critical thinking to interrogate and contextualise AI outputs."},{"img":"assets/figures/c03-3.png","title":"HUMAN-CENTRIC ORCHESTRATION: FIVE INTERPERSONAL SKILLS","alt":"Empathy: handle AI anxiety, extend trust Storytelling: insight into Collaboration: connect tech and action business Human-centric orchestration Ethical judgment: fairness, Adaptability: team resilience transparency"}]},{"h":"Lever 2: Internal dialogue","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>Limiting narratives:</strong> &#x27;I&#x27;m not the right person&#x27;, &#x27;I don&#x27;t want to rock the boat&#x27;, &#x27;I need more qualifications first&#x27;, perfectionism, impostor syndrome, second-guessing."},{"p":"<strong>Opportunity narratives:</strong> &#x27;It&#x27;s OK to be uncomfortable while learning&#x27;, expect divergent opinions, ask open-ended questions, take calculated risks, don&#x27;t let perfect be the enemy of good, separate facts from fiction."},{"p":"Know your &#x27;lid&#x27; (Maxwell&#x27;s Law of the Lid), coach yourself with focused questions, and embrace <strong>reverse mentoring</strong> (e.g. Nestle) with younger generations."}]},{"h":"Lever 3: Emotions and moods","blocks":[{"p":"Emotions predispose how we think, act and communicate; moods are contagious and spread through a team. Check in with your own state of mind and your team&#x27;s several times a day."},{"p":"Common workplace moods today: confusion about where AI fits, fear of layoffs, insecurity and overwhelm. Leaders should cultivate the productive counterparts."},{"p":"<strong>Curiosity + acceptance = ambition</strong>; courage + acceptance = authenticity; ambition + acceptance = flexibility and open-mindedness."},{"img":"assets/figures/c03-4.png","title":"UNPRODUCTIVE VS PRODUCTIVE MOODS (GLORIA FLORES)","alt":"Unproductive moods Productive moods Confusion, resignation, frustration Curiosity, acceptance, perseverance Fear, anxiety, insecurity, overwhelm Patience, ambition, resolution → Arrogance, impatience, boredom Confidence and trust Distrust and skepticism Curiosity + acceptance = ambition"}]}],"takeaways":["Know your primary and supplementary style, and work on your blind spots while leveraging sweet spots.","Cultivate a flexible mindset: fixed on vision, growth-oriented on the path.","Shift from directive management to human-centric orchestration of people and AI.","Treat AI as a decision partner; you are the decision architect with ethical oversight.","Watch your internal dialogue: replace limiting narratives with opportunity narratives.","Pick one productive mood (e.g. curiosity) and practise it daily for the next 30 days.","Lead with the 3 Cs: Character, Competence and Commitment (mission first, people always).","Stay HUMBLE: Honesty, a sense of the Universe beyond self, Modesty, Belief."],"quote":"Everything rises and falls on leadership.","quoteBy":"John C. Maxwell","video":"media/videos/class-03.mp4","videoSecs":530,"notes":"media/notes/class-03-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"According to the class, when is a fixed mindset actually useful?","a":"Where rules, compliance or data protection matter","w":["When leading a transformation programme","When the team needs to innovate fast","Never: it should always be replaced by a growth mindset"],"x":"A fixed mindset is not 'bad': it suits process-heavy, compliance or data-protection work. Growth mindset is essential for transformation and innovation."},{"q":"What is the 'flexible mindset' the class recommends?","a":"Fixed on vision and goals, flexible on the path","w":["Flexible on goals, fixed on the process","Always growth-oriented in every situation","Switching styles based on seniority"],"x":"Being agile does not mean always growth; being decisive does not mean always fixed."},{"q":"In a hybrid decision model, what role does the leader play?","a":"Decision architect who calibrates trust between human judgment and AI","w":["Approver who signs off whatever AI recommends","Data scientist who tunes the model","Observer who lets AI decide routine and strategic matters"],"x":"AI is positioned as a decision partner, not a replacement, with the leader providing ethical oversight."},{"q":"Which combination produces ambition, according to the moods framework?","a":"Curiosity + acceptance","w":["Courage + acceptance","Patience + perseverance","Confidence + trust"],"x":"Curiosity + acceptance = ambition; courage + acceptance = authenticity; ambition + acceptance = flexibility."},{"q":"Which of these is a 'limiting narrative' in internal dialogue?","a":"'I need more qualifications first'","w":["'It's OK to be uncomfortable while learning'","'Don't let perfect be the enemy of good'","'Expect divergent opinions'"],"x":"Limiting narratives include impostor syndrome, perfectionism and 'I'm not the right person'. The others are opportunity narratives."},{"q":"What does HUMBLE stand for in the class takeaways?","a":"Honesty, a sense of the Universe beyond self, Modesty, Belief","w":["Humility, Understanding, Mastery, Balance, Leadership, Empathy","Hope, Unity, Mission, Belief, Learning, Energy","Honesty, Usefulness, Motivation, Brilliance, Loyalty, Ethics"],"x":"Stay HUMBLE: Honesty, a sense of the Universe beyond self, Modesty, Belief."}],"cards":[{"f":"The 3 Cs of leadership","b":"Character, Competence and Commitment (mission first, people always)."},{"f":"Blind spots vs sweet spots","b":"Blind spots diminish performance; sweet spots are strengths to cultivate."},{"f":"Five interpersonal skills for human-centric orchestration","b":"Empathy, collaboration, ethical judgment, adaptability and storytelling."},{"f":"Maxwell's Law of the Lid","b":"Your leadership ability sets the 'lid' on your effectiveness and your team's."},{"f":"Reverse mentoring","b":"Senior leaders learn from younger colleagues (e.g. Nestle), useful for staying AI-literate."},{"f":"What AI lacks","b":"Genuine emotion, emotional nuance and contextual judgment."},{"f":"Player-coach mindset","b":"Stay curious and AI-literate about both possibilities and limitations while coaching others."}]},{"id":"c04","num":4,"module":"leadership","title":"AI for Leaders","subtitle":"The BANI World, AI Hype vs Reality and Second-Order Thinking","date":"12 April 2026","faculty":"Programme Co-Director (Information Systems)","workshop":false,"hook":"Before building an AI strategy, a Chief Digital & AI Officer must make sense of a brittle, anxious, non-linear world, cut through hype and 'AI washing', and learn to anticipate the second- and third-order effects of technology.","sections":[{"h":"Learning as a senior executive: the praxis frame","blocks":[{"p":"The module opens the AI and Generative AI for Leaders track, before moving to digital transformation strategy. Learning at senior level combines <strong>theory</strong> (frameworks, models), <strong>action</strong> (demos, tools) and, most importantly, <strong>reflection</strong> after each session."},{"p":"Without reflection, a programme is as passive as watching a video; each participant should pick the takeaways most relevant to their own domain."}]},{"h":"Making sense of today's world: VUCA to BANI","blocks":[{"p":"VUCA (Volatile, Uncertain, Complex, Ambiguous) came from the US military in the 1980s. COVID triggered a new lens: <strong>BANI</strong>, Brittle, Anxious, Non-linear, Incomprehensible."},{"p":"Design with BANI in mind: build resilience into IT architecture and supply chains, and remember that people adopting new tech (AI, AR/VR, neurotech) will be anxious."},{"img":"assets/figures/c04-1.png","title":"FROM VUCA TO BANI - AND HOW LEADERS RESPOND","alt":"Brittle Anxious Non-linear 1234Incomprehensible Looks strong but breaks Anxiety at individual and Unicorns without profit; Drowning in data, yet hard easily (COVID supply organisational level (AI careers jump or collapse. to make sense of events. chains). Respond: build disruption). Respond: Respond: context, Respond: transparency and capacity and resilience by empathy and mindfulness adaptability, flexibility intuition"}]},{"h":"Think beyond the obvious: science fiction and the arc of invention","blocks":[{"p":"HBR: <strong>business leaders should read more science fiction</strong>. It presents plausible alternate realities, shows how fragile and how <strong>malleable</strong> the status quo is, and inspired real products (Asimov&#x27;s Foundation, Kindle). Firms like Apple and Google consult sci-fi writers."},{"p":"Arthur C. Clarke: &#x27;Any sufficiently advanced technology is indistinguishable from magic.&#x27; Language, the trait that sets humans apart, is now being mastered by machines through LLMs."},{"img":"assets/figures/c04-2.png","title":"INDUSTRIAL REVOLUTIONS AND THEIR CIVILISATIONAL SHIFT","alt":"1.0 Steam 2.0 Electricity 3.0 Computers 4.0 AI & IoT Mechanical looms, steam Mass production; growth no IT, automation; borders and Data-driven; decision power longer tied to rivers geography dissolve support to decision automation"}]},{"h":"AI hype vs reality","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>AI washing:</strong> ~40% of European &#x27;AI start-ups&#x27; didn&#x27;t use AI; the US SEC fined firms for misleading AI claims. MIT NANDA: ~<strong>95% of GenAI projects fail</strong>, mostly not technically but on budget, timeline and undelivered value; many never scale beyond the pilot."},{"p":"Adoption is socio-technical (<strong>TOE framework</strong>: Technology, Organisation, Environment). Don&#x27;t use technology for its own sake: Juicero raised ~$120M for an over-engineered juicer that hands could replace. Blockchain is &#x27;a great solution still looking for a problem&#x27;."},{"p":"<strong>Amara&#x27;s Law:</strong> we overestimate a technology&#x27;s effect in the short run and underestimate it in the long run (dot-com, now GenAI). Use value-chain analysis and opportunity cost to judge AI investments."},{"img":"assets/figures/c04-3.png","title":"WHO'S WHO IN AI: MAKERS, SHAPERS, TAKERS ... AND FAKERS","alt":"1Makers 2Shapers 3Takers 4Fakers Researchers, scientists, Strategists and policy End users who apply Hype creators using innovators who build makers who set AI policy, ready-made AI tools to buzzwords without modelsandalgorithms guardrailsandadoption businessneeds substance;fuelAIwashing"}]},{"h":"Second- and third-order thinking","blocks":[{"p":"Great leaders anticipate effects beyond the obvious, like washing machines used to churn lassi. Class exercise on GenAI: banking (fraud, deepfake scams, then erosion of trust), real estate (offices giving way to data centres; servers moved closer to exchanges), airlines (sovereign AIs that must"},{"p":"interoperate), customer experience (AI exposing root causes; AI as counsellor)."},{"p":"Long-run effects may rewrite economics: labour as a factor of production shrinks; ideas like Universal Basic or <strong>Universal Creative Income</strong>; GenAI may erode critical thinking (&#x27;Is Google making us stupid?&#x27;)."},{"img":"assets/figures/c04-4.png","title":"FIRST-, SECOND- AND THIRD-ORDER EFFECTS: UPI IN INDIA","alt":"1st order 2nd order 3rd order Fast, convenient, safe alternative to Smartphones at bottom of pyramid; cyber UPI becomes a global diplomacy tool, cash fraud; lending and gaming apps; toffee copied abroad makers hit"}]},{"h":"What a CDAIO must do","blocks":[{"p":"AI must not be a black box. Separate <strong>traditional AI</strong> use cases from <strong>GenAI</strong> ones (&#x27;wisdom is knowing many problems are still better solved by traditional AI&#x27;)."},{"p":"Stay confident, keep your <strong>hands dirty</strong> with tools, and speak each stakeholder&#x27;s language: tech, business, legal and the board."},{"img":"assets/figures/c04-5.png","title":"AI MEANS DIFFERENT THINGS TO DIFFERENT PEOPLE","alt":"Statistician: maths & models Psychologist: cognitive decline Engineer: GPUs, servers, data The CDAIO connects the dots Social scientist: bias, divide Business: automation & ROI Legal: GDPR, DPDP, compliance"}]}],"takeaways":["Reflect after every session; learning at senior level happens through reflection.","Use BANI to design for resilience, empathy, context and transparency.","Read science fiction to think beyond the obvious and imagine alternate futures.","Beware fakers and AI washing; most GenAI failures are about value, not code.","Amara's Law: short-run hype, long-run underestimation.","Always map first-, second- and third-order effects of a technology.","Choose traditional AI vs GenAI deliberately; weigh ROI and opportunity cost.","Keep your hands dirty and wear the right hat for each stakeholder."],"quote":"Any sufficiently advanced technology is indistinguishable from magic.","quoteBy":"Arthur C. Clarke","video":"media/videos/class-04.mp4","videoSecs":519,"notes":"media/notes/class-04-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"What does BANI stand for?","a":"Brittle, Anxious, Non-linear, Incomprehensible","w":["Bold, Agile, Networked, Intelligent","Brittle, Ambiguous, Neutral, Inconsistent","Balanced, Adaptive, Novel, Integrated"],"x":"BANI emerged after COVID as a successor lens to VUCA (Volatile, Uncertain, Complex, Ambiguous)."},{"q":"Amara's Law says we tend to...","a":"Overestimate a technology's effect in the short run and underestimate it in the long run","w":["Underestimate a technology in the short run and overestimate it in the long run","Double computing power every 18 months","Value networks by the square of their users"],"x":"The dot-com era and GenAI both illustrate Amara's Law."},{"q":"According to MIT NANDA, why do most GenAI projects fail?","a":"Mostly on budget, timeline and undelivered value, not technology","w":["The models are not accurate enough","Lack of GPUs","Regulation blocks deployment"],"x":"~95% fail, many never scaling beyond the pilot; adoption is socio-technical (TOE framework)."},{"q":"In the 'who's who in AI', who fuels AI washing?","a":"Fakers","w":["Makers","Shapers","Takers"],"x":"Fakers are hype creators using buzzwords without substance."},{"q":"UPI becoming a global diplomacy tool is an example of what?","a":"A third-order effect","w":["A first-order effect","A second-order effect","Amara's Law"],"x":"1st: fast payments; 2nd: smartphones at the bottom of the pyramid, fraud, lending apps; 3rd: global diplomacy tool."},{"q":"What does the TOE framework say adoption depends on?","a":"Technology, Organisation and Environment","w":["Time, Operations and Execution","Talent, Output and Efficiency","Tools, Outcomes and Ethics"],"x":"Adoption is socio-technical, not just about the technology."}],"cards":[{"f":"VUCA","b":"Volatile, Uncertain, Complex, Ambiguous: a US military lens from the 1980s."},{"f":"How to respond to 'Brittle'","b":"Build capacity and resilience (e.g. supply chains, IT architecture)."},{"f":"AI washing","b":"Claiming to use AI without substance; ~40% of European 'AI start-ups' didn't use AI."},{"f":"Makers, Shapers, Takers","b":"Makers build models; Shapers set policy and guardrails; Takers apply ready-made tools."},{"f":"Praxis frame for senior learning","b":"Theory + action + reflection after every session."},{"f":"Arthur C. Clarke's quote","b":"'Any sufficiently advanced technology is indistinguishable from magic.'"},{"f":"Juicero lesson","b":"Don't use technology for its own sake: ~$120M for a juicer hands could replace."}]},{"id":"c05","num":5,"module":"ai-foundations","title":"AI Fundamentals for Leaders","subtitle":"Intelligence, the AI-ML- Deep Learning-GenAI Stack and How Models Are Built","date":"19 April 2026","faculty":"Programme Co-Director (Information Systems)","workshop":false,"hook":"A Chief Digital & AI Officer does not need to code models, but must share a precise vocabulary with the tech team: what intelligence and AI are, where machine learning, deep learning and GenAI fit, what a 'model' really is, and how a sound modelling project is run and evaluated.","sections":[{"h":"Opening: stay informed, but avoid the filter bubble","blocks":[{"p":"Recent news (new frontier models, Gartner/MIT data on failing GenAI projects) prompted a reminder: senior leaders must form <strong>independent views</strong>. Headline apps and personalised feeds create <strong>filter</strong> <strong>bubbles</strong> and a narrative set by Big Tech. Diversify sources and read full articles."},{"p":"Build a <strong>shared vocabulary</strong> in your organisation so tech teams, business, vendors and the board mean the same thing by &#x27;AI&#x27;, &#x27;model&#x27; or &#x27;training&#x27;."}]},{"h":"What is intelligence, and what is AI?","blocks":[{"p":"Don&#x27;t fall into the intelligence-vs-consciousness debate popular in the media. <strong>Intelligence</strong> = an agent&#x27;s general ability to achieve goals in a wide range of environments, whether human, animal or machine. There are many intelligences: linguistic, spatial, musical, interpersonal, emotional, not just IQ."},{"p":"<strong>AI</strong> (John McCarthy, 1956) = the science and engineering of making intelligent machines. It borrows from human biology but need not be limited by it (e.g. seeing infrared)."},{"p":"<strong>Turing test:</strong> most computer scientists now believe modern LLMs pass it. The business question is the <strong>reverse Turing test</strong>: CAPTCHA, which also makes users label data for free."}]},{"h":"How managers spend time, and three AI business needs","blocks":[{"p":"Survey: managers spend ~50% of time on administration and coordination, ~30% on problem-solving and collaboration, ~10% on strategy and innovation, ~7% on developing people and stakeholders. AI should shift time toward the last three."},{"p":"Most AI use cases fall into three needs. Personalisation brings a paradox: AI-written emails all read alike (&#x27;a copy of a copy&#x27;), so emotion and trust are lost. In health, AI-generated reports increasingly carry disclaimers."},{"p":"<strong>Domain experts</strong> (doctors, educators, engineers), not Big Tech, should set the narrative for how AI is used in their field (TPACK: content, pedagogy, technology in education)."},{"img":"assets/figures/c05-1.png","title":"THREE BUSINESS NEEDS AI SERVES","alt":"1Gain insight 2Engage 3Automate Data analysis, prediction, Hyper-personalised engagement with Business process automation: RPA to pattern-finding (e.g. engine-sensor customers and employees; intelligent, multi-agent automation anomaly detection) computer-generated communication"}]},{"h":"Decision support vs decision automation","blocks":[{"p":"Where the cost of error is high (aero engines, healthcare), AI acts as a <strong>decision support system</strong>, with human experts keeping final authority. Example: a borescope image-filtering tool was paused over safety concerns, while an engine-data tool now cuts report time from a week to minutes, with an SME check."},{"p":"Analytics project vs AI/ML project: analytics delivers a <strong>report</strong> with insights; AI/ML delivers a <strong>software</strong> <strong>module</strong> embedded in a larger application (e.g. face recognition in DigiYatra)."}]},{"h":"The AI stack and what a model is","blocks":[{"p":"Not all AI is ML (expert systems, IVR menus). ML, popular for 15-20 years, discovers patterns &#x27;without being explicitly programmed&#x27;. Deep learning uses multi-layer neural networks, made practical by GPUs (AlexNet). GenAI creates content, and advances in NLP and vision quickly cross over."},{"p":"A <strong>model</strong> is a mathematical description of a pattern, e.g. salary = beta0 + beta1 x experience. <strong>Training</strong> means finding parameters (weights) that minimise error, whether 2 parameters or billions in an LLM. Often <strong>traditional ML</strong> beats GenAI for a defined task (e.g. credit risk). Consider small language models (SLMs) and owning your AI rather than renting it."},{"img":"assets/figures/c05-2.png","title":"THE AI STACK: EACH LAYER IS A SUBSET OF THE ONE BEFORE","alt":"AI Machine learning Deep learning Generative AI Incl. rule-based expert Learns logic from data Multi-layer neural nets, Creates new text, images, systems (if-then logic) (1959, Samuel) GPUs (2014+) audio, video"}]},{"h":"Running a modelling project well","blocks":[{"p":"Business owners should define the <strong>evaluation metric</strong>; there is no universal accuracy threshold, as it depends on what is at stake. A 1% accuracy gain may not justify its cost. Models <strong>drift</strong> as behaviour changes and need retraining."},{"p":"Ask your team: did you check for under/overfitting? Did you hold out test data and use k-fold cross-validation? Are you using real production data? More data does not always mean a better model."},{"img":"assets/figures/c05-3.png","title":"UNDERFITTING VS OVERFITTING","alt":"Underfitting Overfitting Model misses the underlying pattern Model learns noise and outliers Poor even on training data Great in testing, poor in the real world vs Too simple for the problem Fix: clean data, train/test split, k-fold cross-validation"}]}],"takeaways":["Avoid the filter bubble: diversify news sources and form your own view.","Build a shared AI vocabulary across tech, business and the board.","Intelligence = ability to achieve goals; skip the consciousness debate.","Map every AI use case to insight, engagement or automation.","Let domain experts, not vendors, drive AI's narrative in your industry.","AI > ML > deep learning > GenAI; GenAI is not always the right tool.","Use CRISP-DM; business must own the evaluation metric.","Watch for overfitting, drift and the pilot-to-production gap."],"quote":"If you cannot measure it, you cannot manage it.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-05.mp4","videoSecs":497,"notes":"media/notes/class-05-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"How does the class define intelligence?","a":"An agent's general ability to achieve goals in a wide range of environments","w":["The ability to pass the Turing test","Consciousness plus reasoning","A high IQ score"],"x":"Skip the consciousness debate; intelligence is goal achievement across environments."},{"q":"Which ordering of the AI stack is correct, from broadest to narrowest?","a":"AI > Machine learning > Deep learning > Generative AI","w":["Machine learning > AI > Deep learning > GenAI","Deep learning > Machine learning > AI > GenAI","GenAI > Deep learning > Machine learning > AI"],"x":"Each layer is a subset of the one before. Not all AI is ML (e.g. rule-based expert systems)."},{"q":"What does 'training' a model mean?","a":"Finding parameters (weights) that minimise error","w":["Writing if-then rules by hand","Collecting more data","Deploying the model to production"],"x":"Whether 2 parameters or billions in an LLM, training finds the weights that minimise error."},{"q":"A model that is great in testing but poor in the real world is most likely...","a":"Overfitting","w":["Underfitting","Drifting","Well-generalised"],"x":"Overfitting learns noise and outliers. Fixes: clean data, train/test split, k-fold cross-validation."},{"q":"Who should define the evaluation metric for a modelling project?","a":"The business owner","w":["The data scientist","The vendor","There is a universal accuracy threshold"],"x":"There's no universal threshold: it depends on what is at stake."},{"q":"In high cost-of-error domains (aero engines, healthcare), AI should act as...","a":"A decision support system with human experts keeping final authority","w":["A fully automated decision maker","A replacement for subject-matter experts","A reporting dashboard only"],"x":"Decision support vs decision automation depends on the cost of error."}],"cards":[{"f":"Three business needs AI serves","b":"Gain insight, engage, automate."},{"f":"Reverse Turing test","b":"CAPTCHA: proving you're human, which also makes users label data for free."},{"f":"What is a model?","b":"A mathematical description of a pattern, e.g. salary = beta0 + beta1 x experience."},{"f":"Analytics project vs AI/ML project","b":"Analytics delivers a report with insights; AI/ML delivers a software module embedded in an application."},{"f":"Underfitting","b":"Model too simple; misses the underlying pattern and is poor even on training data."},{"f":"Model drift","b":"Behaviour changes over time so models degrade and need retraining."},{"f":"CRISP-DM","b":"A standard process for running data-mining/modelling projects, with business owning the metric."}]},{"id":"c06","num":6,"module":"ai-foundations","title":"Machine Learning Problem Types and Model Evaluation","subtitle":"Regression, Classification, Clustering, Metrics and Bias","date":"26 April 2026","faculty":"Programme Co-Director (Information Systems)","workshop":false,"hook":"A future Chief AI Officer must look at any business problem and say what kind of ML problem it is (supervised or unsupervised, regression or classification), how the model's quality should be measured, and whether the data behind it is balanced and fair.","sections":[{"h":"Opening: careers, domain knowledge and AI assurance","blocks":[{"p":"Non-technical professionals worried about relevance were advised to <strong>build visible IP</strong>: patents, papers, conference talks and a GitHub or Hugging Face portfolio."},{"p":"Security is often seen as a roadblock and creates technical debt when skipped; <strong>AI audit and AI assurance</strong> are fast-growing roles. <strong>Domain knowledge</strong> remains the differentiator: technical skills are easier to hire than deep understanding of the business."}]},{"h":"Supervised learning: regression and classification","blocks":[{"p":"Recap of the AI to ML to deep learning to GenAI stack and CRISP-DM. Traditional ML still dominates <strong>edge AI</strong>, where compute is limited."},{"p":"<strong>Supervised</strong> learning needs labels (ground truth, Y) plus attributes (features, X)."},{"p":"<strong>Regression</strong> predicts a continuous number: energy use, property prices, customer lifetime value, disease progression, demand, employee performance."},{"p":"<strong>Classification</strong> predicts a category: churn, credit risk, fraud, readmission, attrition, purchase intent, equipment failure, phishing. Problems can be <strong>reframed</strong>: predicting a stock price (regression) can become predicting up or down (classification); a fruit&#x27;s weight from an image is regression, its type is classification."},{"img":"assets/figures/c06-1.png","title":"FRAMING A PREDICTION PROBLEM","alt":"Supervised: Labels (ground truth) available Reframing: Same problem can be Regression: Predict a number: recast, e.g. stock up/down price, demand, CLV, weight Business problem Clustering: Group similar Classification: Predict a objects: segments, anomalies class: churn, fraud, go/no-go Unsupervised: No labels: let patterns emerge"}]},{"h":"Case: classifying mortgage documents","blocks":[{"p":"A traditional classifier can sort documents cheaply; a <strong>GenAI conversational layer</strong> on top improves the experience. Decide with guardrails, human-in-the-loop, cost, volume, AI maturity and the risk of <strong>shadow IT</strong>."},{"p":"&#x27;You going to AI&#x27; (separate tools) vs <strong>&#x27;AI coming to you&#x27;</strong> (embedded in Excel, Word): startups that are thin wrappers risk being absorbed."}]},{"h":"Unsupervised learning: clustering","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>Clustering</strong> groups objects so that members of a group are more similar to each other than to other groups, using all attributes together (height and weight, colour, size, markers)."},{"p":"Humans usually <strong>name the clusters</strong> with domain knowledge (e.g. utilities: high sales and low fuel cost = &#x27;high performers&#x27;); GenAI now helps with auto-labelling and topic naming."},{"p":"Uses: customer segmentation (STP), taxonomy in biology, land use, document grouping, <strong>anomaly and fraud detection</strong> without labels."},{"p":"RFP go/no-go: a classifier on past bids gives <strong>decision support</strong>; GenAI frames the reasoning; agents may later automate parts."}]},{"h":"AI as a general purpose technology","blocks":[{"p":"Like electricity, AI will become a <strong>general purpose technology</strong>. Today most firms still treat AI as isolated point solutions. To scale, leaders must <strong>re-imagine the enterprise IT stack</strong> as a boundaryless, interoperable system of machines, employees, customers and partners."},{"p":"Use-case portfolios will be sorted later into quick wins, champions, reassess and unsuitable cases. Culture matters: what works in one company can fail in another."}]},{"h":"Evaluating regression models","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>MAE</strong> (mean absolute error): the average size of the gap between actual (Y) and predicted (Y-hat), ignoring direction. It can <strong>never be negative</strong>, a quick interview test for data scientists."},{"p":"<strong>RMSE</strong> (root mean square error) squares errors, so it <strong>penalises large errors</strong>; outliers should usually be handled in data preparation. R-squared and others are in the slides."}]},{"h":"Evaluating classification models","blocks":[{"p":"The <strong>confusion matrix</strong> compares actual and predicted classes: TP, TN (correct), FP (Type I) and FN (Type II)."},{"p":"<strong>Accuracy</strong> = (TP + TN) / all cases. It is simple but <strong>deceptive</strong>, yet it often appears in RFPs and vendor contracts."},{"p":"Life-on-planets example: 1,000 planets, one with life; a model that says &#x27;no life&#x27; everywhere is <strong>99.9% accurate and worthless</strong>. Pick <strong>recall</strong> when false negatives are costly and <strong>precision</strong> when false positives are costly; F1 balances both. Define clearly what &#x27;positive&#x27; means."},{"p":"Participants applied this: re-classifying DevOps alerts cut maintenance cost by about 30%; an RFP force-majeure penalty clause was a flagged risk."},{"p":"Metrics keep evolving (novelty, diversity, serendipity, repeat-recommendation utility); leaders may <strong>create and validate their</strong> <strong>own</strong>."},{"img":"assets/figures/c06-2.png","title":"THE CONFUSION MATRIX (ACTUAL VS PREDICTED)","alt":"True positive 12False positive (Type I) Model says yes, reality yes (correct) Model says yes, reality no 3False negative (Type II) 4True negative Model says no, reality yes Model says no, reality no (correct)"}]},{"h":"Bias from imbalanced data","blocks":[{"p":"Amazon&#x27;s CV screening model was biased against women because <strong>historical hiring data</strong> was mostly male; proxies such as &#x27;captain of the women&#x27;s team&#x27; leak gender."},{"p":"Early face and gender models were far less accurate for women and ethnic minorities."},{"p":"Ask your team: is the data imbalanced, and how is it handled? Options: <strong>undersampling, oversampling, SMOTE, ensembles</strong> and the right metrics. Document it."},{"img":"assets/figures/c06-3.png","title":"FROM IMBALANCED DATA TO FAIRER MODELS","alt":"Check balance Undersample Oversample SMOTE Right metrics + ensembles e.g. 90% male CVs in Shrink the majority Repeat or add Synthesise minority Then document what history class minority records samples was done"}]}],"takeaways":["First ask: are labels available? That decides supervised vs unsupervised.","Regression predicts numbers; classification predicts categories; problems can be reframed.","Clustering finds groups without labels; humans or GenAI name them.","Traditional ML for decisions, GenAI for framing and explaining results.","MAE is never negative; RMSE penalises large errors.","Accuracy alone misleads: insist on the confusion matrix in contracts.","Recall when misses are costly, precision when false alarms are costly.","Check data balance and bias mitigation before deployment."],"quote":"Do not rely just on accuracy: the devil is in the details.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-06.mp4","videoSecs":496,"notes":"media/notes/class-06-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"Predicting a property's price is which kind of problem?","a":"Regression","w":["Classification","Clustering","Reinforcement learning"],"x":"Regression predicts a continuous number; classification predicts a category."},{"q":"What decides whether a problem is supervised or unsupervised?","a":"Whether labels (ground truth) are available","w":["The size of the dataset","Whether GPUs are used","Whether it is regression or classification"],"x":"Supervised learning needs labels (Y) plus features (X); clustering works without labels."},{"q":"A model says 'no life' for all 1,000 planets (one has life). What does this show?","a":"Accuracy can be 99.9% and the model still worthless","w":["The model has perfect recall","Precision is the best metric here","The data is balanced"],"x":"Accuracy is deceptive with imbalanced data: insist on the confusion matrix."},{"q":"When false negatives are costly, which metric should you prioritise?","a":"Recall","w":["Precision","Accuracy","MAE"],"x":"Recall when misses are costly; precision when false alarms are costly; F1 balances both."},{"q":"Which is true about MAE?","a":"It can never be negative","w":["It squares errors to penalise outliers","It is used for classification","It is the same as R-squared"],"x":"MAE averages absolute gaps between actual and predicted. RMSE squares errors and penalises large ones."},{"q":"Why was Amazon's CV screening model biased against women?","a":"Historical hiring data was mostly male","w":["The algorithm was explicitly programmed to prefer men","It used too few features","It was an unsupervised model"],"x":"Proxies like 'captain of the women's team' leaked gender from imbalanced historical data."}],"cards":[{"f":"False positive","b":"Type I error: model says yes, reality is no."},{"f":"False negative","b":"Type II error: model says no, reality is yes."},{"f":"Accuracy formula","b":"(TP + TN) / all cases."},{"f":"SMOTE","b":"Synthesises new minority-class samples to fix imbalanced data."},{"f":"Clustering","b":"Groups objects so members are more similar to each other than to other groups; humans or GenAI name the clusters."},{"f":"'You going to AI' vs 'AI coming to you'","b":"Separate AI tools vs AI embedded in existing software (Excel, Word); thin wrappers risk being absorbed."},{"f":"RMSE","b":"Root mean square error: squares errors, so it penalises large errors."}]},{"id":"c07","num":7,"module":"ai-foundations","title":"How Machines Understand Text and Images","subtitle":"NLP Basics, Embeddings, Computer Vision and the GPU Revolution","date":"10 May 2026","faculty":"Programme Co-Director (Information Systems)","workshop":false,"hook":"There is no machine intelligence without knowledge representation. This class showed how unstructured text and images are turned into structured numbers that algorithms and GPUs can process, and what leaders should ask about metrics, pre-processing and annotation.","sections":[{"h":"Opening: programme flow and AI etiquette","blocks":[{"p":"Unannounced AI note-takers (Fireflies, Read.ai) were removed from the session: organisations need an <strong>AI policy for</strong> <strong>recordings</strong> and meeting bots."},{"p":"AI basics come first so that every participant shares the vocabulary needed for later strategy, transformation and governance modules; a no-code AI agents workshop has been added."}]},{"h":"Metrics in practice and shared vocabulary","blocks":[{"p":"Classical metrics (precision, recall, F1) are extended for <strong>RAG systems</strong> and <strong>ranking metrics</strong> in recommenders, because screen space is the most precious real estate."},{"p":"A fintech leader tracks <strong>AI coverage</strong>, <strong>AI resolution rate</strong> and customer experience with AI; an AML model approved by regulators spotted risks 3-6 months earlier than legacy rules."},{"p":"Beware words like &#x27;coverage&#x27; or &#x27;support&#x27; that mean something precise to data scientists. Metrics differ between sandbox and production (MLOps); RICE-style scoring helps prioritise."},{"p":"Creating a new, validated metric for your domain is a route to <strong>original IP</strong>."}]},{"h":"Language: syntax is not semantics","blocks":[{"p":"&#x27;There is no machine intelligence without knowledge representation.&#x27; Computers handled numbers and chess early, but language was hard until recently."},{"p":"Chomsky&#x27;s &#x27;Colorless green ideas sleep furiously&#x27; is <strong>grammatically perfect but meaningless</strong>; broken grammar can still communicate. Syntax and semantics are largely independent, so teaching machines grammar rules alone failed. Text mining (from information retrieval and library science) is keyword-driven; <strong>NLP</strong> uses machine learning to understand and generate language and underpins GenAI."}]},{"h":"NLP tasks and business uses","blocks":[{"p":"Summarisation, sentiment and emotion analysis (turning customers into <strong>advocates</strong>), personality detection, gender detection, sarcasm detection, translation, evaluation of answers or RFP bids, writing assistants."},{"p":"From an ML view these are mostly <strong>classification problems</strong>: text plus labels."},{"p":"Sarcasm missed by models can cause hallucination-like errors; context is key. Explore India&#x27;s <strong>Bhashini</strong> for Indian languages."},{"p":"<strong>Chatbots are enterprise-oriented</strong> (deliberately restrictive); <strong>virtual assistants are individual-oriented</strong> and perform tasks for the user."},{"img":"assets/figures/c07-1.png","title":"NLP TASKS ARE MOSTLY CLASSIFICATION PROBLEMS","alt":"Summarisation Machine translation (Bhashini) Sentiment and emotion Text in, label out Sarcasm detection Personality (vs MBTI surveys) Gender detection from CVs"}]},{"h":"Turning text into structure","blocks":[{"p":"Key terms: <strong>document</strong> (a tweet to a book), <strong>term</strong> (single or multi-word, e.g. &#x27;golden handshake&#x27;), <strong>corpus</strong> (domain collection). Pre-processing: tokenisation, stop-word removal, stemming (crude chopping) or lemmatisation (root words, costlier), lower-casing, removing digits or punctuation. Choices depend on context (CAPS may signal emotion), so modelling is <strong>art and science</strong>. Pre-processing matters again because <strong>compute and tokens are costly</strong> in the LLM era."},{"p":"The <strong>document-term matrix</strong> feeds standard classifiers; documents become vectors, so closeness means similarity (plagiarism checks, recommendations, translation quality)."},{"p":"TF-IDF scores term importance; n-grams keep more context at higher cost; POS tagging helps entity recognition."},{"p":"<strong>Embeddings</strong> give each word a vector so meaning is context-aware (&#x27;bank&#x27; near river vs near interest rate); Word2Vec was a landmark."},{"img":"assets/figures/c07-2.png","title":"FROM RAW TEXT TO A STRUCTURED MATRIX","alt":"Tokenise Remove stop Stem / Bag of words Document-term Embeddings words lemmatise matrix Break text into a, the, of: consigning, Order lost, Rows = Each word a words, phrases little meaning, consignment to frequency kept documents, vector of or sentences high cost 'consign' columns = terms numbers carrying"}]},{"h":"Subjectivity, lexicons and hands-on","blocks":[{"p":"&#x27;Actors good, music brilliant, film fails to strike a chord&#x27; gets ratings from 1 to 5 stars from humans, so labels themselves are subjective."},{"p":"Beyond ML, <strong>lexical approaches</strong> use expert-built dictionaries such as <strong>LIWC</strong> and NRC. Emojis can be noise or signal, depending on context."}]},{"h":"Computer vision and the GPU revolution","blocks":[{"p":"Image processing works on pixels; <strong>computer vision</strong> extracts meaning (classification, detection, segmentation, captioning, visual Q&amp;A)."},{"p":"Images are already structured: each pixel is a number. Edge (boundary) detection underlies feature learning."},{"p":"<strong>2012 AlexNet</strong> trained neural networks on GPUs and cut ImageNet error sharply; GPUs excel at the matrix maths behind neural nets, transforming NVIDIA&#x27;s fortunes."},{"img":"assets/figures/c07-3.png","title":"WHY GPUS AND NVIDIA TOOK OFF","alt":"Images are Neural nets = 2012 AlexNet Deep learning GPUs strategic matrices matrix maths boom Each pixel already a Linear algebra at the Trained on GPUs; Error falls to near Compute is again a number (RGB) core ImageNet error 25% to zero constraint in the LLM 16% era"}]},{"h":"Retail use case and the annotation budget","blocks":[{"p":"Computer vision solves retail&#x27;s <strong>last-mile shelf problem</strong>: checking planogram compliance, stock-outs and placement agreed with brands, instead of manual audits."},{"p":"Store layout drives dwell time (beer and diapers apart in big stores, together in convenience stores)."},{"p":"<strong>Annotation</strong> (labelling boundaries, parts, damage type) takes most of a CV project&#x27;s time. Plan who does it (interns, outsourcing, auto-annotation) and do not overpay; tools like the VGG Image Annotator help."}]}],"takeaways":["Set an AI policy for meeting bots and recordings.","Agree a shared vocabulary for metrics with tech teams.","Syntax is not semantics: meaning needs representation.","Most NLP tasks are classification problems on text.","Pre-processing choices change models and costs.","Embeddings turn words into meaning-aware vectors.","GPUs won because neural nets are matrix maths.","Budget and plan annotation in vision projects."],"quote":"There is no machine intelligence without knowledge representation.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-07.mp4","videoSecs":506,"notes":"media/notes/class-07-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"What does Chomsky's 'Colorless green ideas sleep furiously' illustrate?","a":"Syntax is not semantics","w":["Stop words carry most meaning","Sarcasm is easy to detect","Grammar rules are enough for machines to understand language"],"x":"It is grammatically perfect but meaningless, which is why teaching machines grammar rules alone failed."},{"q":"From an ML point of view, most NLP tasks are...","a":"Classification problems: text in, label out","w":["Regression problems","Clustering problems","Rule-based expert systems"],"x":"Sentiment, sarcasm, personality and gender detection all map text to labels."},{"q":"What do embeddings give each word?","a":"A vector of numbers carrying context-aware meaning","w":["A frequency count in the document","A grammatical role tag","A unique ID with no meaning"],"x":"'Bank' lands near 'river' or near 'interest rate' depending on context; Word2Vec was a landmark."},{"q":"Stemming vs lemmatisation: which is true?","a":"Stemming chops crudely; lemmatisation finds root words at higher cost","w":["Lemmatisation is cruder and cheaper","Both are identical","Stemming removes stop words"],"x":"E.g. 'consigning', 'consignment' reduced to 'consign'."},{"q":"Why did GPUs and NVIDIA take off with deep learning?","a":"Neural networks are matrix maths, which GPUs excel at","w":["GPUs store more data","GPUs were cheaper than CPUs","GPUs run rule-based systems faster"],"x":"2012's AlexNet trained on GPUs and cut ImageNet error sharply (25% to 16%)."},{"q":"What usually takes most of the time in a computer-vision project?","a":"Annotation (labelling)","w":["Choosing the GPU","Writing the algorithm","Deploying to the cloud"],"x":"Plan who annotates (interns, outsourcing, auto-annotation) and don't overpay."}],"cards":[{"f":"Document-term matrix","b":"Rows = documents, columns = terms; feeds standard classifiers."},{"f":"TF-IDF","b":"Scores how important a term is to a document relative to the corpus."},{"f":"Bag of words","b":"Text representation where word order is lost but frequency is kept."},{"f":"Chatbot vs virtual assistant","b":"Chatbots are enterprise-oriented and restrictive; virtual assistants perform tasks for an individual."},{"f":"Image processing vs computer vision","b":"Image processing works on pixels; computer vision extracts meaning (classification, detection, segmentation)."},{"f":"Bhashini","b":"India's platform for translation and AI in Indian languages."},{"f":"Lexical approaches","b":"Expert-built dictionaries such as LIWC and NRC, used instead of or alongside ML."}]},{"id":"c08","num":8,"module":"ai-foundations","title":"Neural Networks to Generative AI","subtitle":"Transformers, Tokens, Foundation Models and AI as a General Purpose Technology","date":"17 May 2026","faculty":"Programme Co-Director (Information Systems)","workshop":false,"hook":"Leaders need just enough intuition about neural networks and transformers to judge compute, cost and hype, and then must lift their eyes: like electricity, AI's real value will come from redesigning how the enterprise works, not from bolting AI onto old processes.","sections":[{"h":"Opening: accountability, expertise and programme focus","blocks":[{"p":"Some firms find LLM agent token costs exceed a junior developer&#x27;s salary. &#x27;The agent did it&#x27; shows an <strong>accountability gap</strong>: AI has no agency, so deep human expertise may become more valuable."},{"p":"This is a <strong>strategy programme</strong>; technical hands-on (n8n no-code agents workshop, possible LangChain session) is offered separately. A quiz on all content so far comes in about two weeks."},{"p":"Suggested sources: ZDNet, ByteByteGo, There&#x27;s An AI For That, State of AI, HBR, MIT Sloan, Machine Learning Mastery, Stanford AI Index."}]},{"h":"Why leaders should understand neural networks","blocks":[{"p":"Artificial neural networks are the <strong>building blocks of deep learning and GenAI</strong>. Intuition, not maths, helps you set calibrated expectations, judge hype vs substance, assess compute budgets, evaluate vendors and talk to boards."},{"p":"Inspired by biological neurons: inputs, processing, output. Intelligence comes from the <strong>connections</strong> between many neurons in input, hidden and output layers."}]},{"h":"How the network learns","blocks":[{"p":"Each input is multiplied by a <strong>weight</strong>, summed, and passed through an <strong>activation function</strong> (sigmoid, ReLU, GELU). &#x27;Billions of parameters&#x27; means billions of weights."},{"p":"Weights start random. A forward pass gives an output; the <strong>loss</strong> (error) drives a <strong>backward pass</strong> that adjusts weights. Repeating this is <strong>back-</strong> <strong>propagation</strong>, using <strong>gradient descent</strong>."},{"p":"Hyper-parameters such as <strong>learning rate</strong> and <strong>epochs</strong> are set by people. Simple maths at huge scale is why training needs so much GPU time and money."},{"p":"Example: re-framing a fraud model around recall rather than blanket accuracy cut estimates by over 60%. Security leaders need deeper knowledge: altering a few neurons can bypass compliance."},{"p":"For recommenders, use ready-made services unless you have the infrastructure; serving complex models can cost more than they earn (the Netflix Prize lesson)."},{"img":"assets/figures/c08-1.png","title":"HOW A NEURAL NETWORK LEARNS","alt":"Inputs x Weights w Sum + activation Output vs actual Back-propagation Numbers: text, Start random; the Decides how strongly Loss (one case) and Adjust weights to cut images, sound as 'billions of a neuron fires cost (average) error (gradient matrices parameters' descent)"}]},{"h":"Transformers and attention","blocks":[{"p":"The 2017 Google paper <strong>&#x27;Attention Is All You Need&#x27;</strong> changed AI as much as AlexNet. Earlier models read text sequentially."},{"p":"<strong>Attention</strong> lets the model relate words regardless of distance (&#x27;bank&#x27; and &#x27;loan&#x27; far apart) and weigh which words matter; processing became parallel, more accurate and scalable (inspired partly by the film Arrival)."},{"p":"GPT = Generative Pre-trained Transformer. ChatGPT made a huge pre-trained model accessible to everyone."},{"img":"assets/figures/c08-2.png","title":"BEFORE AND AFTER 'ATTENTION IS ALL YOU NEED' (2017)","alt":"RNN / CNN era Transformer era Reads data sequentially, word by word Processes all words in parallel Struggles with links between distant words Attention weighs which words matter in context vs Harder to scale Scales on GPUs: the basis of GPT and GenAI"}]},{"h":"Tokens, foundation models and hallucination","blocks":[{"p":"We are moving from an API economy to a <strong>token economy</strong>: tokens are the unit of cost. Estimate token costs for typical use cases (an email, code, a report summary). Some customers now also ask for the <strong>carbon footprint</strong> of AI."},{"p":"<strong>Foundation models</strong> are large pre-trained networks behind many tasks; <strong>frontier models</strong> push the boundary. <strong>Sovereign AI</strong> (India&#x27;s own foundation models such as Sarvam) reduces dependence on US and Chinese models."},{"p":"Critics call LLMs <strong>&#x27;stochastic parrots&#x27;</strong>. Hallucinations (fake citations in consulting reports, journalism, research) now have business consequences; fluent output invites misplaced trust. Mitigate with structured data, prompts and guardrails."}]},{"h":"The AI stack: what non-tech firms decide","blocks":[{"p":"Layers: semiconductors, cloud, foundation models, applications. Non-tech firms mostly decide at the application layer, but must consider <strong>GRC</strong>, data residency, private vs public models, talent and cost at lower layers."},{"p":"Participants stressed <strong>model- and cloud-agnostic architecture</strong>, <strong>model routers</strong> (cheap models for simple queries), and contract clauses demanding <strong>interoperability</strong> to avoid ERP-style painful migrations."},{"img":"assets/figures/c08-3.png","title":"THE MODERN AI STACK: WHERE NON-TECH FIRMS DECIDE","alt":"1Applications 2Foundation models Where most firms create value; use-case fit and outcomes OpenAI, Anthropic, Meta, open source; sovereign AI; model routers 3Cloud infrastructure 4Semiconductors AWS, Azure, Google; public vs private, cost, data residency NVIDIA, AMD, Intel; supply-chain and geopolitical risk"}]},{"h":"'Eventually everything becomes a toaster'","blocks":[{"p":"Technology tends to <strong>commoditise</strong>; tech moats (even patents) are temporary. Computer-vision work that needed expensive data scientists in 2019 now takes two lines of code."},{"p":"For individuals: document your work, keep an active <strong>GitHub / Hugging Face profile</strong>, publish and contribute to open source to keep an edge."}]},{"h":"AI as a general purpose technology: redesign, not bolt-on","blocks":[{"p":"Electricity&#x27;s real gains came decades later, when factories were <strong>redesigned</strong> around it: flexible layouts, assembly lines, less downtime, new management structures."},{"p":"Most firms are in AI&#x27;s &#x27;early electrification&#x27;: incremental use. CXOs must lead the redesign of roles, processes and decisions. Group exercise (HR): from chat layers on old systems to <strong>agents orchestrated</strong> over policies and workflows; AI-led JD creation and screening with human review; mixed teams of humans and agents; &#x27;SaaS is dead?&#x27;."},{"p":"Homework: deep-dive your vertical; read Carr&#x27;s HBR article <strong>&#x27;IT Doesn&#x27;t Matter&#x27;</strong> (2003) and the debate, then write &#x27;AI Doesn&#x27;t Matter&#x27;."},{"img":"assets/figures/c08-4.png","title":"THE ELECTRICITY LESSON FOR AI","alt":"Steam factory Early electric Redesigned AI today AI-native factory enterprise Central engine, Swap steam for one Motor per machine; Mostly bolted on, Redesign workflows, shafts and belts set big motor; same assembly lines; new incremental roles and decisions the layout layout management"}]}],"takeaways":["Accountability cannot be delegated to an AI agent.","Neural nets learn by adjusting weights to cut error.","Compute cost comes from simple maths at huge scale.","Attention and transformers made GenAI possible.","Think in tokens: estimate cost per use case.","Watch for hallucinations; expertise matters more.","Build model- and cloud-agnostic, interoperable architecture.","Real AI value comes from redesigning the enterprise."],"quote":"Eventually everything becomes a toaster.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-08.mp4","videoSecs":505,"notes":"media/notes/class-08-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"What does 'billions of parameters' refer to?","a":"Billions of weights in the network","w":["Billions of training documents","Billions of users","Billions of GPUs"],"x":"Each input is multiplied by a weight; parameters are those weights."},{"q":"How does a neural network learn?","a":"Back-propagation adjusts weights to reduce error, using gradient descent","w":["Engineers hand-set every weight","It memorises every training example","It adds new neurons each epoch"],"x":"Forward pass gives output, loss drives a backward pass that adjusts weights."},{"q":"Which 2017 paper introduced the transformer?","a":"'Attention Is All You Need'","w":["'IT Doesn't Matter'","'What Is Strategy?'","'ImageNet Classification with Deep CNNs'"],"x":"Attention lets models relate distant words and process text in parallel."},{"q":"What does GPT stand for?","a":"Generative Pre-trained Transformer","w":["General Purpose Technology","Graph Processing Tensor","Guided Prediction Tool"],"x":"ChatGPT made a huge pre-trained transformer accessible to everyone."},{"q":"The 'electricity lesson' says AI's real value will come from...","a":"Redesigning workflows, roles and decisions around AI","w":["Swapping old tools for AI tools in the same process","Buying the largest model","Cutting headcount quickly"],"x":"Electricity's gains came decades later when factories were redesigned around it."},{"q":"In the modern AI stack, where do most non-tech firms decide?","a":"The application layer","w":["Semiconductors","Foundation model training","Building their own cloud"],"x":"But they must still consider GRC, data residency, private vs public models and cost at lower layers."}],"cards":[{"f":"Token economy","b":"Tokens are the unit of AI cost; estimate token cost per use case."},{"f":"Hyper-parameters","b":"Settings chosen by people, like learning rate and number of epochs."},{"f":"Activation function","b":"Decides how strongly a neuron fires, e.g. sigmoid, ReLU, GELU."},{"f":"'Eventually everything becomes a toaster'","b":"Technology commoditises; tech moats (even patents) are temporary."},{"f":"Foundation vs frontier models","b":"Foundation models are large pre-trained networks behind many tasks; frontier models push the boundary."},{"f":"Stochastic parrots","b":"A critique of LLMs: fluent output without understanding, risking hallucinations."},{"f":"Model router","b":"Sends simple queries to cheaper models to control cost."}]},{"id":"c09","num":9,"module":"ai-value","title":"Traditional Automation vs Agentic AI","subtitle":"RPA, a Decision Matrix, Governance and an n8n Hands-on","date":"24 May 2026","faculty":"Guest faculty: Head of AI, health-tech company","workshop":true,"hook":"Not every workflow needs an AI agent. Leaders should know when rule-based automation (APIs, RPA) is cheaper and safer, when agentic AI earns its token cost, and how to govern probabilistic agents working alongside people.","sections":[{"h":"What automation means","blocks":[{"p":"Automation turns repetitive human tasks into machine work, with little or no human intervention."},{"p":"Example: customer complaint emails. Instead of a person reading and routing each one, software"},{"p":"categorises them (screen broken, keyboard faulty) and passes them on."},{"p":"Traditional automation needs <strong>structured, deterministic</strong> input; agentic AI handles <strong>unstructured</strong> input (free text, email) and can decide its own actions. &#x27;Vibe coding&#x27; (building apps by describing them) shows how far this has come."}]},{"h":"From APIs to RPA to agents","blocks":[{"p":"Digital evolved from enterprise software to user-generated platforms; automation from code to <strong>APIs</strong> to <strong>RPA</strong>. <strong>Robotic process automation</strong> is software that mimics human clicks (e.g. collecting leads from LinkedIn or Maps), not humanoid robots or physical AI."},{"p":"Many RPA vendors now brand themselves &#x27;agentic&#x27;; under the hood most are <strong>hybrids</strong>."},{"img":"assets/figures/c09-1.png","title":"HOW AUTOMATION HAS EVOLVED","alt":"Software & ERP APIs RPA Agentic AI Hybrid Enterprises build, Programmatic links Software bots mimic LLM agents read Most 'agentic' users consume between systems human clicks (UiPath, unstructured input platforms mix rules Automation Anywhere, and choose actions and AI"}]},{"h":"Class debate: RPA or AI agents?","blocks":[{"p":"RPA bots are the <strong>low-hanging fruit</strong> for agentic replacement, but several firms parked such moves after projecting token costs 6-7 months ahead."},{"p":"AI pricing has been <strong>subsidised</strong> by investors; ROI is justified when AI solves problems old tools could not, not when it replaces something that already works."},{"p":"RPA is cheap with predictable, sunk licence costs; token costs swing month to month. RPA bots broke when a UI changed; an agent can adapt."},{"p":"Avoid &#x27;a hammer for every nail&#x27;: many &#x27;AI&#x27; products are just process flows. Some firms cut costs by self-hosting open-weight models on their own GPU servers."},{"p":"For physical products (aerospace, automotive), faster paperwork does not change manufacturing costs; and regulation of AI is still uncertain."},{"img":"assets/figures/c09-2.png","title":"TRADITIONAL AUTOMATION VS AGENTIC AI","alt":"Traditional (rules, APIs, RPA) Agentic AI Structured, deterministic inputs Handles unstructured text, email, images Fixed if-then rules; breaks when UI changes Probabilistic; can adapt and self-correct vs Instant and cheap; near-zero marginal cost Slower; token cost on every run, variable month to month Predictable licence cost, easy to govern Needs guardrails, monitoring, interpretability"}]},{"h":"A decision matrix for choosing","blocks":[{"p":"Score <strong>complexity</strong>, <strong>autonomy</strong>, <strong>latency sensitivity</strong> and <strong>marginal cost</strong> from 1 to 5 to guide traditional vs agentic choices; classic criteria (volume, repetition, error rates) still matter."},{"p":"Agents are slower and every prompt generates new tokens, so <strong>marginal cost never falls to zero</strong> as it does for traditional software. Complaint routing: moderate complexity, low autonomy, latency tolerant, cost sensitive: a light AI classifier is enough."},{"p":"Banking examples: AML alert investigation (complex, 45 minutes to days) suits agents; real-time <strong>sanctions screening</strong> (exact matching, no delay) stays rule-based."},{"img":"assets/figures/c09-3.png","title":"DECISION MATRIX: SCORE EACH FACTOR 1 TO 5","alt":"1Complexity 2Autonomy 3Latency tolerance Structured and linear (1) vs Suggest only (1) vs act and decide on Must be instant (1) vs can wait (5) unstructured, non-linear (5) its own (5) 4Cost tolerance 5Example: sanctions screening 6Example: AML alert investigation Need near-zero marginal cost (1) vs Real-time exact matching: rules win 45 min to days of analysis: agentic high value per run (5) fits"}]},{"h":"Governance and security of agents","blocks":[{"p":"Traditional software is deterministic and easy to govern. Agents are <strong>probabilistic</strong>, like people, so firms must now govern <strong>humans plus</strong> <strong>agents</strong>; organisation design will change."},{"p":"Open-weight models (including Chinese ones) can be self-hosted, but trust, hidden behaviours and limited <strong>interpretability</strong> remain concerns; alternatives include Google&#x27;s Gemma, Meta&#x27;s Llama and Mistral."},{"p":"Protect sensitive data (health, banking) with redaction and certified clouds. Build <strong>guardrails into prompts from the design stage</strong> (&#x27;shift left&#x27;): persona, scope, domain drift, factual accuracy."}]},{"h":"Hands-on: n8n lead qualification","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>n8n</strong> is a drag-and-drop workflow tool for both rule-based and agentic automation: a 14-day cloud trial, self-hosting or third-party hosting."},{"p":"The same form-to-email lead process was built twice: once with Switch rules, once with an AI Agent node driven by system and user prompts."},{"p":"The agent qualified leads and drafted emails without explicit rules; business rules, few-shot examples, structured JSON output and confidence checks keep it reliable."},{"img":"assets/figures/c09-4.png","title":"N8N DEMO: QUALIFYING WEB-FORM LEADS","alt":"Rule-based workflow AI agent workflow Form trigger, then a Switch node Form trigger, then an AI Agent node Explicit rules on budget and company size System prompt: 'You are a lead qualification vsexpert' High vs low quality written to a data table User prompt passes name, email, budget Fixed template email for high-quality leads Scores the lead and drafts a personalised email Fiddly data-type mismatches Add few-shot rules, memory, tools, guardrails"}]}],"takeaways":["Automate repetitive work; pick the cheapest tool that works.","Rules suit structured, deterministic, latency-critical tasks.","Agents suit unstructured, judgement-heavy, adaptive tasks.","Token costs recur and vary; RPA costs are predictable.","Score complexity, autonomy, latency and cost before choosing.","Many 'agentic' products are hybrids under the hood.","Govern humans plus agents; build guardrails in early.","No-code tools like n8n let leaders prototype quickly."],"quote":"If you have a hammer, every problem looks like a nail.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-09.mp4","videoSecs":503,"notes":"media/notes/class-09-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"Which input does traditional automation need?","a":"Structured, deterministic input","w":["Free text and email","Images and video","Any input, as long as rules exist"],"x":"Agentic AI handles unstructured input and can choose its own actions."},{"q":"What is robotic process automation (RPA)?","a":"Software bots that mimic human clicks","w":["Humanoid robots in factories","Physical AI on the shop floor","LLM agents that write code"],"x":"E.g. UiPath or Automation Anywhere collecting leads from LinkedIn or Maps."},{"q":"Why did several firms park plans to replace RPA bots with agents?","a":"Projected token costs were too high and unpredictable","w":["Agents cannot read email","RPA is more accurate on unstructured data","Regulators banned agents"],"x":"RPA licence costs are predictable; token costs recur on every run and swing month to month."},{"q":"Real-time sanctions screening (exact matching, no delay) is best handled by...","a":"Rule-based automation","w":["An autonomous AI agent","A generative AI chatbot","Manual review only"],"x":"Latency-critical, exact matching tasks suit rules; AML alert investigation suits agents."},{"q":"Which four factors does the decision matrix score from 1 to 5?","a":"Complexity, autonomy, latency tolerance and cost tolerance","w":["Volume, speed, price and brand","Accuracy, recall, precision and F1","People, process, data and technology"],"x":"Classic criteria (volume, repetition, error rates) still matter alongside these."},{"q":"What does 'shift left' mean for agent guardrails?","a":"Build guardrails into prompts from the design stage","w":["Add guardrails after go-live","Move the agent to a cheaper model","Let users set their own guardrails"],"x":"Persona, scope, domain drift and factual accuracy are designed in early."}],"cards":[{"f":"Traditional automation marginal cost","b":"Near zero once built; agents pay token cost on every run."},{"f":"Why agents need governance","b":"They are probabilistic like people, so firms must govern humans plus agents."},{"f":"n8n","b":"Drag-and-drop workflow tool for both rule-based and agentic automation."},{"f":"Making an AI agent reliable","b":"Business rules, few-shot examples, structured JSON output and confidence checks."},{"f":"Hybrid automation","b":"Most 'agentic' platforms mix rules and AI under the hood."},{"f":"'Hammer for every nail'","b":"Many 'AI' products are just process flows: pick the cheapest tool that works."},{"f":"Vibe coding","b":"Building apps by describing them in natural language."}]},{"id":"c10","num":10,"module":"ai-value","title":"Prioritising AI Use Cases","subtitle":"'AI Doesn't Matter', Strategic Cognition vs Operations and the Impact- Feasibility Portfolio","date":"31 May 2026","faculty":"Programme Co-Director (Information Systems)","workshop":false,"hook":"A CDAIO's job is not generating AI ideas but allocating scarce resources across many competing AI opportunities, balancing long-term strategic vision with Monday-morning operational demands, using a scoring checklist tailored to the organisation.","sections":[{"h":"Homework debate: from 'IT Doesn't Matter' to 'AI Doesn't Matter'","blocks":[{"p":"Carr&#x27;s 2003 HBR article argued IT had become a commodity infrastructure. Participants applied it to AI."},{"p":"Takeaways: <strong>fast followers</strong> can avoid pioneers&#x27; costs; tools will get cheaper; stay <strong>model-agnostic</strong>; prioritise security and risk over hype; <strong>execution</strong> matters more than the shared model."},{"p":"Counterpoints: move first when competitors could erode your <strong>USP</strong>; from an Indian lens, being only a fast follower leaves R&amp;D and value creation abroad."},{"p":"Most digital initiatives forced onto old ways of working did not pay off; AI needs <strong>new ways of working and new business models</strong>. Model-agnostic means applications can switch foundation model, like appliances that work with any electricity supplier; route simple tasks to cheaper models."},{"img":"assets/figures/c10-1.png","title":"'AI DOESN'T MATTER'? CLASS VIEWS ON CARR'S ARGUMENT","alt":"Fast follower Lead or build Tech moats are temporary; AI is commoditising Move first when your USP is at risk Tools get cheaper: avoid paying the pioneer's price India must build, not only consume, AI vs Stay model-agnostic, focus on risk and security Execution and new business models matter most Learn from big players' mistakes Bolting AI onto old processes yields little"}]},{"h":"Group work: what an AI-native redesign looks like","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>HR recruitment</strong>: flip the funnel. AI scans project roadmaps for talent gaps, searches the market and pitches to candidates; recruiters will also &#x27;hire&#x27; AI agents."},{"p":"<strong>Banking back office</strong>: legacy systems and edge cases limit AI; today&#x27;s bots are &#x27;band-aids&#x27;. AI-native means real-time unstructured data, model-agnostic architecture and humans plus agents."},{"p":"<strong>Operations</strong>: coordination gaps between sourcing, production and maintenance; AI is used for dashboards, not decisions. Future: closed-loop, self-optimising operations with shared <strong>digital twins</strong> and predictive maintenance, plus feedback loops."}]},{"h":"Strategic cognition vs operational leverage","blocks":[{"p":"CXOs must balance the <strong>big picture</strong> (where the business will be in 5-10 years) with <strong>Monday-morning</strong> firefighting, KPIs and templates."},{"p":"<strong>DEC</strong> had great people, products and execution but failed to read tomorrow&#x27;s market: a failure of strategic cognition. &#x27;What is measured is managed&#x27;, and quarterly financial ratios pull focus to today."},{"p":"Ask: are you also investing in discovering what could make your <strong>current business model obsolete</strong>? (e.g. AI answers threatening search advertising)."},{"p":"<strong>Adobe</strong> (2008-09) laid off staff, bought Omniture and moved from boxed software to subscriptions and analytics: unpopular then, visionary now."},{"img":"assets/figures/c10-2.png","title":"DIGITAL EQUIPMENT CORPORATION: A FAILURE OF STRATEGIC COGNITION","alt":"1970s-80s Excellent execution Missed the shift Collapse Mini-computer pioneer, No. 2 Talent, products, current Optimised for today's Now a business-school after IBM customers margins, not tomorrow's failure case market"}]},{"h":"'We invested in AI; where is the impact?'","blocks":[{"p":"Efficiency gains alone rarely justify AI; look for <strong>new problems</strong> that were not solvable before."},{"p":"A bank&#x27;s AI phone agent for fraud calls failed in two cases, causing write-offs; it bridged with humans, kept searching and later found an agent that works. Persist, but course-correct."},{"p":"Know your portfolio: which initiatives, licences and returns. Be bold enough to say when investments are wrong."},{"p":"AI is not overnight savings; evaluate iteratively. Distinguish <strong>business transformation</strong> from digital add-ons: one firm found a chemical plant&#x27;s &#x27;fish-eye&#x27; defect in 4-6 weeks by focusing on the business problem."}]},{"h":"The CDAIO's real job: allocate resources","blocks":[{"p":"Every department wants AI budget. The CDAIO consolidates use cases and builds an <strong>organisation-wide roadmap</strong>; the hard part is allocation, not ideas."},{"p":"Judge <strong>feasibility</strong> (data, infrastructure, skills, budget, governance, legal, financial) against <strong>business impact</strong>."},{"p":"Mistakes: only quick wins, only moonshots, treating all equally, or feasibility alone. Instead, manage AI like an <strong>investment</strong> <strong>portfolio</strong>; if forced to pick one, start with a quick win."},{"p":"70-80% of IT projects fail, mostly on cost, time and stakeholders rather than technology."},{"img":"assets/figures/c10-3.png","title":"AI USE-CASE PORTFOLIO: BUSINESS IMPACT X FEASIBILITY","alt":"Champions (high impact, high feasibility) 12Research cases (high impact, low feasibility) Star projects with significant value; pursue with full Promising but data or technology not ready; keep on radar, support fund carefully 3Quick wins (lower impact, high feasibility) 4Reassess later (low impact, low feasibility) Done in 30-90 days; prove value and build confidence Park, do not discard; tech may mature into a quick win"}]},{"h":"Workshop: build your own scoring checklist","blocks":[{"p":"Off-the-shelf templates (Gartner, Forrester, consultancies) must be <strong>customised</strong>; one size does not fit all."},{"p":"Groups scored banking fraud, aircraft engine servicing, supply chain and e-commerce use cases on financial value, volume, speed to market, data readiness, process maturity, risk, strategic alignment and <strong>FTEs affected</strong>."},{"p":"Board-style challenges: how is financial value measured? Is fraud prevention truly quantifiable? Risk must <strong>reduce</strong> the score. Translate scores into quadrants, visualise them, and agree them with trusted peers."},{"p":"Reading: Porter&#x27;s &#x27;What Is Strategy?&#x27;; &#x27;AI for Managers&#x27; (course textbook), chapter 2, before the digital module."},{"img":"assets/figures/c10-4.png","title":"BUILDING YOUR OWN PRIORITISATION CHECKLIST","alt":"List use cases Define criteria Rubric 1-5 Weighted score Visualise & present From finance, HR, Value, volume, speed, Agreed with Then place each in a Convince the board marketing, operations data, process, risk, stakeholders; risk quadrant with a clear story FTEs weighted negatively"}]}],"takeaways":["Tech moats are temporary; execution and business models win.","Decide when to lead and when to fast-follow.","Balance long-term vision with daily operational demands.","Ask what could make your business model obsolete.","Seek new problems AI can solve, not just efficiency.","The CDAIO allocates resources across many use cases.","Manage AI as a portfolio: champions, quick wins, research, reassess.","Customise a scoring checklist and present it persuasively."],"quote":"What is measured is managed.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-10.mp4","videoSecs":509,"notes":"media/notes/class-10-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"Carr's 2003 HBR article argued that IT...","a":"Had become commodity infrastructure, not a source of advantage","w":["Was the main source of competitive advantage","Should be outsourced entirely","Would replace strategy"],"x":"Participants applied the same argument to AI: 'AI Doesn't Matter'."},{"q":"Why is DEC a business-school failure case?","a":"A failure of strategic cognition: it optimised for today's market and missed the shift","w":["Poor execution and weak products","Lack of talent","Over-investment in AI"],"x":"DEC had great people, products and execution but failed to read tomorrow's market."},{"q":"In the impact-feasibility portfolio, what are high-impact, low-feasibility cases called?","a":"Research cases","w":["Champions","Quick wins","Reassess later"],"x":"Promising, but data or technology is not ready: keep on radar and fund carefully."},{"q":"If forced to pick only one AI use case to start, the class suggests...","a":"A quick win","w":["A moonshot","The cheapest research case","Whatever the CEO likes"],"x":"Quick wins (30-90 days) prove value and build confidence."},{"q":"According to the class, what is the CDAIO's hardest job?","a":"Allocating scarce resources across many competing use cases","w":["Generating AI ideas","Writing code","Choosing the foundation model"],"x":"Every department wants AI budget; the hard part is allocation, not ideas."},{"q":"In a scoring checklist, how should risk be treated?","a":"It should reduce the score","w":["It should be ignored","It should increase the score","It should be scored only by IT"],"x":"Board-style challenge: risk must reduce the score."}],"cards":[{"f":"Champions","b":"High impact, high feasibility: star projects, pursue with full support."},{"f":"Quick wins","b":"Lower impact, high feasibility: done in 30-90 days to prove value."},{"f":"Reassess later","b":"Low impact, low feasibility: park, don't discard."},{"f":"Model-agnostic","b":"Applications can switch foundation models, like appliances that work with any electricity supplier."},{"f":"Strategic cognition vs operational leverage","b":"Balancing the 5-10 year picture with Monday-morning firefighting and KPIs."},{"f":"Adobe 2008-09","b":"Moved from boxed software to subscriptions and analytics (bought Omniture): unpopular then, visionary now."},{"f":"IT project failure rate","b":"70-80%, mostly on cost, time and stakeholders rather than technology."}]},{"id":"c11","num":11,"module":"strategy","title":"What Digital Really Means","subtitle":"Digital Economics, the Long Tail, Digital Transformation and the Digital Mindset","date":"7 June 2026","faculty":"Programme Co-Director (Information Systems)","workshop":false,"hook":"The programme now turns from AI to digital. Digital goods rewrite business economics (near-zero marginal cost, the long tail, reach with richness, interactivity, data), and digital transformation is a profound change of business models and mindset that each organisation must define for itself.","sections":[{"h":"Opening: defining the CDAIO role","blocks":[{"p":"The next modules cover digital technology, architecture, digital and AI transformation strategy and business models; strategy vocabulary (value proposition, competitive advantage) must be shared like technical vocabulary."},{"p":"Multi-cloud set-ups often emerge bottom-up (ML on one cloud, GenAI on another); shadow IT then needs standard guidelines. The <strong>Supreme Court of India&#x27;s draft on AI use in courts</strong> is a model governance document (accountability, transparency, fairness, audit). A Vedanta job ad asks the Chief AI and Digital Officer to &#x27;<strong>Define</strong> and lead&#x27; the transformation roadmap: CDAIOs must <strong>own the</strong> <strong>narrative</strong> of what AI and digital mean in their domain. Some JDs now report to the CFO."},{"p":"Leaders return from conferences demanding AI; healthcare must stay cautious (99% accuracy is not enough) while fintech experiments more. Separate hype from substance; most failures ignore &#x27;boring&#x27; data quality, governance and change management."}]},{"h":"Digital goods and their economics","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>Digital products</strong> are delivered by digital means, built from software and data, often on platforms: apps, e-books, streaming, SaaS, APIs."},{"p":"Marginal cost of copies, storage, delivery and iteration falls towards <strong>zero</strong> (though AI compute is currently pushing some costs up). When production is abundant, value shifts to <strong>scarce complements</strong>: attention, trust, distribution, proprietary data and customer relationships (Spotify&#x27;s context-aware personalisation). Patents and IP are only temporary moats."},{"img":"assets/figures/c11-1.png","title":"HOW DIGITAL REWRITES BUSINESS ECONOMICS","alt":"1Near-zero marginal cost 2Cheap iteration 3Scarce complements Extra copies, storage and delivery Updates pushed online, no CDs or Attention, trust, distribution, data, cost almost nothing physical patches customer relationships The long tail Reach AND richness 456Interactivity and data Serve niche demand that physical Millions of customers, each with a Users shape the experience; usage shelves cannot stock personalised experience data becomes a core asset"}]},{"h":"The long tail","blocks":[{"p":"Physical stores stock what sells; online stores can also serve <strong>niche demand</strong> at almost no inventory cost, with recommenders guiding discovery."},{"p":"Class examples: VR show-flats for niche interior styles; secure archival of clinical-trial records; engaging the 90% of B2B fintech customers who generate 10% of revenue."},{"p":"Exercise: find demand in your industry that is unserved because it is too expensive, segmented or niche."},{"img":"assets/figures/c11-2.png","title":"THE LONG TAIL: PHYSICAL VS DIGITAL","alt":"Physical bookstore Online / digital store Shelf space and inventory cost are limited Near-zero cost to list another title Stocks only popular 'head' titles Serves both head and long tail vs Niche demand goes unserved Recommenders surface niche items Sales per square metre drive decisions Examples: VR show-flats, re-engaging low-value B2B customers"}]},{"h":"Reach, richness, interactivity and data","blocks":[{"p":"Historically firms traded <strong>reach</strong> (newspapers) against <strong>richness</strong> (private wealth advisers); digital delivers both, up to hyper-personalised content. Read the BCG/HBR classic on reach and richness."},{"p":"Television is passive; TikTok and Reels are <strong>interactive</strong> and highly <strong>engaging</strong>."},{"p":"Usage data improves products, so data may matter as much as the product itself. Debate: data advantages of large states, the need for <strong>sovereign AI</strong>, and &#x27;AI neutrality&#x27; akin to net neutrality."}]},{"h":"What digital transformation is (and is not)","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>Digitisation</strong> converts information; <strong>digitalisation</strong> improves operations; <strong>digital transformation</strong> profoundly changes processes, competencies and business models to exploit digital technologies including AI."},{"p":"It is driven by <strong>mindset</strong> more than skill set: believing digital must change how we operate and how others see us, digital-first thinking, agility, customer-centricity and belief in the process."},{"p":"Examples: Adobe&#x27;s Omniture bet; a QSR&#x27;s digital ordering only worked after menus, staff behaviour and SLAs changed; a vaccine re-booking platform built in months was shelved because the client&#x27;s culture was not ready."},{"img":"assets/figures/c11-3.png","title":"DIGITISATION TO DIGITAL TRANSFORMATION","alt":"Digitisation Digitalisation Digital transformation Driven by mindset Physical information to Digital tech improves Profound change to Beliefs, culture, people and digital (paper form to individual operations (e.g. processes, competencies and leadership more than online form) leave workflow) business models technology"}]},{"h":"Sacred cows and why transformations fail","blocks":[{"p":"Group task: define a digital mindset for an industry, identify and flip five <strong>sacred cows</strong>, and imagine 2036. Healthcare: &#x27;patient must go to the doctor&#x27; and paper records flipped to tele-consultation, connected vitals and drone-delivered medicines; trust and accountability remain issues."},{"p":"BFSI: physical branches, paper loan files, office-only work and physical recovery; what is a big change for one bank is business as usual for a digital-native one, so <strong>define digital for your organisation</strong>."},{"p":"Surveys show many transformations stall: causes include market disruption, missing competencies and culture, but also controllable ones: poor <strong>alignment with business strategy</strong>, weak shared commitment, flawed strategy and unclear communication."},{"p":"Homework: find one successful and one failed digital transformation."},{"img":"assets/figures/c11-4.png","title":"SACRED COW EXERCISE: CHALLENGE UNQUESTIONED BELIEFS","alt":"Define digital Find 5 sacred Flip each one Imagine 2036 Reflect mindset cows For your industry, as Paper, speed, What would the Future-state model What holds the team a senior leader meeting, rule beliefs digital alternative and key initiatives back? What will you be? change?"}]}],"takeaways":["Own and define what AI and digital mean in your organisation.","Digital pushes marginal costs towards zero.","Value shifts to attention, trust, data and relationships.","Use the long tail to serve unmet niche demand.","Digital offers both reach and richness.","Digitisation and digitalisation are not transformation.","Transformation is driven by mindset and culture.","Align digital strategy with business strategy."],"quote":"Digital transformation is driven by mindset more than technology.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-11.mp4","videoSecs":510,"notes":"media/notes/class-11-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"What happens to marginal cost with digital goods?","a":"It falls towards zero","w":["It rises with every copy","It stays the same as physical goods","It depends only on hardware"],"x":"Copies, storage, delivery and iteration cost almost nothing (though AI compute is pushing some costs up)."},{"q":"When production is abundant, value shifts to...","a":"Scarce complements: attention, trust, distribution, data and relationships","w":["Cheaper production","More patents","Larger warehouses"],"x":"Patents and IP are only temporary moats."},{"q":"What does the long tail let digital businesses do?","a":"Serve niche demand that physical shelves cannot stock","w":["Focus only on best-sellers","Increase inventory costs","Avoid recommendation engines"],"x":"Recommenders surface niche items at near-zero listing cost."},{"q":"Which describes digital transformation (not digitisation or digitalisation)?","a":"A profound change to processes, competencies and business models","w":["Converting a paper form to an online form","Automating a leave workflow","Buying new software"],"x":"Digitisation converts information; digitalisation improves operations; transformation changes the business model."},{"q":"Digital transformation is driven mainly by...","a":"Mindset and culture","w":["Technology spend","Vendor choice","Cloud migration"],"x":"Example: a vaccine re-booking platform was shelved because the client's culture was not ready."},{"q":"Historically firms traded off reach against richness. What does digital allow?","a":"Both reach and richness at once","w":["Only reach","Only richness","Neither"],"x":"Millions of customers, each with a personalised experience."}],"cards":[{"f":"Digitisation","b":"Physical information to digital (paper form to online form)."},{"f":"Digitalisation","b":"Digital tech improves individual operations (e.g. a leave workflow)."},{"f":"Sacred cow exercise","b":"Define a digital mindset, find 5 unquestioned beliefs, flip them, imagine 2036."},{"f":"Interactivity","b":"Users shape the experience (TikTok vs passive TV); usage data becomes a core asset."},{"f":"Controllable causes of failed transformation","b":"Poor alignment with strategy, weak shared commitment, flawed strategy, unclear communication."},{"f":"The CDAIO's narrative job","b":"Own and define what AI and digital mean in your organisation."},{"f":"Shadow IT","b":"Bottom-up tools and clouds adopted outside central IT; needs standard guidelines."}]},{"id":"c12","num":12,"module":"strategy","title":"Aligning Digital and AI with Business Strategy","subtitle":"Operational Effectiveness vs Strategy, Trade-offs, Fit, Moats and Porter's Five Forces","date":"14 June 2026","faculty":"Programme Co-Director (Information Systems)","workshop":false,"hook":"A CDAIO does not write business strategy but must be strategically literate enough to translate it into digital and AI execution and to challenge it. Strategy is not operational effectiveness: it is unique positioning built on trade-offs, activities that fit together, defensible moats and a clear value proposition in an attractive industry.","sections":[{"h":"Homework: digital transformation wins and failures","blocks":[{"p":"Successes shared: <strong>Tata Steel</strong> (its digital transformation playbook), <strong>Domino&#x27;s</strong>, the Tata-Airbus <strong>C295</strong> digitised assembly line, Disney-Pixar and LifeChurch."},{"p":"Failures: <strong>Kodak</strong>; <strong>GE Digital</strong> (too big, too fast, culture); <strong>Ola Electric</strong> (digitised heavily but neglected supply chain and service); a hospital ERP/HIS rollout reversed for poor change management."},{"p":"A hospital mobile EMR app succeeded for viewing records but failed for taking action, because the culture did not change."}]},{"h":"Where the CDAIO sits in strategy","blocks":[{"p":"Strategy exists at three levels: <strong>corporate</strong> (which markets), <strong>business</strong> (how to compete) and <strong>functional</strong>. The CDAIO usually does not create business strategy but <strong>translates it</strong> into digital and AI execution."},{"p":"This needs <strong>strategic literacy</strong>: competitive advantage, integration, M&amp;A, technical debt, and finance as the language of business."},{"p":"The CEO represents shareholders and maximises shareholder value. Theories of the firm (transaction cost, agency theory and agency cost) explain how organisations behave."},{"p":"The CDAIO&#x27;s job includes the right to <strong>challenge</strong>: bring digital and AI insight that may change the rules of the game assumed in a strategy retreat."}]},{"h":"Operational effectiveness is not strategy","blocks":[{"p":"TQM, Six Sigma, automation and benchmarking push a firm toward the <strong>productivity frontier</strong>, but rivals copy them, causing <strong>competitive convergence</strong>."},{"p":"A firm inside the frontier either has costs that are too high or delivers value that is too low."},{"p":"<strong>Value</strong> is the customer&#x27;s willingness to pay. <strong>Price value</strong> comes from low cost (cost leadership); <strong>non-price value</strong> from brand, quality, design, service and convenience (Apple, Bisleri vs local water, Starbucks vs a tea stall)."},{"p":"Customer and user can differ (a parent buys, a child uses), though in IT they are often treated as the same."},{"img":"assets/figures/c12-1.png","title":"OPERATIONAL EFFECTIVENESS IS NOT STRATEGY","alt":"Operational effectiveness Strategy Doing the same things better (TQM, Six Sigma, Being different: a unique position in the market automation, benchmarking) Deliberate trade-offs and choosing what NOT to do Moves the firm toward the productivity frontier vs Value: price value or non-price value (brand, Easily copied by rivals quality, service) Leads to competitive convergence (look-alike Activities that fit and reinforce each other smartphones, clouds)"}]},{"h":"Strategy is unique positioning and trade-offs","blocks":[{"p":"You cannot simultaneously be lowest cost and most customised, fastest and most carefully controlled, or mass market and niche premium. Strategy is often about <strong>what not to do</strong>."},{"p":"Examples: Epic Systems and other health-tech leaders <strong>turn customers away</strong> after due diligence; American Express serves an affluent niche with superior dispute service; Vertu is niche premium; Stanley Black &amp; Decker bet on battery technology for cordless tools."},{"p":"In regulated Indian banking, SBI stays largely mass market (with a rural mandate) while Standard Chartered targets niche premium; shifts toward HNI clients show <strong>emergent strategy</strong>, where what happens differs from what was planned."}]},{"h":"Fit: activities that reinforce each other","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>Fit</strong> is when activities lock together like a jigsaw so that 1 + 1 equals 11. Porter names three types: <strong>simple consistency</strong>, <strong>reinforcing</strong> and <strong>optimisation of effort</strong>."},{"p":"At IKEA every activity supports low cost: no free delivery, few sales assistants, limited customisation, flat-pack logistics, and design, packaging and transport co-optimised to remove waste. India needed local adaptations such as assembly help."},{"p":"Fit makes imitation hard: copying flat-pack alone does not work without self-service stores and customer assembly. Read Porter&#x27;s classic article <strong>&#x27;What Is Strategy?&#x27;</strong>."},{"img":"assets/figures/c12-2.png","title":"IKEA: HOW FIT CREATES A SELF-REINFORCING CYCLE","alt":"Flat-pack design Cheap transport Self-service and Lower prices Higher volume DIY Furniture ships in Dense stacking, No delivery or Savings passed on to Economies of scale flat boxes better space use assembly staff customers cut cost further"}]},{"h":"Building long-term defensibility","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>Economies of scale</strong> lower unit cost and raise entry barriers (IKEA, Jio&#x27;s free launch). Digital lowers the cost of scaling for you and for rivals, as boutique consultancies show."},{"p":"<strong>Brand</strong>: Maggi survived a food-safety crisis; brand recall and brand value can be measured. A brand that becomes a verb (Xerox, Photoshop, Google) creates legal risk."},{"p":"<strong>Embedding</strong>: SAP or Microsoft 365 are so woven into workflows that switching costs are huge; AI firms now embed assistants into daily work (Copilot in Office, Gemini in search, AI code security scans)."},{"p":"<strong>Network effects</strong>: value rises with users (one telephone is worthless). Watch for saturation, and do not confuse them with <strong>viral effects</strong>, which only acquire users. Networks can be one-, two- or three-sided."},{"p":"<strong>IP and patents</strong> are powerful but usually temporary moats (&#x27;West Coast innovates, East Coast litigates&#x27;)."}]},{"h":"Value proposition and choosing where to play","blocks":[{"p":"A <strong>value proposition</strong> is the specific value a product promises to a clearly defined customer segment: who are the customers, which needs are solved, and how are we uniquely better than alternatives?"},{"p":"Examples: US mortgage IT specialist; a patented U-shaped metro viaduct girder; product life-cycle services."},{"p":"<strong>Porter&#x27;s Five Forces</strong> judge an industry&#x27;s long-term profitability: new entrants, buyer power, supplier power, substitutes and rivalry. &#x27;Long term&#x27; depends on the industry cycle (steel vs FMCG). Homework: how do digital and AI change each force?"},{"img":"assets/figures/c12-3.png","title":"PORTER'S FIVE FORCES: HOW ATTRACTIVE IS AN INDUSTRY?","alt":"Threat of new entrants Buyer power 123Supplier power Scale, brand loyalty, capital, Number and size of customers, price Few suppliers (e.g. chips) means policy, distribution, switching cost sensitivity, ability to switch strong bargaining power 4Threat of substitutes 5Rivalry 6Digital-era question Often overlooked: number and Number, diversity, concentration, How are digital and AI rewriting each differentiation of substitutes growth, exit barriers; co-opetition force?"}]}],"takeaways":["The CDAIO translates business strategy into digital and AI execution, and must be literate enough to challenge it.","Operational effectiveness is necessary but copyable; it leads to competitive convergence.","Value is the customer's willingness to pay, from price value or non-price value.","Strategy is a unique position built on trade-offs: decide what you will not do.","Fit between activities (IKEA) makes a strategy hard to imitate.","Moats: economies of scale, brand, embedding, network effects and (temporarily) IP.","A value proposition answers who, which need, and why we are uniquely better.","Use Porter's Five Forces to judge industry attractiveness, and ask how digital and AI rewrite each force."],"quote":"Strategy is about unique positioning, and it is all about trade-offs: you often have to focus more on what you do not want to do.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-12.mp4","videoSecs":485,"notes":"media/notes/class-12-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"According to Porter, operational effectiveness is...","a":"Necessary but easily copied, leading to competitive convergence","w":["The same as strategy","A source of lasting advantage","Irrelevant for digital firms"],"x":"TQM, Six Sigma and benchmarking push toward the productivity frontier, but rivals copy them."},{"q":"How is 'value' defined in the class?","a":"The customer's willingness to pay","w":["The firm's cost of production","Market capitalisation","Revenue per employee"],"x":"Price value comes from low cost; non-price value from brand, quality, design, service."},{"q":"Strategy is best described as...","a":"A unique position built on trade-offs, including what not to do","w":["Doing the same things better than rivals","Being lowest cost and most customised at once","Benchmarking against leaders"],"x":"You cannot be lowest cost and most customised, or mass market and niche premium, at the same time."},{"q":"What makes IKEA's strategy hard to imitate?","a":"Fit: activities that reinforce each other","w":["A patent on flat-pack furniture","Lowest-cost raw materials","Celebrity advertising"],"x":"Copying flat-pack alone fails without self-service stores and customer assembly."},{"q":"Which is NOT one of Porter's Five Forces?","a":"Government subsidies","w":["Threat of substitutes","Buyer power","Threat of new entrants"],"x":"The five are: new entrants, buyer power, supplier power, substitutes and rivalry."},{"q":"How do network effects differ from viral effects?","a":"Network effects raise value with more users; viral effects only acquire users","w":["They are the same thing","Viral effects create switching costs","Network effects only apply to telecoms"],"x":"One telephone is worthless; value rises as more join."}],"cards":[{"f":"Three levels of strategy","b":"Corporate (which markets), business (how to compete), functional."},{"f":"Moats","b":"Economies of scale, brand, embedding, network effects and (temporarily) IP."},{"f":"Embedding","b":"Being so woven into workflows (SAP, Microsoft 365) that switching costs are huge."},{"f":"Emergent strategy","b":"What actually happens differs from what was planned."},{"f":"Value proposition","b":"Who are the customers, which needs are solved, and how are we uniquely better?"},{"f":"Three types of fit (Porter)","b":"Simple consistency, reinforcing, and optimisation of effort."},{"f":"The CDAIO's right to challenge","b":"Bring digital and AI insight that may change the rules assumed in strategy."}]},{"id":"c13","num":13,"module":"strategy","title":"Strategy Frameworks for the CDAIO","subtitle":"Value Propositions, Marketing Myopia, PESTEL, Five Forces, Value Chain and VRIO","date":"21 June 2026","faculty":"Programme Co-Director (Information Systems)","workshop":false,"hook":"The closing strategy-literacy session. Firms analyse the outside world with PESTEL and Porter's Five Forces, and look inside with value chain analysis and VRIO. The CDAIO's job is to add a digital and AI lens to each framework, especially to the linkages that turn 1 + 1 into 11.","sections":[{"h":"Opening: export bans, Netflix, Ford and CSFs","blocks":[{"p":"News on US limits for an advanced AI model prompted a history lesson: Steve Jobs turned an export ban on a Mac into marketing, and the EU forced Microsoft to sell Windows without Internet Explorer."},{"p":"A participant compared <strong>Netflix</strong> (DVD-by-mail to cloud streaming and personalisation, revenue $152M in 2002 to $39B in 2024) with <strong>Ford&#x27;s</strong> failed shift to a mobility company (Chariot, autonomous vehicles). <strong>Critical Success Factors</strong> (MIT, 1979) are the ancestor of modern KPIs and still guide executive"},{"p":"dashboard design. The 2008 <strong>Netflix Prize</strong> ($1M) democratised data science; the RecSys challenge continues it."}]},{"h":"Writing a sharp value proposition","blocks":[{"p":"Textbook form: for <strong>which customer segment</strong>, we provide <strong>what benefit</strong>, <strong>how</strong>, unlike the alternatives. In practice: why should your audience care?"},{"p":"Examples: <strong>Amazon</strong> vast selection, low prices, fast delivery; <strong>Uber</strong> transport on demand with minimal waiting; <strong>Netflix</strong> personalised entertainment anytime; <strong>Tesla</strong> sustainable transport with high performance; <strong>Apple</strong> a seamless premium experience across devices; <strong>Google</strong> organise the world&#x27;s information. Participant examples: telecom coverage everywhere; aerospace fuel efficiency without losing power; sustainable high-quality copper for India&#x27;s growth."}]},{"h":"Marketing myopia: know which business you are in","blocks":[{"p":"Levitt&#x27;s idea: firms fail when obsessed with their <strong>product</strong> instead of the <strong>customer problem</strong> it solves. Kodak feared digital would cannibalise film. With hindsight failures look obvious, but inertia and quarterly pressure make them hard to see from inside."},{"img":"assets/figures/c13-1.png","title":"MARKETING MYOPIA: PRODUCT FOCUS VS THE REAL NEED","alt":"1US railroads 2Nokia Saw themselves as rail, not mobility; lost to cars 'Connecting people' while phones became productivity tools Blockbuster 34Kodak DVD rental, not entertainment; beaten by Netflix streaming Film, not preserving memories; invented then shelved digital"}]},{"h":"External analysis: PESTEL","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>Political</strong> (stability, corruption, trade limits: SpaceX Rideshare bars some countries; India screens bidders from neighbouring countries), <strong>Economic</strong> (rates, income), <strong>Social</strong> (Japan&#x27;s ageing vs India&#x27;s young population), <strong>Technological</strong>, <strong>Environmental</strong> (data-centre resistance) and <strong>Legal</strong> (antitrust)."},{"p":"Watch the next gold rushes after AI: <strong>space</strong> and <strong>neurotechnology</strong>."}]},{"h":"Porter's Five Forces in practice","blocks":[{"p":"Define the sector precisely: low-cost airlines differ from full-service carriers. Teams score each factor 1 to 5 and agree an aggregate."},{"p":"Hidden business: in 2020 American Airlines was worth $6.5B but its loyalty programme ~$24B; United $10B vs ~$22B. Co-branded cards earn more than flying, and helped airlines survive COVID."},{"p":"Homework: run Five Forces on your industry and ask whether platforms, data and AI raise or dilute each force."},{"img":"assets/figures/c13-2.png","title":"FIVE FORCES: INDIAN LOW-COST AIRLINES","alt":"Rivalry: HIGH New entrants: MODERATE 123Buyer power: HIGH Same routes, price wars, perishable Regulation, slots and capital, but Low switching cost and full price seats, high fixed cost leasing and UDAN help transparency online 4Supplier power: VERY HIGH 5Substitutes: MODERATE 6Verdict Two aircraft makers, fuel firms, Trains and buses, depending on time Structurally unattractive, even for pilot shortage, slots sensitivity efficient operators"}]},{"h":"Internal analysis: the value chain and linkages","blocks":[{"p":"The firm is a series of activities that should each add value; the goal is <strong>margin</strong>. Value chains are <strong>company specific</strong> (Tata Motors differs from Maruti)."},{"p":"<strong>Linkages</strong> create 1 + 1 = 11: HR training lifts sales performance; IoT monitoring improves service; a consultancy links its expert database (and an internal AI agent) to sales proposals."},{"p":"<strong>Harley-Davidson</strong> linked its plant <strong>and suppliers</strong> through IoT, cutting production scheduling from 21 days to 6 hours to offer the customisation younger riders want."},{"img":"assets/figures/c13-3.png","title":"PORTER'S VALUE CHAIN: PRIMARY ACTIVITIES","alt":"Inbound Operations Outbound Sales and Service Margin logistics logistics marketing Supplier links, Turning inputs Distribution Campaigns, CRM, Maintenance, Value added shipment into products channels pricing after-sales minus cost tracking"}]},{"h":"Group exercise: AI across the value chain","blocks":[{"p":"Groups mapped AI onto a metals firm, a fintech (fraud checks, video KYC, branch decisions) and DHL (robots, AI invoicing, route optimisation)."},{"p":"Strategy decides where AI goes: <strong>cost leadership</strong> points AI at routing and back-office cost; <strong>differentiation</strong> points it at booking, tracking and returns. Value chains extend into <strong>value webs</strong> of partners."}]},{"h":"VRIO and emergent strategy","blocks":[{"p":"A resource gives <strong>sustained advantage</strong> only if valuable, rare, inimitable and backed by organisation. <strong>Intended</strong> strategy rarely equals <strong>realised</strong> strategy; emergent strategy fills the gap. Aim to minimise it."}]}],"takeaways":["A value proposition states who you serve, which need you meet and why you are better than alternatives.","Avoid marketing myopia: define your business by the customer need, not the product.","Use PESTEL and Five Forces for external analysis, value chain and VRIO for internal analysis.","Five Forces show some industries (airlines) are structurally unattractive however efficient you are.","Value chain analysis is company specific; the real gains come from linkages.","IoT and AI pay off when they connect partners and suppliers, as at Harley-Davidson.","Business strategy (cost leadership or differentiation) decides where AI is applied.","The CDAIO adds the digital and AI lens to every framework the strategy team uses."],"quote":"Think of linkages not as 1 plus 1 equals 2, but as 1 plus 1 equals 11.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-13.mp4","videoSecs":484,"notes":"media/notes/class-13-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"What is marketing myopia?","a":"Focusing on your product instead of the customer need it serves","w":["Spending too little on marketing","Targeting too many segments","Ignoring competitors' prices"],"x":"Railroads saw themselves as rail, not mobility, and lost to cars."},{"q":"PESTEL covers Political, Economic, Social, Technological, Environmental and...","a":"Legal","w":["Logistical","Leadership","Labour"],"x":"PESTEL and Five Forces are tools for external analysis."},{"q":"What does VRIO stand for?","a":"Valuable, Rare, Inimitable, Organisation","w":["Value, Revenue, Innovation, Operations","Vision, Resources, Investment, Outcomes","Velocity, Reach, Impact, Ownership"],"x":"A resource gives sustained advantage only if all four hold."},{"q":"In 2020, American Airlines' loyalty programme was worth roughly how much compared to the airline itself?","a":"~$24B vs $6.5B for the airline","w":["About the same","Half the airline's value","Nothing; it was a cost centre"],"x":"Co-branded cards earn more than flying: the 'hidden business'."},{"q":"Harley-Davidson's IoT links cut production scheduling from...","a":"21 days to 6 hours","w":["6 months to 6 weeks","1 week to 1 day","48 hours to 4 hours"],"x":"Linkages to plant and suppliers enabled the customisation younger riders want."},{"q":"If a firm competes on cost leadership, where should AI mainly go?","a":"Routing and back-office cost","w":["Booking experience and personalisation","Brand campaigns","Premium product design"],"x":"Differentiation would point AI at booking, tracking and returns instead."}],"cards":[{"f":"Value chain primary activities","b":"Inbound logistics, operations, outbound logistics, sales and marketing, service, leading to margin."},{"f":"Linkages: 1 + 1 = 11","b":"Real gains come from connecting activities (e.g. HR training lifting sales)."},{"f":"Critical Success Factors (MIT, 1979)","b":"Ancestor of modern KPIs; still guides executive dashboard design."},{"f":"Indian low-cost airlines verdict","b":"Structurally unattractive: high rivalry, high buyer power, very high supplier power."},{"f":"Textbook value proposition","b":"For [segment], we provide [benefit], how, unlike the alternatives."},{"f":"Kodak's myopia","b":"Saw itself as film, not preserving memories; invented then shelved digital."},{"f":"Intended vs realised strategy","b":"They rarely match; emergent strategy fills the gap."}]},{"id":"c14","num":14,"module":"strategy","title":"Enterprise Architecture","subtitle":"The Glue from Strategy to Execution, CRM Basics and IBM's Salesforce Transformation in the AI Era","date":"28 June 2026","faculty":"Guest faculty: Senior Product Manager, IBM","workshop":false,"hook":"Enterprise architecture is a business scaling discipline, not just an IT topic. It is the glue that turns strategy into execution, and IBM's move of 30,000 to 40,000 sellers onto Salesforce shows how to build it incrementally and then add AI as a layer on top.","sections":[{"h":"Why enterprise architecture matters","blocks":[{"p":"A guest session from IBM&#x27;s sales transformation lead. EA is a <strong>business scaling and transformation discipline</strong>, not just an IT topic."},{"p":"A comic video showed a simple web shop drowning in duplicate orders: one badly designed system can damage the whole business."},{"p":"Amazon, Netflix, Uber and Tesla stand out for their <strong>ability to scale decisions</strong> to millions of customers. Transformations need strategy, people, process, data and technology aligned; if one layer is off, they fail (buying Salesforce without changing processes changes little)."},{"p":"CEOs know their strategy but struggle to execute it. EA is <strong>the glue between strategy and execution</strong>."}]},{"h":"The four layers of the EA pyramid","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>Business</strong> architecture sets requirements and the future state; <strong>data</strong>, <strong>application</strong> and <strong>technology</strong> layers exist to support it. EA planning needs many stakeholders: current state, future vision, roadmap."},{"p":"A restaurant that grows from one store to five cities must move from paper registers to a central database, cloud inventory system and point-of-sale devices. At 500 stores the question becomes how to change without breaking anything."},{"img":"assets/figures/c14-1.png","title":"THE FOUR LAYERS OF ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE (TOP DRIVES BOTTOM)","alt":"Business architecture Data architecture Application Technology architecture architecture Requirements, problems, Databases, data flows, Systems and tools impacted Hosting, networks, future state inputs and outputs by decisions integration, devices"}]},{"h":"The 'hairball' problem","blocks":[{"p":"A video showed one-off data feeds built for individual users multiplying into a <strong>hairball architecture</strong> nobody controls, so replacing one application breaks others."},{"p":"The fix: every project writes a <strong>project start architecture</strong>, reviewed by an <strong>architecture board</strong>. EA documents the current state, evaluates designs and sketches the future, preserving the ability to change."},{"p":"EA stays critical while humans make decisions; guardrails may feel like a blocker to app teams, but systems thinking protects the enterprise."},{"img":"assets/figures/c14-2.png","title":"EXAMPLE: ONE RESTAURANT VS A FIVE-CITY CHAIN","alt":"One store Five stores across India Few people, few resources Business: one view of inventory Pen-and-paper inventory register Data: centralised database to Manual order taking Application: cloud inventory system (e.g. SAP) Works because scale is small Technology: Wi-Fi and point-of-sale in every store"}]},{"h":"CRM fundamentals","blocks":[{"p":"Eras: <strong>ERP</strong> (1980s, systems of record), <strong>digital</strong> (2000s, systems of engagement), <strong>AI</strong> (2020s, systems of intelligence). CRM tracks the customer life cycle from outreach to acquisition, conversion and retention. Salesforce automates lead conversion, scores leads with AI (Einstein) and gives managers real-time dashboards and forecasts."},{"img":"assets/figures/c14-3.png","title":"CORE CRM FLOW","alt":"Lead Contact Account Opportunity Forecast and quote Someone met, e.g. a Qualified after calls The company the A dollar value: won Territories, business card at a and follow-ups contact works for or lost dashboards, billing conference"}]},{"h":"IBM's Salesforce transformation","blocks":[{"p":"IBM was the only Fortune 100 firm not on Salesforce. In 2021 it chose to move <strong>30,000 to 40,000 sellers</strong> in one year, retiring many of ~150 tools for quotes, contracts and billing."},{"p":"Requirements varied: accurate data on 500,000+ accounts, contacts that update when people change jobs, restricted government accounts in the UK and US, a Japanese-language interface."},{"p":"Delivery: <strong>cross-functional workstreams</strong> per CRM module to cut dependencies, supported by business, platform and Salesforce architects; <strong>scrum of scrums</strong> in two-week sprints against a quarterly release plan."},{"p":"Architecture: an <strong>interim architecture in 30 days</strong>, business architecture refined in dedicated sessions, and the full stack <strong>evolved incrementally</strong> from feedback. Start any analysis with the business problem; customisation was kept in-house."}]},{"h":"Adding AI as a layer, not a replacement","blocks":[{"p":"The bottom layers (cloud, applications, enterprise data and knowledge) stay; AI agents, copilots and models are added on top. IBM is making the marketing-to-sales hand-off smarter and auto-generating quotes and contracts, with <strong>governance and</strong> <strong>responsible AI</strong> to guard against hallucination. Loss reasons captured in CRM could be analysed by AI to turn losses into wins. Do not migrate to a fancier tool just for AI features: <strong>become AI smart</strong> on the architecture you have."},{"img":"assets/figures/c14-4.png","title":"AI-ERA ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE: NEW LAYERS ON AN INTACT BASE","alt":"1AI agents 2Copilots and assistants 3LLM and AI models Automate flows such as Help sellers act on high-propensity Scoring, drafting quotes and marketing-to-sales lead routing leads contracts Enterprise data and knowledge Business applications 456Governance and responsible AI Unchanged foundation CRM, ERP and others stay in place Guard against hallucination and poor-quality leads"}]}],"takeaways":["Enterprise architecture is a business scaling discipline that links strategy to execution.","Great companies win by scaling decisions; all transformation layers must stay aligned.","The pyramid runs business, data, application, technology: the business layer drives the rest.","Uncontrolled point-to-point fixes create a hairball; architecture boards and start architectures prevent it.","CRM flows from lead to contact, account and opportunity, with territories and forecasts.","Large transformations need cross-functional workstreams, agile delivery and incremental architecture.","Start every architecture analysis from a clearly defined business problem.","Treat AI as a governed layer on top of existing architecture, not a reason to rip and replace."],"quote":"Look at AI as a layer, not as a solution in itself. Try to become AI smart.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-14.mp4","videoSecs":455,"notes":"media/notes/class-14-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"Enterprise architecture is best described as...","a":"The glue between strategy and execution, a business scaling discipline","w":["An IT-only documentation exercise","A cloud migration plan","A CRM product"],"x":"CEOs know strategy but struggle to execute; EA connects them."},{"q":"Which layer of the EA pyramid drives the others?","a":"Business architecture","w":["Technology architecture","Data architecture","Application architecture"],"x":"Business sets requirements; data, application and technology layers exist to support it."},{"q":"What is the 'hairball' problem?","a":"One-off point-to-point data feeds multiply until nobody controls the architecture","w":["Too many cloud vendors","Legacy mainframes","Duplicate CRM licences"],"x":"Fix: project start architectures reviewed by an architecture board."},{"q":"What is the correct core CRM flow?","a":"Lead, contact, account, opportunity, forecast and quote","w":["Account, lead, quote, contact, opportunity","Opportunity, lead, contact, account","Contact, quote, lead, forecast"],"x":"A lead becomes a qualified contact at an account; an opportunity has a dollar value."},{"q":"How should AI be added to existing enterprise architecture?","a":"As a governed layer on top of an intact base","w":["By ripping and replacing core systems","By migrating to whichever tool has the newest AI features","Only through new standalone apps"],"x":"'Look at AI as a layer, not as a solution in itself. Try to become AI smart.'"},{"q":"Systems of record, engagement and intelligence correspond to which eras?","a":"ERP (1980s), digital (2000s), AI (2020s)","w":["Cloud, mobile, ERP","AI, ERP, digital","Mainframe, PC, internet"],"x":"CRM sits across these eras."}],"cards":[{"f":"IBM's Salesforce move","b":"30,000-40,000 sellers moved in one year (2021), retiring many of ~150 tools."},{"f":"Four EA layers","b":"Business, data, application, technology (top drives bottom)."},{"f":"Project start architecture","b":"Each project documents its architecture for review by an architecture board."},{"f":"Transformation alignment","b":"Strategy, people, process, data and technology must all line up."},{"f":"Interim architecture","b":"IBM produced one in 30 days, then evolved the full stack incrementally."},{"f":"Scrum of scrums","b":"Coordinates many agile teams; IBM used two-week sprints against quarterly releases."},{"f":"Restaurant scaling example","b":"From pen-and-paper registers to a central database, cloud inventory and point-of-sale."}]},{"id":"c15","num":15,"module":"transformation","title":"Do You Need Digital Transformation","subtitle":"IT vs IS, Aligning Technology with Business Strategy, the Information Intensity Matrix and How Digital Firms Grow","date":"6 July 2026","faculty":"Faculty: Information Systems (Digital Transformation module)","workshop":false,"hook":"The first session of the digital transformation module takes a bottom-up view: check whether your industry is ripe for disruption, treat technology as an enabler of business strategy rather than a strategy of its own, and remember that transformation is mostly process, people and leadership.","sections":[{"h":"Opening: technology is not a solution","blocks":[{"p":"The new faculty (information systems, ex-Samsung R&amp;D engineer, researcher in internet governance and AI-enabled organisational learning) will cover digital transformation bottom-up: do you need it, technology, strategy, change and leadership, then platform business models."},{"p":"Disclaimer: management is a <strong>social science</strong>. Success needs a hundred things to go right; failure needs only one to go wrong. Cartoons on blockchain and agentic AI hype made the point that <strong>technology-first thinking</strong> is a leading cause of transformation failure."}]},{"h":"Does IT (or AI) matter?","blocks":[{"p":"Nicholas Carr&#x27;s &#x27;IT Doesn&#x27;t Matter&#x27; (HBR 2003) used the <strong>resource-based view</strong>: only resources that are valuable, rare, inimitable and non-substitutable (VRIN) give sustained advantage, and commoditising IT does not. Lessons: spend less, follow rather than lead, manage vulnerabilities."},{"p":"Microsoft&#x27;s CEO argues that for frontier firms the risk of under-investing in AI exceeds the risk of over-investing; most firms, however, use technology to <strong>enable</strong> processes, new business models and customer experience."},{"p":"A participant noted that GenAI lets business leaders experiment without depending on engineers. Experiments are a must; big bets should be <strong>inside-out</strong>, starting from the capability the business needs."}]},{"h":"IT vs IS, and the leadership trap","blocks":[{"p":"One enterprise software firm told staff to use AI for &#x27;efficiency&#x27;; output rose but no chargeable features appeared, so it redefined the metric as <strong>billable features</strong>. Leaders must define value metrics clearly."},{"p":"Beware <strong>AI washing</strong>: using AI as the stated reason for lay-offs or changes that were wanted anyway."},{"img":"assets/figures/c15-1.png","title":"INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY VS INFORMATION SYSTEMS","alt":"Information technology Information system The technology itself: hardware, code, tools Technology interacting with people, processes, structure and culture Blockchain, AI, cloud, ERP software vsThe same ERP can succeed in one firm and fail in Same features wherever it is bought another Vendors such as Oracle and SAP build it Power shifts and job fears drive resistance Users such as Tata Steel or Asian Paints live with it"}]},{"h":"Aligning technology with business strategy","blocks":[{"p":"Functional strategies (operations, marketing, technology) must stay <strong>subservient to business strategy</strong>. There is no &#x27;AI strategy&#x27;, only a business strategy enabled by AI; digital transformation is really business transformation using digital technologies."},{"p":"Firms can mix strategies across units (<strong>ambidextrous organisations</strong>), but each unit needs its own culture."},{"img":"assets/figures/c15-2.png","title":"TECHNOLOGY MUST SERVE THE BUSINESS STRATEGY","alt":"1Cost leadership 2Product differentiation 3Customer niche Cut cost X: lean IT that lowers Raise willingness to pay Y: best Raise customer lifetime value for a inventory, working capital and suppliers, talent and tools, e.g. chosen segment (a book-ahead process cost (Walmart) security (iPhone) restaurant)"}]},{"h":"Value chain and the information intensity matrix","blocks":[{"p":"Technology can improve individual value chain activities and create <strong>forward and backward linkages</strong> (service data improving R&amp;D and operations; quality data fixing production fast)."},{"p":"Ask two questions: how <strong>information intensive is your value chain</strong>, and how much better could your <strong>product or service</strong> become with more, timelier information (a bank can price loans per customer)? Do it for your industry, then your firm."},{"p":"Incumbents miss threats because success blinds them. Build <strong>absorptive capacity</strong> and <strong>boundary-spanning roles</strong>: place sharp people where innovation and venture capital are flowing. Reading: Rogers&#x27; Digital Transformation Playbook and Roadmap, Platform Revolution."},{"img":"assets/figures/c15-3.png","title":"INFORMATION INTENSITY MATRIX (PORTER AND MILLAR)","alt":"High value-chain intensity, low product content 12High on both axes Oil refining, large-scale cement logistics: digitise Banking, insurance, newspapers: transform or be disrupted operations and logistics (Digit, Airbnb, Uber) 3Low on both axes 4Low intensity, high product content Simple physical goods such as soap: less pressure to Products whose information can improve them; look harder at transform your chain"}]},{"h":"How digital firms grow differently","blocks":[{"p":"Ansoff&#x27;s matrix: grow by <strong>scale</strong> (new markets) or <strong>scope</strong> (new products). Traditional firms diversify into adjacent industries slowly (Bajaj: two-wheelers to insurance, loans, a full BFSI and tech player); data-rich digital firms leap further (Amazon: books to marketplace to AWS)."},{"p":"Platforms scale without being limited by their own capital: Amazon, Quora or Uber add sellers, users or drivers on the same technology, while Walmart had to build stores."},{"p":"Class debate: scale is not profit (Amazon&#x27;s retail is funded by AWS; many scaled IPOs lose money), and IBM-Maersk&#x27;s TradeLens blockchain was shelved after five years for lack of adoption. Digital must serve a long-term business strategy."}]},{"h":"Digitisation, digitalisation and transformation","blocks":[{"p":"Automating single tasks makes you a <strong>faster caterpillar</strong>; redesigning processes makes you a <strong>butterfly</strong>. A US pharmacy chain gained billions mainly by <strong>re-ordering process steps</strong>, with minimal technology change."},{"p":"With an ERP, either change your process to the software&#x27;s best practice (resistance, training) or customise the software (cost, lost best practice). Fix the process first, then bring in technology."},{"p":"Change management is the biggest reason for failure and success: &#x27;culture eats strategy for breakfast&#x27;. Transformation is maybe 25% technology and must start with leadership. Next week: the <strong>Gartner hype cycle</strong>."},{"img":"assets/figures/c15-4.png","title":"FROM FASTER CATERPILLAR TO BUTTERFLY","alt":"Digitisation Digitalisation Business process Digital transformation re-engineering Data and single tasks go A whole process redesigned Fix the process before Broad, leadership-led change digital (resume screening) (recruitment) adding technology across functions"}]}],"takeaways":["Technology-first thinking is a leading cause of digital transformation failure.","Most firms use technology as an enabler; experiment widely, but invest inside-out.","Information systems are technology plus people, process, structure and culture.","There is no AI strategy, only a business strategy enabled by AI.","Cost leaders use IT to cut cost; differentiators use it to raise willingness to pay.","High information intensity in value chain and product means transform or be disrupted.","Digital firms grow in scope and scale faster, but scale is not the same as profit.","Fix the process and manage change before automating: become a butterfly, not a faster caterpillar."],"quote":"Never make a technology strategy. Always make a business strategy which AI can enable.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-15.mp4","videoSecs":469,"notes":"media/notes/class-15-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"What is the difference between IT and IS?","a":"IS is technology interacting with people, processes, structure and culture","w":["IS is just hardware","IT includes organisational culture","They are the same"],"x":"The same ERP can succeed in one firm and fail in another."},{"q":"The class says there is no 'AI strategy', only...","a":"A business strategy enabled by AI","w":["A technology roadmap","A data strategy","A vendor strategy"],"x":"'Never make a technology strategy. Always make a business strategy which AI can enable.'"},{"q":"Which sector is high on both value-chain and product information intensity?","a":"Banking and insurance","w":["Soap manufacturing","Oil refining","Cement production"],"x":"High on both axes means transform or be disrupted."},{"q":"'A faster caterpillar' refers to...","a":"Automating single tasks without redesigning the process","w":["A fully transformed digital business","A slow cloud migration","A failed AI pilot"],"x":"Redesigning processes makes you a butterfly."},{"q":"According to the class, transformation is roughly what share technology?","a":"About 25%","w":["About 90%","About 50%","0%"],"x":"The rest is process, people and leadership; 'culture eats strategy for breakfast'."},{"q":"What is the leading cause of digital transformation failure noted in this class?","a":"Technology-first thinking","w":["Too little budget","Choosing the wrong cloud","Hiring too many data scientists"],"x":"Technology is an enabler of business strategy, not a strategy of its own."}],"cards":[{"f":"VRIN","b":"Valuable, rare, inimitable, non-substitutable: resources that give sustained advantage."},{"f":"Ambidextrous organisation","b":"Different strategies across units, each with its own culture."},{"f":"Ansoff's matrix","b":"Grow by scale (new markets) or scope (new products)."},{"f":"TradeLens lesson","b":"IBM-Maersk's blockchain was shelved after 5 years for lack of adoption."},{"f":"AI washing in lay-offs","b":"Using AI as the stated reason for changes wanted anyway."},{"f":"Absorptive capacity","b":"Ability to spot and absorb outside innovation; build boundary-spanning roles."},{"f":"ERP dilemma","b":"Change your process to the software's best practice, or customise the software at a cost."}]},{"id":"c16","num":16,"module":"transformation","title":"Technology Capabilities and Digital Business Models","subtitle":"the Hype Cycle, Technology Laws, Blockchain Reality Check, Platforms and Ecosystem Orchestrators","date":"12 July 2026","faculty":"Faculty: Information Systems (Digital Transformation module)","workshop":false,"hook":"Time technology investments with the hype cycle, judge each technology by the capabilities it gives rather than its buzz, and remember that every technology pays off in only three ways: better customer experience, more efficient operations or a reinvented business model.","sections":[{"h":"Applying last week's frameworks","blocks":[{"p":"A participant showed how European luxury carmakers lose share in China to BYD and others because design decisions sit in Europe while Chinese rivals release new models weekly; the fix is local autonomy."},{"p":"Another mapped customer complaints for Google Workspace onto the <strong>information intensity matrix</strong>: where information is high but unstructured (HR, legal), specialist AI tools are entering, which fed the product backlog."}]},{"h":"The hype cycle: timing your investment","blocks":[{"p":"Value needs the technology <strong>and</strong> its ecosystem (IoT needs connectivity, cloud, skills and cybersecurity). Frontier firms invest at the trigger; enabling firms should wait for the slope of enlightenment."},{"p":"On Gartner&#x27;s AI cycle mainly computer vision and NLP had reached productivity; for most needs, regression, decision trees and random forests suffice. Firms are moving from LLMs to <strong>SLMs</strong> trained on their own data."},{"p":"Markets reward hype partly because vendors and early-adopter partners amplify it. Beware AI trained on AI-generated content."},{"img":"assets/figures/c16-1.png","title":"GARTNER HYPE CYCLE: WHEN SHOULD YOU INVEST?","alt":"Innovation Peak of inflated Trough of Slope of Plateau of trigger expectations disillusionment enlightenment productivity Frontier firms and Media hype, little Low ROI; technology Experimenters (e.g. Mainstream benefits; startups experiment proof of value or ecosystem immature Nike) start investing cautious adopters join"}]},{"h":"Why technology arrives 'slowly, then suddenly'","blocks":[{"p":"Many separate advances converge: IoT needed tiny low-power sensors, telecoms, cloud and security. <strong>Moore&#x27;s law</strong> (computing cost halves about every 18 months), <strong>Gilder&#x27;s law</strong> (bandwidth and data reliability) and <strong>Metcalfe&#x27;s law</strong> (network value) drive most change."}]},{"h":"Technology capabilities","blocks":[{"p":"A <strong>capability</strong> is what a technology enables; <strong>use cases</strong> are the many ways you apply it. Analyse capabilities, then match them to what your strategy needs (inside-out)."},{"p":"Robotic process automation ranges from invoice OCR to warehouse robots. Brain-computer interfaces are at the innovation trigger, with medical uses first; <strong>adoption</strong> and human psychology, including loss of power, stall most technologies."},{"img":"assets/figures/c16-2.png","title":"START FROM CAPABILITIES, NOT USE CASES","alt":"Social media Cloud 123Mobile Rapid creation and diffusion of Pay-as-you-go flexibility to scale On-demand access anywhere (Uber, information (Dropbox began on AWS) Google Maps) IoT / IIoT 3D printing 456Blockchain Smart connected products: predictive Mass customisation (prosthetics, Nike Distributed, decentralised, immutable maintenance, better design (wind shoes); materials ecosystem lagging ledger (land records) turbines)"}]},{"h":"Blockchain and the metaverse: a reality check","blocks":[{"p":"Blockchain began as a <strong>peer-to-peer</strong> ideal to remove trusted third parties (Szabo&#x27;s &#x27;God protocol&#x27;, Bitcoin). Firms that want control adopt it in a diluted form; much of it could be a cryptographically secured database with role-based access."},{"p":"Decent fits: immutable records with history, such as <strong>public permissioned land registries</strong>, and smart contracts (try Hyperledger). Ask: could this be done without blockchain?"},{"p":"Metaverse platforms (Decentraland, Sandbox) are used mainly for gaming and branding (HSBC owns land); Meta&#x27;s rebrand did not pay off yet."}]},{"h":"What a business model is","blocks":[{"p":"A business model covers <strong>value proposition</strong> (why customers care), <strong>value creation</strong> (activities, partners, resources) and <strong>value capture</strong> (revenue model). The <strong>business model canvas</strong> (Zara example) maps all three."},{"p":"Theory distils thousands of businesses; real firms may mix archetypes, and digital technology enables new channels or revenue models."},{"img":"assets/figures/c16-3.png","title":"EVERY TECHNOLOGY PAYS OFF IN ONLY THREE WAYS","alt":"Customer experience: dramatically better Business impact of technology Business model: reinvented Operations: far more efficient value creation and capture processes"}]},{"h":"Digital business model archetypes","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>Platforms</strong> create value by facilitating interactions, as newspapers did for advertisers; digital makes matching easy and powerful. Incumbents miss them through the <strong>innovator&#x27;s dilemma</strong>; counter with R&amp;D, boundary spanners and accelerators (Samsung put half its IoT budget into startups)."},{"p":"<strong>Ecosystem orchestrators</strong> set the rules: Microsoft opened Windows APIs while Mac stayed closed; Apple later opened the App Store; Google built Android with handset makers; SAP built a partner community. Big firms race to orchestrate the next ecosystem. AI business models: AI as a service via APIs, AI-assisted work, <strong>autonomous agents</strong> (goal-driven automation) and enterprise SLMs over internal documents. Next: platforms in depth and digital leadership."},{"img":"assets/figures/c16-4.png","title":"DIGITAL BUSINESS MODEL ARCHETYPES","alt":"Pipeline Platform 123Subscription and freemium Linear value chain; tech cuts cost X Facilitates valuable interactions; Recurring revenue, higher lifetime or raises price Y (Zara, Asian Paints commissions and listing fees (Uber, value (Netflix, Spotify) AR) Quora) 4Advertising-supported 5Ecosystem orchestrator 6Data and AI-driven Free to users, paid by advertisers Sets the rules for partners (Windows, Collect, analyse, monetise data (Google) iOS, Android, SAP) (Bloomberg, AI APIs, agents)"}]}],"takeaways":["Use the hype cycle to avoid investing too early, too late or for too long.","Value needs a mature technology and a mature ecosystem around it.","Moore's, Gilder's and Metcalfe's laws explain why change feels 'slowly, then suddenly'.","Evaluate technologies by capabilities, then map them to your strategic needs.","Adoption and human psychology stall most technologies, not the technology itself.","Ask whether blockchain is truly needed; most uses fit a well-secured database.","Every technology pays off through customer experience, operations or a new business model.","Platforms and ecosystems scale through partners; orchestrators set the rules."],"quote":"Do not start with use cases. Start with the capabilities a technology provides, then see how they help what you want to do.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-16.mp4","videoSecs":481,"notes":"media/notes/class-16-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"When should enabling (non-frontier) firms typically invest in a technology on the hype cycle?","a":"At the slope of enlightenment","w":["At the innovation trigger","At the peak of inflated expectations","Never; wait for the plateau to end"],"x":"Frontier firms invest at the trigger; most others wait for proof of value."},{"q":"Which law says computing cost halves about every 18 months?","a":"Moore's law","w":["Gilder's law","Metcalfe's law","Amara's law"],"x":"Gilder's law covers bandwidth; Metcalfe's law covers network value."},{"q":"Every technology pays off in only three ways. Which is NOT one of them?","a":"Higher share price","w":["Better customer experience","More efficient operations","A reinvented business model"],"x":"Customer experience, operations or business model: nothing else."},{"q":"What should you analyse before use cases?","a":"Technology capabilities","w":["Vendor pricing","Competitor press releases","Gartner rankings"],"x":"'Do not start with use cases. Start with the capabilities a technology provides.'"},{"q":"A useful test before choosing blockchain is...","a":"Could this be done without blockchain?","w":["Is the token price rising?","Do competitors use it?","Is it on the hype cycle peak?"],"x":"Much of it could be a cryptographically secured database with role-based access."},{"q":"What do ecosystem orchestrators do?","a":"Set the rules for partners (e.g. Windows APIs, iOS, Android, SAP)","w":["Hold all inventory themselves","Only sell advertising","Build every component in-house"],"x":"Big firms race to orchestrate the next ecosystem."}],"cards":[{"f":"Five stages of the hype cycle","b":"Innovation trigger, peak of inflated expectations, trough of disillusionment, slope of enlightenment, plateau of productivity."},{"f":"Metcalfe's law","b":"A network's value grows with the number of its users."},{"f":"Business model components","b":"Value proposition, value creation and value capture."},{"f":"Innovator's dilemma","b":"Incumbents miss new models; counter with R&D, boundary spanners and accelerators."},{"f":"Why change feels 'slowly, then suddenly'","b":"Many separate advances converge (e.g. IoT needed sensors, telecoms, cloud, security)."},{"f":"Platform","b":"Creates value by facilitating interactions between groups (Uber, Quora)."},{"f":"Capability vs use case","b":"A capability is what a technology enables; use cases are the many ways you apply it."}]},{"id":"c17","num":17,"module":"transformation","title":"Platform Business Models and Digital Leadership","subtitle":"Network Effects, Launch and Monetisation, Digital Masters, Governance and Change Management","date":"19 July 2026","faculty":"Faculty: Information Systems (Digital Transformation module)","workshop":false,"hook":"Platforms win by orchestrating valuable interactions and network effects rather than controlling resources. Successful digital transformation needs both digital capability and leadership capability: a top-down vision, strong governance, business-IT alignment and disciplined change management.","sections":[{"h":"AI as a business model, and keeping data safe","blocks":[{"p":"AI is software plus statistics: it can be sold as a product (ChatGPT, Claude enterprise) or as a service via APIs, on cloud or on-premises; Copilot is AI as a service. Agents differ only in the <strong>degree of automation</strong>, which depends on task complexity and regulation."},{"p":"Firms build <strong>digital twins</strong> of employees from their emails, channels and documents to preserve knowledge (consent rules differ: barred in Europe). Participants shared in-house LLMs, five agents running night-shift deployments, and an AI voice agent selling insurance. Data governance comes before rollout: isolated machines, enterprise settings, on-premises <strong>small models</strong> connected via MCP."}]},{"h":"What makes a platform","blocks":[{"p":"A platform connects <strong>distinct groups with different value propositions</strong>, lets them interact directly and <strong>facilitates</strong> that interaction. Skype and Zoom are communication tools; a shopping mall or the Amazon marketplace is a platform; no inventory is a good indicator."},{"p":"Hybrids are common: Amazon sells its own goods; Airbnb buys property where demand is proven; food apps open cloud kitchens. Platform-to-pipeline is a data game; pipeline-to-platform is usually pushed by the environment (Intuit)."},{"img":"assets/figures/c17-1.png","title":"PIPELINE VS PLATFORM","alt":"Pipeline (reseller) Platform Linear activities ending in a product or service Facilitates interaction between distinct groups Holds inventory (BigBasket, Netflix licensing) Little or no inventory (Amazon marketplace, Uber) vs Focus: optimise internal resources and cost Focus: orchestrate external participants, ecosystem value Metrics: efficiency, cost X or price Y Metrics: producer-consumer ratio, liquidity, trust"}]},{"h":"Network effects and the money side","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>Same-side</strong> effects: more riders attract riders. <strong>Cross-side</strong> effects: more drivers attract riders and vice versa. Either can be positive or negative."},{"p":"Newspapers and Facebook keep readers free (subsidy side) and charge advertisers (money side); Uber&#x27;s two sides value each other almost equally. Platforms invest in the subsidy side (Google search quality) to keep it engaged."}]},{"h":"Launching, monetising and measuring platforms","blocks":[{"p":"The platform&#x27;s job shifts from resource control to <strong>orchestration</strong> and governance, balancing <strong>ecosystem value</strong> rather than one side. Charge only because you enlarge the pie: instant access, location sharing, feedback and choice. Options: access fees, <strong>enhanced access</strong> (featured listings) and <strong>enhanced curation</strong> where trust matters (SitterCity). Fight leakage (DriveU, Urban Company) by adding value only the platform can give."},{"p":"Key metrics: the right <strong>producer-consumer ratio</strong> (it differs by business and city) at growth, and <strong>liquidity</strong> (intent leading to a transaction), raised by good filters and trust."},{"img":"assets/figures/c17-2.png","title":"SOLVING THE CHICKEN-AND-EGG (PENGUIN) PROBLEM AT LAUNCH","alt":"1Follow the rabbit 2Seeding 3Micro-market Start as a pipeline, open to sellers Create content yourself and set norms Win one niche or city, then expand later (Amazon) (Quora, Stack Overflow, iStock) (Stack Overflow began with code) Single side Money vs subsidy side 456Keep transactions on-platform Give one side a useful tool first Charge the side that values access Add value beyond the match: safety, (redBus software for operators) more (advertisers, not readers) payments, insurance, choice"}]},{"h":"Digital masters: two capabilities","blocks":[{"p":"Frameworks from MIT CISR, Deloitte and McKinsey overlap; map new ones onto what you know. Experiment at the edge: experiments that <strong>complement</strong> your value proposition (Nike&#x27;s sensors and apps) and ones that <strong>challenge</strong> it."},{"p":"A study of hundreds of firms found <strong>no successful bottom-up transformation</strong>: digital capability can be hired, leadership vision cannot."},{"p":"Customer experience is visible and copied quickly; design it <strong>outside-in</strong> against the best services customers use, and change before you must (the Met museum). Operations gains stay hidden and hard to copy, so they give longer advantage. Business models can be substituted (Encyclopaedia Britannica), extended (Nike) or transformed (GE&#x27;s outcome-based model)."},{"img":"assets/figures/c17-3.png","title":"DIGITAL MASTERS MATRIX (DIGITAL VS LEADERSHIP CAPABILITY)","alt":"1Digital masters 2Fashionistas High digital and high leadership capability High digital, low leadership: many projects, no transformative vision 3Conservatives 4Beginners Low digital, high leadership: vision but too risk-averse Low on both; often no perceived threat and slow"}]},{"h":"Leading and governing the change","blocks":[{"p":"A French directories firm reframed itself from printing books to <strong>connecting people</strong> and set bold digital revenue targets. Leading firms ran campaigns to raise every employee&#x27;s <strong>digital IQ</strong>."},{"p":"Governance is political: decide who decides (RACI) on infrastructure, apps and data. Digital masters had 32% higher shared business-IT understanding. Track operational (months), tactical (about a year) and strategic (years) metrics; an agile, fail-fast mindset helps."},{"p":"&#x27;It is not about technology, it is about sociology.&#x27; Use <strong>Kotter&#x27;s</strong> model (urgency, coalition, vision, communication, short-term wins), ADKAR or Lewin; explain the why and celebrate small wins."},{"img":"assets/figures/c17-4.png","title":"LEADERSHIP CAPABILITIES FOR DIGITAL TRANSFORMATION","alt":"Craft a Engage the Govern the Technology Manage change transformative organisation transformation leadership Substitute, extend or Raise everyone's Transformation Business-IT Kotter: urgency, transform digital IQ office, RACI, alignment, decision coalition, vision, steering rights wins"}]}],"takeaways":["Platforms facilitate interactions between distinct groups; pipelines sell what they make or stock.","Network effects (same-side and cross-side) are the engine of platforms.","Price by who values access more: money side vs subsidy side.","Solve the launch problem with rabbit, seeding, micro-market or single-side strategies.","Measure platforms by producer-consumer ratio, liquidity and trust, not efficiency.","Digital masters combine digital capability with top-down leadership capability.","Operations improvements are harder to copy than customer-experience gains.","Govern decision rights, align business and IT, and manage change deliberately."],"quote":"There was not even one case of successful digital transformation happening bottom-up.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-17.mp4","videoSecs":482,"notes":"media/notes/class-17-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"What is a good indicator that a business is a platform rather than a pipeline?","a":"It holds little or no inventory","w":["It has a mobile app","It uses cloud computing","It sells subscriptions"],"x":"Platforms facilitate direct interaction between distinct groups (Amazon marketplace, Uber)."},{"q":"More drivers attracting more riders is an example of...","a":"A cross-side network effect","w":["A same-side network effect","A viral effect","Economies of scale"],"x":"Same-side: more riders attract riders. Cross-side: one side attracts the other."},{"q":"Newspapers keep readers free and charge advertisers. Readers are the...","a":"Subsidy side","w":["Money side","Supply side","Leakage side"],"x":"Charge the side that values access more."},{"q":"Stack Overflow starting with code questions only is which launch strategy?","a":"Micro-market","w":["Follow the rabbit","Single side","Seeding"],"x":"Win one niche or city, then expand."},{"q":"Firms with high digital capability but low leadership capability are called...","a":"Fashionistas","w":["Digital masters","Conservatives","Beginners"],"x":"Many projects, no transformative vision."},{"q":"Why do operations improvements give longer advantage than customer-experience gains?","a":"They stay hidden and are hard to copy","w":["They are cheaper","Customers notice them more","They need no technology"],"x":"Customer experience is visible and copied quickly."}],"cards":[{"f":"Platform metrics","b":"Producer-consumer ratio, liquidity and trust, not efficiency."},{"f":"Liquidity","b":"Share of intent that leads to a transaction; raised by good filters and trust."},{"f":"Follow the rabbit","b":"Start as a pipeline, then open to sellers (Amazon)."},{"f":"Leakage","b":"Users taking transactions off-platform; fight it by adding value only the platform gives."},{"f":"Kotter's model","b":"Urgency, coalition, vision, communication, short-term wins."},{"f":"Bottom-up transformation","b":"In a study of hundreds of firms, not one succeeded bottom-up."},{"f":"Substitute, extend, transform","b":"Ways business models change: Britannica (substituted), Nike (extended), GE (transformed)."}]},{"id":"c18","num":18,"module":"product","title":"Product Thinking and Digital Product Strategy","subtitle":"Problem-First Mindset, Outcomes over Outputs, Product Types, Pricing and the Product Life Cycle","date":"26 July 2026","faculty":"Faculty: Digital Product Management module","workshop":false,"hook":"Digital products are how companies showcase innovation, but building an app is easy and delivering outcomes is hard. Product thinking starts with a problem worth solving, treats every idea as a hypothesis to test, and uses the product's core nature, business model and life-cycle stage to shape strategy and pricing.","sections":[{"h":"Why products matter in digital transformation","blocks":[{"p":"Core pillars of DT go beyond people, process and technology: an <strong>innovation mindset</strong>, <strong>data at the</strong> <strong>centre</strong>, customer experience and customers as <strong>active co-creators</strong>. Products are one way a firm showcases innovation."},{"p":"Users pay only for <strong>value</strong>: convenience, reliability, premium features, priority, peace (an ad-free YouTube). Nike&#x27;s running app sells nothing, yet it builds community, loyalty and stickiness in a world of easy brand switching and <strong>multi-homing</strong>."},{"p":"Not every DT initiative is a digital product: re-engineering internal processes (IoT on a shop floor) is transformation, but a product fulfils a bounded utility for an audience."}]},{"h":"Problem first, outcomes over outputs","blocks":[{"p":"Treat every idea as an <strong>opinion and a hypothesis</strong> to test; keep what is validated, reshape or drop the rest. The hard part is not building an app (hours with no-code) but financial viability and continuous growth."},{"p":"Problem first beats technology first: AI investments without a pertinent problem create the"},{"p":"<strong>productivity paradox</strong>. A problem can be a pain point or a <strong>North Star metric</strong> to improve; passion projects (Google+) can still fail commercially."},{"p":"Tinder won on user experience, not a better algorithm; Tata Nano shows the gap between what customers say and what they do, so combine <strong>attitudinal and behavioural</strong> research throughout."},{"img":"assets/figures/c18-1.png","title":"PROJECT MINDSET VS PRODUCT MINDSET","alt":"Project / build trap Product mindset Focus on outputs and deliverables Focus on outcomes: metrics, value, impact Build it, close it, move on Continuous experimentation after launch vs Solution or technology first (plug AI everywhere) Problem first: is it worth solving, for whom? Success = shipped on time and budget Success = adoption, retention, willingness to pay"}]},{"h":"Lessons from Namma Yatri and UPI","blocks":[{"p":"UPI became a simple mass product that included the informal sector. <strong>Namma Yatri</strong>&#x27;s founders drove autos to empathise with drivers, then changed the pricing model to <strong>zero commission via subscription</strong>, showed pickup and drop before acceptance, and paid drivers directly."},{"p":"It leveraged <strong>digital public infrastructure</strong> (ONDC) to cut upfront technology cost, and widened the problem from point-to-point rides to end-to-end mobility (metro plus last mile)."}]},{"h":"Innovation types and ways of thinking","blocks":[{"p":"Innovation is novelty that creates impact: as an outcome (product, process, business model), as a process and as a culture. Digital innovation also <strong>modifies user habits</strong> and needs top-management buy-in."},{"p":"Sustaining (improve existing), breakthrough (unconventional skills for hard, well-defined problems), disruptive (Netflix vs Blockbuster) and basic research (quantum). Wicked problems such as the SDGs need technology plus other domain expertise. Design thinking workshops, idea champions, six hats, first-principles and <strong>systems thinking</strong> help firms deploy innovation at scale."},{"img":"assets/figures/c18-2.png","title":"DESIGN THINKING: FROM EMPATHY TO EXPERIMENT","alt":"Empathise Define Ideate Prototype Experiment Immerse in the user's Frame the problem Generate many Put something in Test hypotheses, keep context worth solving alternatives users' hands what works"}]},{"h":"Product properties, pricing and go-to-market","blocks":[{"p":"A product&#x27;s core type, <strong>granularity</strong> (modules that can be bundled) and <strong>trialability</strong> (freemium tiers, a free chapter) set the limits of pricing: pay-per-use, commission, subscription, licensing, volume discounts."},{"p":"With <strong>consumerisation of IT</strong>, Dropbox and LLMs launched to consumers first: lower barriers to demonstrate value, cheap experimentation and data for learning; employees then carry habits into the enterprise."},{"p":"Today&#x27;s products ride on cloud, APIs, data and platforms; network effects drive exponential growth, but rapid commoditisation means retention and new value propositions matter most. Pricing often works back from market price sensitivity."},{"img":"assets/figures/c18-3.png","title":"THREE CORE KINDS OF DIGITAL PRODUCT","alt":"Tools and utilities Content-based 123Service-based Fixed purpose, execute a task (MS Value lies in the information itself Value via interactions and touch Office, maps, UPI) (OTT, music, news) points (Practo, Swiggy, e-commerce)"}]},{"h":"Life cycle and the product leader's role","blocks":[{"p":"Every product moves through introduction, growth, maturity and decline. Strategy aims to stay on growth: <strong>strip unused</strong> <strong>attributes</strong>, breakaway repositioning, or <strong>stealth rebranding</strong> after a public failure. SAP&#x27;s cloud shift opened SMEs and new verticals."},{"p":"Product leaders lead without authority across marketing, development and design, balancing <strong>desirability, feasibility and</strong> <strong>viability</strong>; they prioritise, manage trade-offs (sales over-promising, technical debt) and keep a vision and a roadmap that may stay about 25% in flux."}]}],"takeaways":["Building an app is easy; delivering outcomes that users value and pay for is the real product challenge.","Start with a problem worth solving for a defined audience, not with a technology looking for a use.","Every idea is a hypothesis: design small experiments and keep only what the evidence validates.","Customers are active co-creators; empathise by immersing in their context, as Namma Yatri's founders did.","What customers say, what they say they do and what they actually do differ; mix attitudinal and behavioural research.","A product's core type, granularity and trialability shape which pricing and business models are possible.","Consumer-first launches lower the barrier to demonstrating value and pave the road into the enterprise.","Manage the life cycle deliberately: strip, reposition or rebrand to put a mature product back on growth."],"quote":"Building is not at all a difficult task; the ability to deliver outcomes is the essence of product thinking.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-18.mp4","videoSecs":577,"notes":"media/notes/class-18-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"What does a 'product mindset' focus on?","a":"Outcomes: adoption, retention, willingness to pay","w":["Outputs and deliverables","Shipping on time and budget","Plugging AI everywhere"],"x":"The project/build trap measures success by shipping; products measure impact."},{"q":"Treating every idea as a hypothesis means...","a":"Design experiments and keep only what evidence validates","w":["Build every idea in full","Ask the CEO to decide","Rely on what customers say in surveys"],"x":"Keep what is validated, reshape or drop the rest."},{"q":"What does the Tata Nano case show?","a":"The gap between what customers say and what they do","w":["Low price always wins","Better algorithms beat UX","Cars cannot be digital products"],"x":"Combine attitudinal and behavioural research throughout."},{"q":"Namma Yatri changed its pricing model to...","a":"Zero commission via driver subscription","w":["Higher commission per ride","Surge pricing","Advertising-funded rides"],"x":"Founders drove autos to empathise with drivers."},{"q":"MS Office, maps and UPI are which core kind of digital product?","a":"Tools and utilities","w":["Content-based","Service-based","Platform-based"],"x":"Content-based: OTT, music, news. Service-based: Practo, Swiggy."},{"q":"Which three dimensions do product leaders balance?","a":"Desirability, feasibility and viability","w":["Speed, scope and cost","Design, code and testing","Price, place and promotion"],"x":"Product leaders lead without authority across marketing, development and design."}],"cards":[{"f":"Productivity paradox","b":"AI investment without a pertinent problem fails to raise productivity."},{"f":"Design thinking stages","b":"Empathise, define, ideate, prototype, experiment (test)."},{"f":"Trialability","b":"Freemium tiers or a free chapter let users try before buying, shaping pricing."},{"f":"Granularity","b":"Modules that can be bundled or sold separately."},{"f":"Product life cycle","b":"Introduction, growth, maturity, decline."},{"f":"Consumer-first launch","b":"Dropbox and LLMs launched to consumers first; employees carry habits into enterprises."},{"f":"Four innovation types","b":"Sustaining, breakthrough, disruptive and basic research."}]},{"id":"c19","num":19,"module":"product","title":"Product Strategy and Product-Market Fit","subtitle":"Roadmaps, Technical Debt, the Product-Market Fit Pyramid, Business Model Canvas, Design Thinking and AI Product Strategy","date":"2 August 2026","faculty":"Faculty: Digital Product Management module","workshop":false,"hook":"Products succeed when they fit the company's vision, solve a problem customers acknowledge, achieve product-market fit and rest on a viable business model. Design thinking finds problems worth solving; for AI products, the model's quality trade-offs largely lock in the product strategy.","sections":[{"h":"Product planning, roadmaps and technical debt","blocks":[{"p":"Planning runs from a <strong>product vision</strong> tied to the organisation&#x27;s objectives, to portfolio planning, to a (usually quarterly) <strong>roadmap</strong>, then waves and scrums. Some roadmap flux is healthy: a static roadmap means you are not keeping pace. Size the market opportunity (even 1 to 2% of a large market can be worth millions), study <strong>direct and indirect competitors</strong> (tech entrants from unexpected corners) and regulation (real-money gaming apps shut overnight)."},{"p":"<strong>Technical debt</strong> accrues when quality is traded for speed, from architecture choices, integrations and ageing third-party APIs, and when sales over-promise. CXOs must decide which trade-offs they accept and how they will repay them."}]},{"h":"Why products succeed or fail","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>Know your company</strong>: products that do not serve the firm&#x27;s goals end up as spin-offs. Use <strong>personas</strong> to anchor values, define metrics for every stage, and analyse company, customers, collaborators, competitors and climate."},{"p":"<strong>Problem-solution fit</strong>: customers must acknowledge the problem themselves; immerse in their context rather than leading them. In B2B, pilots test fit, but large clients may copy the idea."},{"p":"Target early adopters first, then cross the gap to the <strong>early majority</strong>, who want different things; every feature should map to a value proposition for a segment."},{"img":"assets/figures/c19-1.png","title":"CRITICAL SUCCESS FACTORS FOR A PRODUCT","alt":"Know your company Problem-solution Product-market Business model Growth fit fit Fit the vision, Customers acknowledge Needs + business Viable costs and Scale and expand personas, metrics, the problem value + operations revenues segments 5Cs"}]},{"h":"Product-market fit and experimentation","blocks":[{"p":"PMF is the overlap of <strong>customer needs, business value</strong> flowing back to the firm and <strong>operational capability</strong>; it is a moving target. Track indicators such as <strong>NPS</strong> (% promoters scoring 9 to 10 minus % detractors scoring 0 to 6) and industry indices. Use A/B tests and feature flags to validate ideas, but define the <strong>objective evaluation criterion</strong> and the right population: testing a new website only on subscribers says nothing about new sign-ups."}]},{"h":"The PMF pyramid, MVPs and the business model","blocks":[{"p":"Start testing at the lowest uncertain layer; an <strong>MVP</strong> tests all layers. Market MVPs (explainer video, smoke test) gauge prospects; a fake-door test checks a new feature. CXOs should ask which core value proposition each product delivers and how it ties to the North Star metric."},{"p":"Problem-solution fit does not guarantee PMF, and PMF does not guarantee profit: you need a viable <strong>business model canvas</strong>. Use BMCs for current, future and competitor scenarios (in freemium, paying users fund free ones). The <strong>value proposition</strong> <strong>canvas</strong> maps jobs, pains and gains to pain relievers and gain creators."},{"img":"assets/figures/c19-2.png","title":"PRODUCT-MARKET FIT PYRAMID (TEST HYPOTHESES FROM THE BOTTOM UP)","alt":"Target customer Underserved needs Value proposition Feature set (MVP) User experience Who, and do they Unmet or poorly met Needs plus added Core functionality The visible layer exist? benefits"}]},{"h":"Design thinking in practice: cutting hospital no-shows","blocks":[{"p":"Design thinking (Herbert Simon, Stanford d.school, IDEO) is human-centred innovation balancing <strong>desirability, feasibility and viability</strong>: empathise, define, ideate, prototype (even paper), test, with cycles of divergence and convergence."},{"p":"Groups mapped a cancer patient&#x27;s interview: the real problem was <strong>transport</strong>, not lack of care. Ideas included scheduled rides, USSD and voice tools for feature phones, tele-consults and check-in calls. The metric is <strong>missed appointments</strong> (say 14% down to 7%). The real hospital ran three prototypes, partnered with Lyft&#x27;s API, then sold the solution to other hospitals."},{"img":"assets/figures/c19-3.png","title":"EMPATHY MAP USED IN THE HOSPITAL NO-SHOW EXERCISE","alt":"1Says 2Thinks 3Feels 4Does Direct quotes from the Reasoning and beliefs Emotions: fear, Actions, including what interview frustration, guilt they avoid"}]},{"h":"AI product strategy: the dental AI case","blocks":[{"p":"An AI model is not a product until it offers a complete user experience; seek value propositions possible <strong>only</strong> with AI, and define a <strong>minimum viable quality</strong>. AI mainly does <strong>prediction</strong>; clinicians keep <strong>judgment</strong> and action."},{"p":"Choosing <strong>precision vs sensitivity</strong> locks in strategy: clinicians want missed pathologies caught, while insurers fear false approvals. An <strong>AI factory</strong> operating model lets a firm build, test and ship models fast amid uncertainty."},{"img":"assets/figures/c19-4.png","title":"THE AI FACTORY OPERATING MODEL","alt":"Data pipeline Algorithm Experimentation Software Virtuous cycle development platform infrastructure Ethical sourcing, Build and train Test on real-world Deploy with a UX More usage, more cleaning models data, tune layer data, better models"}]}],"takeaways":["Tie every product to the company's vision and North Star metric, or it will struggle for buy-in.","A roadmap in moderate flux is healthy; a frozen one signals you are not keeping pace with change.","Technical debt is a strategic trade-off: know which shortcuts you accept and how you will repay them.","Customers must acknowledge the problem; immerse yourself in their context rather than leading them.","Product-market fit needs customer needs, business value and operational capability together.","Design experiments with a clear evaluation criterion and a population that matches your target.","Problem-solution fit is not PMF, and PMF is not profit: get the business model canvas right.","For AI products, model quality trade-offs such as precision vs sensitivity decide whom you can serve."],"quote":"The moment you decide on your model's precision and sensitivity, you are pretty much locking your product strategy.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-19.mp4","videoSecs":512,"notes":"media/notes/class-19-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"A roadmap that never changes signals...","a":"You are not keeping pace with change","w":["Excellent planning","Strong governance","Product-market fit"],"x":"Moderate flux is healthy."},{"q":"How is NPS calculated?","a":"% promoters (9-10) minus % detractors (0-6)","w":["Average rating out of 10","% promoters plus % passives","Number of reviews per month"],"x":"NPS is one indicator of product-market fit."},{"q":"Which statement is true?","a":"Problem-solution fit is not PMF, and PMF is not profit","w":["PMF guarantees profit","Problem-solution fit guarantees PMF","A good MVP guarantees a business model"],"x":"You also need a viable business model canvas."},{"q":"Where should you start testing on the PMF pyramid?","a":"At the lowest uncertain layer (target customer)","w":["At the user experience layer","At the feature set","Anywhere; order does not matter"],"x":"Layers: target customer, underserved needs, value proposition, feature set, UX."},{"q":"In the hospital no-show exercise, what was the real problem?","a":"Transport, not lack of care","w":["Doctor shortages","Appointment software bugs","High fees"],"x":"The real hospital partnered with Lyft's API and sold the solution to other hospitals."},{"q":"In AI products, what decision 'pretty much locks your product strategy'?","a":"Choosing the model's precision vs sensitivity","w":["Choosing a cloud provider","Picking a logo","Setting the release date"],"x":"In the dental AI case, clinicians want missed pathologies caught; insurers fear false approvals."}],"cards":[{"f":"Technical debt","b":"Accrues when quality is traded for speed; CXOs decide which shortcuts to accept and how to repay."},{"f":"Fake-door test","b":"Shows a feature that doesn't exist yet to measure interest."},{"f":"Empathy map","b":"Says, thinks, feels, does."},{"f":"Value proposition canvas","b":"Maps jobs, pains and gains to pain relievers and gain creators."},{"f":"AI factory","b":"Data pipeline, algorithm development, experimentation platform, software infrastructure, virtuous cycle."},{"f":"Crossing the gap","b":"Move from early adopters to the early majority, who want different things."},{"f":"5Cs analysis","b":"Company, customers, collaborators, competitors and climate."}]},{"id":"c20","num":20,"module":"product","title":"Prototyping and MVP Execution","subtitle":"Wireframes, Fidelity Levels, Rapid to Extreme Prototyping, AI Design Tools and Building the Riskiest-Assumption MVP","date":"9 August 2026","faculty":"Guest faculty: Product Manager, e- commerce","workshop":false,"hook":"Prototypes give an idea a visual, testable form before anything is built for production; MVPs are the smallest usable product that tests your riskiest assumption end to end. AI tools have cut prototyping time three to five times, but the discipline of choosing what to test, and for whom, remains.","sections":[{"h":"Why prototype: give the idea a visual form","blocks":[{"p":"Describing an e-commerce idea in words (search bar, brand page, cart, checkout, payments) is hard for others to picture. A <strong>wireframe</strong> is a single static screen; linking wireframes into a clickable flow makes a <strong>mock-up or prototype</strong>; adding data and backend integration makes a <strong>proof of concept</strong>. Prototyping lets users, tech, design, finance, compliance, marketing and sales give feasibility input <strong>before</strong> anything reaches production; e.g., tech may say a listing page cannot load millions of items at once."},{"p":"Not every change needs a POC: a simple download button can go straight to build, while data from many services (catalogue, pricing, promotions, payments) calls for a POC to test feasibility."},{"img":"assets/figures/c20-1.png","title":"FROM IDEA TO PROOF OF CONCEPT: THE FIDELITY LADDER","alt":"Concept sketch Low fidelity Mid fidelity High fidelity Proof of concept Visual form of the Wireframe: where What elements look Polished, ready for Working, with data idea elements sit like tech and APIs"}]},{"h":"Fidelity, AI tools and cost","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>Low fidelity</strong> shows where elements sit; <strong>mid</strong> shows what they look like; <strong>high</strong> is the polished, compliant design handed to tech. AI tools have cut prototyping time roughly <strong>3 to 5x</strong>, and stakeholders now expect working POCs rather than static screens."},{"p":"Tools like <strong>Figma</strong> (frames, layers, assets, plugins and now prompt-driven agents) and Claude can generate wireframes or a working POC from a prompt with role, task, context, output format and constraints. Experts still refine by hand, and token costs rise from wireframe to prototype to POC."}]},{"h":"Choosing a prototyping approach","blocks":[{"p":"Match the method to uncertainty and reversibility: if uncertainty is low and the change is a <strong>two-way door</strong> (easy to roll back), just build it; one-way doors and high uncertainty need prototypes. Google Maps&#x27; restaurant pins and flickering &#x27;take flyover&#x27; prompt were refined through rapid iterations."},{"p":"Use <strong>evolutionary or incremental</strong> prototyping for big changes like a hyper-personalised store, and <strong>extreme</strong> prototyping where hardware feel or new integrations (IIoT, OCR) carry risk. <strong>Wizard of Oz</strong> tests AI experiences before the model exists."},{"img":"assets/figures/c20-2.png","title":"TYPES OF PROTOTYPING AND WHEN TO USE THEM","alt":"Rapid (throwaway) Evolutionary 123Incremental Quick mock-up for feedback, then Start small and keep refining with Separate modules built and merged discarded feedback later Extreme Wizard of Oz 456Just build it UX, dummy data, then real APIs: A human plays the AI model behind the Low uncertainty and a two-way door deployable UX"}]},{"h":"MVP execution: test the riskiest assumption","blocks":[{"p":"An MVP collects the most validated learning with the least effort: a <strong>skateboard, not a wheel</strong>. Full builds are one-way doors that commit capital, headcount and reputation, and more features make adoption harder."},{"p":"Long-list all features, identify the <strong>riskiest assumption</strong> and use the PMF pyramid (need, target market, value proposition) before features. Features can pivot (Uber Share), but value proposition and target market cannot without hurting the brand."},{"p":"Cases: Google Maps tested whether people prefer an <strong>interactive in-browser map</strong> over MapQuest&#x27;s reloads; Amazon chose <strong>books</strong> to test online buying and payment. With AI agents, add <strong>guardrails and governance</strong> to the MVP (an agent can retry a button 1,000 times or leak data)."},{"img":"assets/figures/c20-3.png","title":"PRIORITISING MVP FEATURES (MOSCOW)","alt":"1Must have 2Should have 3Could have 4Won't have (yet) Needed to test the Better value: list all Delighters: Park for later releases riskiest assumption coupons that apply hyper-personalised store"}]}],"takeaways":["Wireframes give an idea visual form; linked wireframes form a prototype; adding data and APIs makes a POC.","Prototypes bring tech, finance, compliance and users into the conversation before costly production work.","Pick fidelity by risk: a small UI change needs a wireframe, a multi-system feature needs a POC.","AI tools cut prototyping time 3 to 5x, but prompts need role, task, context, format and constraints.","Two-way-door changes with low uncertainty can be built directly; one-way doors deserve prototypes.","An MVP is the smallest product usable end to end: a skateboard, not a wheel.","Build the MVP around the riskiest assumption and the need, market and value proposition behind it.","MVPs that involve AI agents need governance and guardrails designed in from the start."],"quote":"The right way to build an MVP is to build something usable end to end, not a component that adds no value on its own.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-20.mp4","videoSecs":477,"notes":"media/notes/class-20-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"What turns linked wireframes into a proof of concept?","a":"Adding data and backend/API integration","w":["Adding colours","Printing them","Adding more screens"],"x":"Wireframe (one screen) -> prototype (clickable flow) -> POC (working with data)."},{"q":"By roughly how much have AI tools cut prototyping time?","a":"3 to 5 times","w":["10 to 20 times","Not at all","About 10%"],"x":"Stakeholders now expect working POCs rather than static screens."},{"q":"A low-uncertainty, easily reversible change should usually...","a":"Just be built","w":["Get an extreme prototype","Get a Wizard of Oz test","Be cancelled"],"x":"Two-way doors can be built directly; one-way doors deserve prototypes."},{"q":"What is a Wizard of Oz prototype?","a":"A human plays the AI model behind the UX","w":["A fully automated AI demo","A paper sketch","A prototype built by an agency"],"x":"It tests AI experiences before the model exists."},{"q":"An MVP should be...","a":"The smallest product usable end to end: a skateboard, not a wheel","w":["One polished component","The full product with fewer users","A slide deck"],"x":"Build around the riskiest assumption."},{"q":"In MoSCoW, 'Must have' features are those...","a":"Needed to test the riskiest assumption","w":["That delight users","That the CEO wants","Planned for later releases"],"x":"Should = better value; Could = delighters; Won't = park for later."}],"cards":[{"f":"Low / mid / high fidelity","b":"Low: where elements sit. Mid: what they look like. High: polished, ready for tech."},{"f":"Rapid (throwaway) prototyping","b":"Quick mock-up for feedback, then discarded."},{"f":"Evolutionary prototyping","b":"Start small and keep refining with feedback."},{"f":"Extreme prototyping","b":"UX, then dummy data, then real APIs; used where integrations carry risk."},{"f":"Good prompt for AI design tools","b":"Role, task, context, output format and constraints."},{"f":"What can pivot in an MVP?","b":"Features can pivot; value proposition and target market cannot without hurting the brand."},{"f":"Amazon's MVP choice","b":"Books, to test online buying and payment."}]},{"id":"c21","num":21,"module":"product","title":"Product-Led Growth and Agile Leadership at Scale","subtitle":"PLG Framework and Metrics, Onboarding to Value, Agile Values, Big Room Planning and Scaling Frameworks","date":"16 August 2026","faculty":"Guest faculty: Product leader, IBM","workshop":false,"hook":"Product-led growth lets customers find value in the product before they pay, so the product itself drives acquisition, conversion and expansion, measured by a few shared team metrics. Agility is a set of values and principles, not just a process, and scaling it is above all a leadership problem.","sections":[{"h":"From sales-led to product-led growth","blocks":[{"p":"The classic SaaS model was <strong>sales-led</strong>: marketing acquires leads, sales gets a contract signed, customer success drives engagement, and expansion (upgrades) is where the real money lies. Since about 2015 this has been disrupted by <strong>product-led growth (PLG)</strong>, where customers try the product and find value before paying."},{"p":"PLG answers two pressures: customers&#x27; <strong>willingness to pay is at an all-time low</strong> amid endless choice, and <strong>customer acquisition costs</strong> have hit the roof. Letting people try a base version costs far less than buying customers with campaigns and discounts."},{"p":"It spreads beyond software: adding a chip and sensors to heavy equipment creates data for"},{"p":"subscription services such as maintenance alerts and usage insights. Most participants described their firms as hybrid."},{"img":"assets/figures/c21-1.png","title":"SALES-LED VS PRODUCT-LED GROWTH","alt":"Sales-led Product-led Acquire, monetise, engage, expand Discover, activate, convert, retain and expand Pay first, find value later Try first (freemium, trial), pay once value is vsfound Marketing, sales and success work in silos Shared team metrics across functions High customer acquisition cost Lower acquisition cost; sales focuses on qualified leads"}]},{"h":"Getting users to value: onboarding","blocks":[{"p":"Marketing drives discovery through smart content (Grammarly&#x27;s articles nudging a free trial); customer success then uses emails, in-app guides and chatbots to help users <strong>activate</strong>. Large enterprises still buy through sales, so many firms run both motions."},{"p":"In the <strong>SafetyCulture</strong> exercise (vehicle and site inspection checklists), the class sorted onboarding steps into <strong>high-leverage activities</strong> (invite teammates, create a checklist and options, run the first inspection, confirm email) and removable friction (Slack notifications, branding, optional tutorials). Offer templates to cut effort."}]},{"h":"Measuring product-led growth","blocks":[{"p":"Commentators list 50 to 100 PLG metrics; track <strong>a few key metrics</strong>, and make them <strong>team metrics</strong> shared by marketing, sales and success, not silo metrics."},{"p":"<strong>Activation</strong> is the step where users find value (sending a first document for signature in DocuSign). <strong>Product-qualified leads</strong> replace marketing-qualified ones: a student signing up to DocuSign is a poor prospect, a business owner signing contracts daily is a strong one (Notion&#x27;s tiers show the same logic). Retention and <strong>net dollar retention</strong> show whether paying users stay and grow. Get these right and the strategy works most of the time, unless the product itself has issues."},{"img":"assets/figures/c21-2.png","title":"A FEW KEY PLG METRICS (TEAM, NOT SILO)","alt":"1Engagement score 2Activation rate 3Product-qualified leads Breadth, depth and frequency of use, Share of sign-ups who reach the value Users whose behaviour shows readiness weighted moment to pay Usage retention Net dollar retention 456Engaged but not activated Paying users still active week on (Start revenue + upgrades - churn) / A call list to find and fix the gap week start revenue"}]},{"h":"Agile leadership at enterprise scale","blocks":[{"p":"Companies that transformed industries and survived (IBM pivoting to hybrid cloud, AI and quantum) share agility. Agile is <strong>values and principles</strong> first; Scrum is the most popular framework. Planning is a team exercise, not the product owner&#x27;s alone, and every sprint should yield a potentially shippable increment. If leadership owns all prioritisation and ignores teams closest to customers, agility becomes a <strong>leadership</strong> <strong>problem</strong>. At scale, <strong>big room (PI) planning</strong> aligns many teams on a roadmap and surfaces dependencies. Move from awareness to small-scale delivery to <strong>agility at scale</strong>, from doing agile to being agile."},{"p":"The best sign that leadership behaviour has changed: nine months in, <strong>teams decide without escalation</strong>. For a 3,000-person firm with 18 squads, many chose SAFe; scrum of scrums can suffice until about 35 to 40 teams."},{"img":"assets/figures/c21-3.png","title":"FRAMEWORKS FOR AGILITY AT SCALE","alt":"1Scrum of scrums 2SAFe 3Community-led models Simple, little jargon; works up to Prescriptive team, program and Few prescribed roles; knowledge about 35 to 40 teams portfolio layers; most widely adopted sharing across teams"}]}],"takeaways":["Product-led growth lets customers find value before paying, lowering acquisition cost.","Expansion through upgrades is where most SaaS revenue is earned.","Design onboarding around high-leverage steps and strip out friction that delays value.","Track a handful of shared team metrics rather than dozens of silo metrics.","Activation rate and product-qualified leads tell you who has found value and is ready to pay.","Net dollar retention shows whether your paying base is growing or shrinking.","Agile is values and principles first; Scrum and other frameworks follow from them.","Agility at scale is a leadership problem: the signal is teams deciding without escalation."],"quote":"If you firmly think agility is a leadership problem, you need to assess it from that perspective.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-21.mp4","videoSecs":482,"notes":"media/notes/class-21-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"In product-led growth, customers...","a":"Find value in the product before paying","w":["Pay first and find value later","Only buy through sales reps","Never pay"],"x":"PLG uses freemium or trials to lower acquisition cost."},{"q":"Which two pressures drove PLG?","a":"Low willingness to pay and very high customer acquisition costs","w":["Cheap advertising and loyal customers","Regulation and taxes","Lack of SaaS products"],"x":"Letting people try a base version costs far less than buying customers."},{"q":"What is 'activation'?","a":"The step where users find value (e.g. sending a first document in DocuSign)","w":["Creating an account","Paying the first invoice","Opening a marketing email"],"x":"Activation rate = share of sign-ups reaching the value moment."},{"q":"How is net dollar retention calculated?","a":"(Start revenue + upgrades - churn) / start revenue","w":["New revenue / total revenue","Churned users / total users","Upgrades / sign-ups"],"x":"It shows whether your paying base is growing or shrinking."},{"q":"The best sign leadership behaviour has changed in an agile transformation is...","a":"Teams deciding without escalation","w":["More status reports","Bigger sprint backlogs","Leaders owning all prioritisation"],"x":"'Agility is a leadership problem.'"},{"q":"Scrum of scrums typically works up to about how many teams?","a":"35 to 40","w":["5","200","Unlimited"],"x":"Beyond that, frameworks like SAFe are often chosen."}],"cards":[{"f":"Sales-led motion","b":"Acquire, monetise, engage, expand."},{"f":"Product-led motion","b":"Discover, activate, convert, retain and expand."},{"f":"Product-qualified lead","b":"A user whose behaviour shows readiness to pay."},{"f":"Team metrics vs silo metrics","b":"Track a few metrics shared by marketing, sales and success."},{"f":"Big room (PI) planning","b":"Aligns many teams on a roadmap and surfaces dependencies."},{"f":"SAFe","b":"Prescriptive team, program and portfolio layers; most widely adopted scaling framework."},{"f":"Where SaaS money is made","b":"Expansion through upgrades."}]},{"id":"c22","num":22,"module":"product","title":"Digital and AI Governance and the AI Toolkit for Product Leaders","subtitle":"Decision Rights, Governance Archetypes, Operating Models and AI Tools across the Product Life Cycle","date":"23 August 2026","faculty":"Guest faculty: Thoughtworks digital health leader; product manager, e-commerce","workshop":false,"hook":"Governance is about who has the right to decide and who is accountable; AI is pushing many firms from feudal to federal models and reshaping team topology. In parallel, a well-chosen AI toolkit now compresses every stage of the product life cycle, as long as data security and quality are protected.","sections":[{"h":"Governance is about decision rights","blocks":[{"p":"Google&#x27;s Project Aristotle showed that <strong>how a team works together</strong>, especially psychological safety, matters more than who is on it. Governance is <strong>decision rights and accountability</strong>: a RACI chart names who is responsible, but you cannot hold someone accountable without empowering them."},{"p":"<strong>Agency theory</strong>: owners (principals) and managers (agents) have different motives; a board wants long-term health while a CEO with a 2 to 5 year tenure may chase quarterly results. Incentives must align agents&#x27; behaviour with what principals want, in product governance as in boards."},{"p":"Governance models must evolve from start-up to scale-up to enterprise as complexity grows, while core values (such as Tata&#x27;s) carry forward."},{"img":"assets/figures/c22-1.png","title":"WHO DECIDES? SIX GOVERNANCE ARCHETYPES","alt":"1Business monarchy 2IT monarchy 3Feudal CEO or CXOs set the agenda Technology leaders decide Each business unit decides alone (silos) Federal Duopoly 456Anarchy Centre plus units: shared guardrails, IT and one business group decide Each team or user decides for itself local agency jointly"}]},{"h":"AI is moving firms from feudal to federal","blocks":[{"p":"Participants at a big-tech firm and a large bank described a shift from <strong>feudal</strong> (each product deciding alone) to <strong>federal</strong>: a central council reviews data security, privacy and responsible-AI principles before teams act, then hands agency back. Some swing back and forth between central and federated analytics."},{"p":"Humans still want &#x27;a neck to catch&#x27;, so accountability stays human. AI is changing team topology: <strong>specifications matter more</strong> than coding, one product owner may pair with just two developers, testing needs fall, and analysts must imagine and prototype, not just transcribe requirements. Think cars, not faster horses."}]},{"h":"Operating models, products and platforms","blocks":[{"p":"Strategy, metrics and talent can be right yet the <strong>operating model</strong> stalls when decision rights and boundaries are fuzzy. Stay <strong>ambidextrous</strong>: defend today&#x27;s revenue, explore the next two years (AI-enabled legacy and mainframe modernisation), and research the next decade (drug discovery). Move from project to <strong>product mode</strong> by rewriting OKRs as business outcomes, e.g. from &#x27;10% higher QPS&#x27; to &#x27;10% of sign-ups from mobile&#x27;. Build <strong>platform teams</strong> small, self-serve and voluntary, and measure value (conversion, revenue, stickiness), not story points. Good governance cuts wasteful work."},{"img":"assets/figures/c22-2.png","title":"IDEAS THAT CARRY INTO AN AI WORLD","alt":"Decision rights Agency theory Integration 1234Ambidexterity No accountability without Align incentives of agents Business and tech share Sustain, explore and empowerment with principals one North Star research at once"}]},{"h":"The AI toolkit for product leaders","blocks":[{"p":"AI compresses PM cycle time, but ship only as fast as users can absorb change (WhatsApp vs weekly game maps) and never at the cost of quality. Needs differ by role: documentation for junior PMs, prioritisation for group PMs, vision and market research for leaders."},{"p":"<strong>Perplexity</strong> scrapes the live web with citations; <strong>Gemini</strong> integrates with Google Workspace and excels at images and long context; <strong>Claude</strong> leads on code and prototypes; <strong>NotebookLM</strong> grounds answers in your own sources. Use themes (quick wins, high impact, strategic bets) and <strong>RICE</strong> (reach, impact, confidence, effort) to prioritise."},{"p":"Package repeatable work as <strong>skills</strong> (e.g. classifying 4,834 stock discrepancies) inside agents. Pick a daily driver, research, visual and vibe-coding tool, and keep confidential data on licensed enterprise tools only."},{"img":"assets/figures/c22-3.png","title":"AI TOOLS ACROSS THE PRODUCT LIFE CYCLE","alt":"Discover and Define and Document and Launch and GTM Measure and learn research prioritise prototype Perplexity, Google Miro boards, RICE in Claude for PRDs and Infographics, guides, NotebookLM, Atlassian Trends, NotebookLM, Sheets with Gemini prototypes, Replit campaigns Rovo summaries Copilot"}]}],"takeaways":["Governance means clear decision rights: you cannot make someone accountable without empowering them.","Align incentives so that agents' behaviour serves what principals actually want.","AI governance is pushing many firms from feudal silos to federal models with central guardrails.","With AI, specifications and domain expertise matter more than raw coding capacity.","Stay ambidextrous: sustain today's business, explore the next horizon and research the long term.","Shift OKRs from technical outputs to business outcomes to move from projects to products.","Match each AI tool to its strength: live research, workspace integration, code, or grounded sources.","Use free AI tools only for non-confidential brainstorming; protect enterprise data."],"quote":"So long as humans are in charge of running enterprises, we will all want a neck to catch.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-22.mp4","videoSecs":466,"notes":"media/notes/class-22-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"What did Google's Project Aristotle find matters most in teams?","a":"How the team works together, especially psychological safety","w":["Who is on the team","Team size","Individual IQ"],"x":"Governance and teamwork are about behaviours, not just structure."},{"q":"'You cannot hold someone accountable without...'","a":"Empowering them","w":["Paying them more","A RACI chart alone","Monitoring them daily"],"x":"Governance = decision rights + accountability."},{"q":"Agency theory describes a conflict between...","a":"Owners (principals) and managers (agents)","w":["IT and business","Customers and suppliers","Two competing firms"],"x":"Incentives must align agents' behaviour with what principals want."},{"q":"AI governance is pushing many firms from which model to which?","a":"Feudal to federal","w":["Federal to anarchy","Monarchy to feudal","Duopoly to anarchy"],"x":"A central council sets guardrails, then hands agency back to teams."},{"q":"Which OKR reflects a business outcome rather than a technical output?","a":"10% of sign-ups from mobile","w":["10% higher QPS","Ship 40 story points per sprint","Migrate 3 servers"],"x":"Moving from project to product mode means rewriting OKRs as outcomes."},{"q":"Which tool is described as grounding answers in your own sources?","a":"NotebookLM","w":["Perplexity","Miro","Replit"],"x":"Perplexity scrapes the live web; Gemini integrates with Workspace; Claude leads on code and prototypes."}],"cards":[{"f":"Six governance archetypes","b":"Business monarchy, IT monarchy, feudal, federal, duopoly, anarchy."},{"f":"RICE","b":"Reach, impact, confidence, effort: a prioritisation score."},{"f":"Ambidexterity","b":"Sustain today, explore the next horizon, research the long term."},{"f":"'A neck to catch'","b":"Humans want accountability to stay human."},{"f":"AI and team topology","b":"Specifications matter more than coding; analysts must imagine and prototype."},{"f":"Confidential data rule","b":"Use free AI tools only for non-confidential work; keep enterprise data on licensed tools."},{"f":"Platform teams","b":"Small, self-serve and voluntary; measured on value, not story points."}]},{"id":"c23","num":23,"module":"responsible-ai","title":"The Role of the Modern CDAIO","subtitle":"AI Principles, Risks, AI Strategy and Why Organisations Need a Chief AI Officer","date":"6 September 2026","faculty":"Faculty: Programme Co-Director (strategy and governance)","workshop":false,"hook":"As AI becomes a general-purpose capability with real risks, organisations need a strategy that ties AI to business goals and a senior leader who owns it. The Chief (Digital and) AI Officer bridges technology and business value, governs risk and responsible AI, and moves AI from pilots to measurable outcomes.","sections":[{"h":"Recap: digital transformation and AI","blocks":[{"p":"Digital transformation is not technology dumped into a firm but technology used to do business more effectively: re-engineering core processes, cutting maker-checker layers and making <strong>evidence-based</strong> <strong>decisions</strong>. IRCTC shows AI predicting the whole travel journey (bus if no train, food, hotels, sights). Participants asked for help <strong>operationalising</strong> theory; the faculty proposed a digital transformation case study. Some participants have already replaced rule-based financial-crime systems with AI and built POCs; non-coders shared that tools like Claude Code lower the barrier."},{"p":"AI systems work with <strong>varying autonomy</strong>. An HR agent can process leave, but empathy and bias remain concerns; the right level of automation depends on the monetary and reputational impact of a wrong decision."}]},{"h":"Responsible AI principles and the AI life cycle","blocks":[{"p":"The <strong>AI system life cycle</strong> runs from planning, design, data collection, model building and validation to deployment, operation and monitoring, and finally retirement, with risks documented, quantified and controlled at each step."},{"p":"The <strong>OECD principles</strong> were the first international standards agreed by governments. Risks should not stop adoption but must be managed; and you remain <strong>accountable</strong> for an AI system whether you built it, outsourced it or bought it (the Delta-CrowdStrike outage sparked a debate on who is liable)."},{"img":"assets/figures/c23-1.png","title":"OECD AI PRINCIPLES (MAY 2019)","alt":"1Benefit people and planet 2Human-centred values 3Transparency Inclusive growth, sustainable Rule of law, human rights, democracy, Explainable, with responsible development, well-being fairness disclosure 4Robust and safe 5Accountability 6Across the life cycle Risks continuously assessed and You own it, whether built, outsourced Plan, build, validate, deploy, managed or bought monitor, retire"}]},{"h":"The landscape and the need for an AI strategy","blocks":[{"p":"Stanford&#x27;s <strong>AI Index</strong>: the US hosts most AI data centres, with chips from one Taiwanese foundry; models win maths olympiad gold yet cannot reliably tell time (the <strong>jagged frontier</strong>); responsible AI lags capability; adoption is spreading at historic speed. India is a sweet spot, with hyperscaler data centres in Visakhapatnam and Hyderabad."},{"p":"An <strong>AI strategy</strong> is a plan to integrate AI so it supports business (or government) goals: deeper insights from data (a data strategy comes first), efficiency, better supply chains, talent and customer experience. Bottom-up ideas rarely scale without top-leadership backing, and boards increasingly ask for a business case."},{"p":"BCG: only 22% of companies have moved beyond pilots and just 4% realise value; McKinsey: 63% rank gen AI a high priority but 91% feel unprepared to do it responsibly."},{"img":"assets/figures/c23-2.png","title":"AI RISKS A LEADER MUST MANAGE","alt":"Malicious use Bias and discrimination 123Transparency failures Deepfakes, disinformation, election Poor data denying jobs or livelihoods Personal data used without consent interference 4Systemic risk 5Loss of control 6Security Chip concentration, geopolitics, Agents acting with real-world impact Cyber attacks on critical regulation infrastructure"}]},{"h":"Why a Chief (Digital and) AI Officer","blocks":[{"p":"Existing roles do not fit: CTOs know legacy and cloud but not GPUs and models; the DPO and CISO have legally defined mandates (a CISO should report to the CEO, board or CRO, not to the CTO or CIO). The <strong>CAIO bridges AI technology and business value</strong>, as at Microsoft, Dell, eBay, Boeing and Cleveland Clinic."},{"p":"A <strong>US presidential memorandum</strong> requires every federal agency to name a CAIO within 60 days (an existing CIO, CDO or CTO may be designated if they have AI expertise). The US Department of Defense CDAO takes data and AI &#x27;from the boardroom to the battlefield&#x27;. India may follow via an AI Act after DPDP."},{"p":"Delivering AI without ready data, talent and governance yields &#x27;stovepipe&#x27; solutions, so the role spans strategy, governance, risk, talent and change, and may fold back into other roles once AI is fully mature."},{"img":"assets/figures/c23-3.png","title":"THE CAIO'S MANDATE","alt":"AI strategy and roadmap Measurable business value Governance council and policies Chief AI Officer Talent, culture and change Risk and responsible AI Use-case prioritisation"}]}],"takeaways":["Digital transformation means using technology to do business better, not just installing it.","Match AI autonomy to the monetary and reputational impact of a wrong decision.","Follow the OECD principles: benefit people and planet, respect rights, be transparent, robust and accountable.","You are accountable for any AI system you deploy, whether built, outsourced or bought.","A data strategy must come before an AI strategy that ties AI to business goals.","Most firms are stuck in pilots; value comes from strategy, governance and measurement.","The CAIO bridges AI technology and business value, owning governance, risk, talent and change.","Governments, led by the US, are mandating Chief AI Officers; others are likely to follow."],"quote":"If we only deliver AI and the data, talent and governance are not ready, we will keep delivering stovepipe solutions.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-23.mp4","videoSecs":504,"notes":"media/notes/class-23-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"What were the first international AI standards agreed by governments?","a":"The OECD AI principles (May 2019)","w":["The EU AI Act","GDPR","The NIST AI RMF"],"x":"Benefit people and planet, human-centred values, transparency, robustness and accountability."},{"q":"If you buy or outsource an AI system, who is accountable for it?","a":"You remain accountable","w":["Only the vendor","Nobody","The regulator"],"x":"Whether built, outsourced or bought."},{"q":"What should come before an AI strategy?","a":"A data strategy","w":["A vendor contract","A marketing campaign","A hiring freeze"],"x":"AI strategy ties AI to business goals, starting from data."},{"q":"According to BCG, what share of companies realise value from AI?","a":"About 4%","w":["About 50%","About 22%","About 91%"],"x":"Only 22% moved beyond pilots and just 4% realise value."},{"q":"Why is a CTO not automatically suited to be the Chief AI Officer?","a":"CTOs know legacy and cloud but not necessarily GPUs and models, and the role must bridge AI and business value","w":["CTOs are not senior enough","Regulators forbid it","CTOs cannot manage budgets"],"x":"The CAIO spans strategy, governance, risk, talent and change."},{"q":"What decides the right level of automation for an AI decision?","a":"The monetary and reputational impact of a wrong decision","w":["The size of the model","The vendor's recommendation","The number of users"],"x":"An HR agent can process leave, but empathy and bias remain concerns."}],"cards":[{"f":"AI system life cycle","b":"Plan, design, collect data, build, validate, deploy, operate and monitor, retire."},{"f":"Jagged frontier","b":"AI can win maths olympiad gold yet fail to tell time reliably."},{"f":"Stovepipe solutions","b":"What you get when AI is delivered without data, talent and governance ready."},{"f":"US CAIO mandate","b":"Every federal agency must name a Chief AI Officer within 60 days."},{"f":"Six AI risks","b":"Malicious use, bias, transparency failures, systemic risk, loss of control, security."},{"f":"Where a CISO should report","b":"To the CEO, board or CRO, not to the CTO or CIO."},{"f":"CAIO mandate","b":"Strategy and roadmap, governance, risk and responsible AI, use-case prioritisation, talent and change, measurable value."}]},{"id":"c24","num":24,"module":"responsible-ai","title":"AI Bias and Responsible AI","subtitle":"Sources of Bias across the AI Life Cycle, the OECD Classification Framework, Accountability and the Programme Assessment Plan","date":"13 September 2026","faculty":"Faculty: Programme Co-Director; assessment briefing by programme faculty","workshop":false,"hook":"AI learns from data that people create, so human, data and algorithmic biases become AI biases unless they are consciously found and reduced. A CDAIO must ask what features a model uses, where bias can enter across the life cycle, and whether each system can stand scrutiny on rights, fairness, transparency and accountability.","sections":[{"h":"What AI bias is and why it matters","blocks":[{"p":"An AI system learns from its training data; if the data is <strong>skewed or incomplete</strong> (more men than women, one geography), its predictions and recommendations tilt the same way. Participants added that reward design and human feedback can also inject bias."},{"p":"Unaddressed bias excludes people, harms decisions in <strong>hiring, policing, credit and healthcare</strong>, reduces business effectiveness and undermines trust, violating the first OECD principle of benefiting people and planet."},{"p":"Responsible AI principles (OECD, NIST, IBM): accurate and reliable, accountable, transparent, fair and human-centric, safe and ethical, secure and resilient, interpretable and documented, privacy-enhanced. Auditors will ask for the policies, controls and risk register behind them."},{"img":"assets/figures/c24-1.png","title":"WHERE BIAS CAN ENTER THE AI SYSTEM LIFE CYCLE","alt":"Plan and design Collect and Build and Verify and Deploy, operate, process data interpret model validate monitor Problem framing, Skewed, incomplete, Feature selection, Bias testing, Drift, hallucination, context, assumptions historical data rewards challenger models feedback loops"}]},{"h":"Lessons from the field","blocks":[{"p":"A bank replacing rule-based anti-money-laundering alerts with AI kept a <strong>human reviewing every one of 10,000 alerts</strong>, cleaned data massively and retrained three times; HSBC&#x27;s Google-built model took four years. Some firms run a <strong>challenger</strong> <strong>model</strong> from a rival vendor to monitor the production model and retire it if it drifts."},{"p":"Data retention and the <strong>right to be forgotten</strong> are hard: backups and trained models may still hold data. Consent is usually prospective, not retrospective, and retention, backup and archival policies must follow the law (GDPR, DPDP)."}]},{"h":"How bias enters: the nursing example","blocks":[{"p":"Asked to shortlist nurses from ten years of Indian hiring data, an AI learns that &#x27;a successful nurse is a woman&#x27;, because nursing is a highly feminised profession (WHO 2025). Human bias in past decisions becomes training data, and <strong>human</strong> <strong>assumptions become AI assumptions</strong> unless consciously addressed."},{"p":"Fixes depend on <strong>context</strong>: remove gender as a feature for hiring, but keep it if you genuinely target products by gender. A CDAIO must ask which features a model uses. Public image data showed male nurses in different roles, and gen AI tools fill gaps with assumptions, so give full context and let them ask questions."},{"p":"Datasets over-represent some groups (Indians share data freely, Europeans less so) and clinical trials under-represent others, so outputs can be skewed for whole populations."},{"img":"assets/figures/c24-2.png","title":"COMMON TYPES OF AI BIAS","alt":"1Algorithmic 2Confirmation 3Prejudice Poorly defined problem or weak Reinforces existing assumptions, Stereotypes in training data: doctors feedback loops misses new patterns male, nurses female Exclusion Measurement and sampling 456Stereotyping Key variables or groups left out of Data unrepresentative of the real Gender, racial or cultural the data population stereotypes amplified"}]},{"h":"Classify, govern and stay accountable","blocks":[{"p":"Use the <strong>OECD classification framework</strong> to surface risks such as bias, explainability and robustness in a technology-neutral way. Keep an <strong>inventory and versioning</strong> of algorithms, agents and datasets so you know which data trained what is in production."},{"p":"Before production, ask: can users <strong>opt out, challenge or correct</strong> an output? Could it affect rights, dignity, privacy or non-discrimination? In defence, every AI action must be auditable back to an accountable human. Self-governance matters too (do not upload personal photos to free tools). <strong>Assessment</strong>: 70% attendance; quizzes 30%, a 15-minute online exam 20%, and a group <strong>capstone</strong> 50% (proposal, review, final submission and a 15 to 20 minute presentation at campus immersion). Optional individual case studies on AI dilemmas may be published."},{"img":"assets/figures/c24-3.png","title":"OECD FRAMEWORK FOR CLASSIFYING AN AI SYSTEM","alt":"People and planet Task and output Economic context AI system AI model Data and input"}]}],"takeaways":["AI inherits bias from data, rewards and the people who build and label it.","Human assumptions become AI assumptions unless they are consciously identified and addressed.","Feature selection is a leadership question: decide by context which attributes a model should see.","Keep humans in the loop for high-stakes decisions and monitor models for drift in production.","Version datasets and models so you can trace what trained the system in production.","Let people opt out, challenge or correct AI outputs that affect them.","Every AI action must be traceable to an accountable human.","Bias cannot be eliminated completely, but diverse teams and testing can minimise it."],"quote":"Human assumptions become AI assumptions unless they are consciously identified and addressed.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-24.mp4","videoSecs":467,"notes":"media/notes/class-24-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"Why does an AI shortlisting nurses from 10 years of Indian hiring data learn 'a successful nurse is a woman'?","a":"Past human hiring patterns become training data","w":["The algorithm was coded to prefer women","Nurses' CVs were missing","Gender is always a useful feature"],"x":"Human assumptions become AI assumptions unless consciously addressed."},{"q":"Should gender always be removed as a model feature?","a":"It depends on context: remove it for hiring, keep it if you genuinely target products by gender","w":["Yes, always","No, never","Only in Europe"],"x":"Feature selection is a leadership question."},{"q":"Bias that reinforces existing assumptions and misses new patterns is called...","a":"Confirmation bias","w":["Exclusion bias","Measurement bias","Algorithmic bias"],"x":"Exclusion leaves out key variables or groups; measurement bias comes from unrepresentative data."},{"q":"Why do some firms run a challenger model from a rival vendor?","a":"To monitor the production model and retire it if it drifts","w":["To double the throughput","To save licence costs","To train on more data"],"x":"Monitoring in production is part of responsible AI."},{"q":"What should you be able to trace for any AI system in production?","a":"Which versioned data trained it, and an accountable human for every action","w":["Only the vendor's name","The GPU used","The developer's salary"],"x":"Keep an inventory and versioning of algorithms, agents and datasets."},{"q":"Which question should be asked before an AI system goes to production?","a":"Can users opt out, challenge or correct an output?","w":["Is the model the largest available?","Did the demo look good?","Is it cheaper than last year?"],"x":"Also ask whether it could affect rights, dignity, privacy or non-discrimination."}],"cards":[{"f":"Six common AI biases","b":"Algorithmic, confirmation, prejudice, exclusion, measurement/sampling, stereotyping."},{"f":"Where bias enters","b":"Plan and design, data, model building, validation, and deployment (drift and feedback loops)."},{"f":"OECD classification framework","b":"People and planet, economic context, data and input, AI model, task and output."},{"f":"Right to be forgotten challenge","b":"Backups and trained models may still hold the data."},{"f":"Consent timing","b":"Consent is usually prospective, not retrospective."},{"f":"Can bias be eliminated?","b":"Not completely, but diverse teams and testing can minimise it."},{"f":"Programme assessment","b":"Quizzes 30%, online exam 20%, group capstone 50%; 70% attendance."}]},{"id":"c25","num":25,"module":"responsible-ai","title":"Classifying AI Systems, Explainability and Transparency","subtitle":"OECD Dimensions, High-Stakes Decisions, Human in the Loop and Opening the AI Black Box","date":"20 September 2026","faculty":"Faculty: Programme Co-Director (strategy and governance)","workshop":false,"hook":"Classifying an AI system along people, context, data, model and task dimensions turns 'responsible AI' from a slogan into concrete design questions. As AI moves into lending, hiring, healthcare and defence, leaders must also be able to explain what a system did and why, keep humans accountable, and monitor it long after launch.","sections":[{"h":"Classifying an AI system broadens the lens","blocks":[{"p":"The OECD dimensions push teams beyond technical performance: is the data collected by humans, sensors or agents, is it personal (then privacy law applies: mask PII, encrypt, anonymise), static or real-time, expert-provided or synthetic? Which function uses it, and who are the users and how AI-literate are they?"},{"p":"Participants said this gives &#x27;responsible AI&#x27; real content. It extends <strong>design thinking</strong> to people and planet, and adds a <strong>time dimension</strong>: a forecast of cheap spare parts today must hold for components needed in 2028."},{"p":"Leadership often rushes to market; rushing without a strong product is a recipe for long-term failure. <strong>Sustainability</strong> now matters too: data-centre heat and emissions are becoming a deal-blocker for carbon-reporting enterprises."},{"img":"assets/figures/c25-1.png","title":"QUESTIONS THE OECD CLASSIFICATION DIMENSIONS RAISE","alt":"1People and planet 2Economic context 3Data and input Rights, jobs, health, environment; Sector, business function, Human, sensor or agent? Personal? who are the users? criticality, scale, maturity Masked, encrypted? AI model Task and output 456Across the life cycle How it is built, updated and Level of autonomy and impact of a Each stage has accountable actors; explained wrong output review regularly"}]},{"h":"High-stakes decisions and human judgment","blocks":[{"p":"In defence and refineries, businesses now demand a <strong>confidence level and full explanation</strong> with every recommendation. Some labs assess job impact and consult policymakers before release. AI can beat humans at ID checks and avoid unconscious bias, but keyword CV filters can reject a 17-year veteran without a certificate."},{"p":"Use <strong>maker-checker-approver</strong> logic: when a decision affects millions or risks life (wrong dosage, a mis-targeted strike), a human with sovereign responsibility must exercise judgment. Experiment where impact is low; open models also enable misuse, so suppliers should verify purpose."}]},{"h":"Monitoring, accountability and the black box","blocks":[{"p":"AI is dynamic: classification and risk assessments must be <strong>reviewed regularly</strong>, models monitored in production and retired if they drift. Linking each dimension to life-cycle stages fixes who is accountable at each step, and dimensions interact (using data beyond its purpose raises risk)."},{"p":"Many systems are <strong>black boxes</strong>: even providers cannot explain outputs, and lab results differ from production, where others deploy the model in unseen ways. Vendors may hide behind &#x27;beta&#x27; labels, making customers the test subjects; &#x27;it came free with the licence&#x27; is not a reason to deploy."},{"p":"Pilots need real data: use staged rollouts (start with 1% of users), pilots in regulated sectors under governing rules, and caution, because one mistake in banking, healthcare or defence can erase your foundations."},{"img":"assets/figures/c25-2.png","title":"TRANSPARENCY VS EXPLAINABILITY","alt":"Transparency: what happened? Explainability: why? Is the process visible and disclosed? What is the reason and justification? What data sources were used? Which factors contributed? vs What governance applies? Counterfactual: what would change the outcome? Users know output is AI-generated Enables appeal, challenge and recourse"}]},{"h":"Explainable AI (XAI) builds trust","blocks":[{"p":"Leaders will be asked why a customer was rejected, a claim denied or a chatbot answered as it did. <strong>Explainable</strong> <strong>AI</strong> gives clear reasons for decisions; <strong>interpretability</strong> lets humans predict what a model will do and spot its mistakes."},{"p":"Explainability enables trust, fairness, accountability, auditability, human oversight and compliance, and lets people <strong>appeal and seek recourse</strong>. Accountability requires traceability, audit trails, decision documentation and named owners. The IRCTC concession flow shows rules and evidence embedded in a largely automated process. Future: as systems grow more capable they may push humans out of the loop; for now humans are the legal guardrails, and the goal is <strong>human judgment plus data</strong> deciding together."},{"img":"assets/figures/c25-3.png","title":"THE TRUST FORMULA FOR AI","alt":"Accurate Fair Explainable Human oversight Trust Reliable predictions No unjust bias Reasons people can A person in or on the Adoption without follow loop resistance"}]}],"takeaways":["Classify every AI system by people and planet, context, data, model and task before you build it.","Personal data triggers privacy duties: mask, encrypt and anonymise.","Do not rush to market with a weak product; it is recipe for long-term failure.","a High-stakes decisions need confidence levels, explanations and an accountable human.","Monitor AI in production, review risk regularly and retire models that drift.","Beware black boxes and 'free' beta features that turn you into the test subject.","Transparency says what happened; explainability says why, and enables recourse.","Trust comes from accuracy, fairness, explainability and human oversight together."],"quote":"A system may be highly accurate, but if you cannot explain its logic, trust is lost and users resist.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-25.mp4","videoSecs":470,"notes":"media/notes/class-25-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"Transparency answers 'what happened?'. Explainability answers...","a":"Why?","w":["Who paid?","When?","Where is the data stored?"],"x":"Explainability gives reasons, contributing factors and counterfactuals, enabling recourse."},{"q":"If data used by an AI system is personal, what follows?","a":"Privacy law applies: mask PII, encrypt and anonymise","w":["Nothing changes","It cannot be used at all","Only the vendor is responsible"],"x":"The OECD 'data and input' dimension raises this question."},{"q":"What is a sensible way to pilot AI in a high-risk setting?","a":"Staged rollout with real data, starting with ~1% of users","w":["Launch to everyone at once","Use only synthetic vendor data","Skip pilots if the vendor is reputable"],"x":"One mistake in banking, healthcare or defence can erase your foundations."},{"q":"'It came free with the licence' is...","a":"Not a valid reason to deploy an AI feature","w":["A good reason to deploy quickly","A regulatory requirement","A sign of a mature product"],"x":"Beta labels can make customers the test subjects."},{"q":"Which combination builds trust in AI, per the trust formula?","a":"Accuracy, fairness, explainability and human oversight","w":["Speed, cost and scale","Branding and marketing","Model size and GPU count"],"x":"'A system may be highly accurate, but if you cannot explain its logic, trust is lost.'"},{"q":"When a decision affects millions or risks life, the class recommends...","a":"Maker-checker-approver logic with a human holding sovereign responsibility","w":["Full automation for speed","Letting the vendor decide","Random sampling of decisions"],"x":"Businesses now demand a confidence level and full explanation with every recommendation."}],"cards":[{"f":"Interpretability","b":"Lets humans predict what a model will do and spot its mistakes."},{"f":"Counterfactual explanation","b":"What would need to change for a different outcome."},{"f":"Accountability needs...","b":"Traceability, audit trails, decision documentation and named owners."},{"f":"Black box problem","b":"Even providers can't always explain outputs; lab results differ from production."},{"f":"Time dimension","b":"A forecast must hold for future needs (e.g. parts needed in 2028)."},{"f":"Sustainability concern","b":"Data-centre heat and emissions are becoming a deal-blocker."},{"f":"Rushing to market","b":"Rushing without a strong product is a recipe for long-term failure."}]},{"id":"c26","num":26,"module":"privacy","title":"AI Sovereignty and Data Privacy","subtitle":"Data Residency, Regulatory and Economic Sovereignty, Security vs Privacy and GDPR Foundations","date":"27 September 2026","faculty":"Faculty: Programme Co-Director (strategy and governance)","workshop":false,"hook":"AI sovereignty means keeping control over the data, infrastructure, models and rules that an AI system depends on, in line with local laws and priorities. Because data is the fuel of AI, a strong data privacy regime is the foundation of any AI governance regime, and GDPR remains the reference law every CDAIO should understand.","sections":[{"h":"What AI sovereignty means","blocks":[{"p":"AI sovereignty is <strong>independent control over AI systems, data, infrastructure and governance</strong>, aligned with local priorities. It covers where data is stored, where GPUs and cloud providers come from, and whether vendors obey the law of the land."},{"p":"Models must be <strong>auditable</strong> to avoid vendor lock-in and keep accountability; some vendors still resist disclosing algorithms. The faculty stressed it applies to legacy systems and to coming waves such as quantum readiness, not just to new AI."},{"p":"There is no hard law yet; it is still a <strong>boardroom discussion</strong>. Leaders should understand their risk landscape and risk appetite, and decide what is feasible now versus over years."},{"img":"assets/figures/c26-1.png","title":"THE DIMENSIONS OF AI SOVEREIGNTY","alt":"1Data sovereignty 2Infrastructure 3Regulatory Residency, jurisdiction and who Where GPUs, cloud and data centres Local laws, regulators and auditable, controls access and deletion sit; avoid vendor lock-in explainable models 4Resilience and security 5Economic 6Culture and language Critical infrastructure must run Keep AI value in the local economy Models that represent local languages independently and resist cyber through startups and local talent and values threats"}]},{"h":"Data, regulatory and economic sovereignty in practice","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>Data sovereignty</strong> reduces exposure to foreign surveillance and conflicting laws. A vendor&#x27;s home country prevails when rules clash, so even Google and Meta are pulled up by the EU, and India now tells platforms to comply or else."},{"p":"Control matters more than location: <strong>data regions</strong> can span the EU while rights to delete data or block users stay with one country. A participant keeps US, EU and Indian customer data in separate cloud regions, but must check where the <strong>encryption keys</strong> sit."},{"p":"Contract clauses are one thing, enforcement another. Big vendors now offer <strong>sovereign or private</strong> <strong>clouds</strong> for defence, energy and banks. Critical infrastructure in India falls under the NCIIPC. <strong>Economic</strong> <strong>sovereignty</strong> keeps AI value local through startups and local talent."}]},{"h":"Why banks care: data sovereignty in financial services","blocks":[{"p":"Banks use sovereign AI for <strong>fraud detection, risk scoring, customer engagement</strong> and decisions, keeping sensitive information inside national or enterprise-controlled environments."},{"p":"Recent outsourcing and GRC frameworks put KYC, AML and strategic, operational and systemic risk under the IT strategy, risk and audit committees of the board. Cross-border fraud needs countries to cooperate on evidence."}]},{"h":"Security protects data, privacy governs its use","blocks":[{"p":"Security is about authentication, encryption, masking, backups and RTO/RPO recovery. Privacy asks <strong>why</strong> personal data is collected, how it is processed and kept, and for how long. Privacy is a fundamental right in many countries. Where a country has weak or no privacy law, <strong>apply the most restrictive policy</strong>, not the least. Privacy law applies to everyone who collects personal data, from NGOs and public sector bodies to private firms."},{"p":"A <strong>good data privacy regime facilitates an AI regime</strong>; platforms like Databricks and Snowflake embed AI across the data life cycle. Rogue agents and unknown unknowns make compliance harder, but the framework stays the same."},{"img":"assets/figures/c26-2.png","title":"DATA SECURITY VS DATA PRIVACY","alt":"Security: protect the data Privacy: govern its use Confidentiality, integrity, availability Why is personal data collected? Authentication and access control Consent and purpose limitation vs Encryption, masking, backups Retention and deletion rules Defends against hackers and leaks Rights of the data subject"}]},{"h":"GDPR: the foundational privacy law","blocks":[{"p":"GDPR governs how organisations <strong>inside and outside the EU</strong> handle EU residents&#x27; personal data, including any firm that offers services to or monitors EU residents. Data protection is about building trust in a digital era."},{"p":"<strong>Personal data</strong> is anything that identifies a living person directly or indirectly: names, aliases, IDs, location, IP address, device ID, genetic and biometric data, vehicle numbers. Processing covers collecting, storing, using, sharing and erasing it."},{"p":"Breach notification and record keeping are mandatory. Participants said knowing GDPR helped them <strong>sequence</strong> <strong>launches, gain first-mover advantage and choose compliant vendors</strong>. India&#x27;s opt-out consent culture shows why consent checkboxes must be clearer."},{"img":"assets/figures/c26-3.png","title":"APPLYING GDPR IN PRACTICE","alt":"Scope Identify Consent Process Notify Serve or monitor EU Map all personal and Lawful basis, clear Controllers and Breach notification residents? It applies identifiable data purpose processors bound by and record keeping contract"}]}],"takeaways":["AI sovereignty means control over data, infrastructure, models and governance, aligned with local law and priorities.","Control matters more than location: know who can access, delete and block, and where your encryption keys sit.","A vendor's home country prevails when laws clash, so assess dependency on foreign cloud, GPU and model providers.","Written contract clauses are not enough; demand audit trails and proof of enforcement.","Data security protects the data; data privacy governs how it is used.","Where local law is weak, adopt the most restrictive policy as your own standard.","A strong data privacy regime is the foundation of an effective AI governance regime.","GDPR reaches any organisation serving or monitoring EU residents, and defines personal data very broadly."],"quote":"Data security protects the data; data privacy governs its use.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-26.mp4","videoSecs":550,"notes":"media/notes/class-26-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"What does AI sovereignty mean?","a":"Independent control over AI data, infrastructure, models and governance, aligned with local priorities","w":["Building every model in-house","Banning foreign cloud providers","Storing all data on paper"],"x":"It covers where data sits, where GPUs come from and whether vendors obey local law."},{"q":"When laws clash, whose law tends to prevail for a vendor?","a":"The vendor's home country","w":["The customer's country","The UN's","Whichever is cheaper"],"x":"So assess dependency on foreign cloud, GPU and model providers."},{"q":"'Control matters more than location' refers to...","a":"Knowing who can access, delete and block data, and where encryption keys sit","w":["Picking the nearest data centre","Using one cloud region","Avoiding encryption"],"x":"Data regions can span the EU while control stays with another country."},{"q":"Data security vs data privacy: which is correct?","a":"Security protects the data; privacy governs its use","w":["Privacy protects the data; security governs its use","They are the same","Security is only about backups"],"x":"Privacy asks why data is collected, how it is processed and for how long."},{"q":"Where a country has weak or no privacy law, you should...","a":"Apply the most restrictive policy as your standard","w":["Apply the least restrictive policy","Collect as much data as possible","Wait for a law"],"x":"Privacy law applies to everyone collecting personal data, including NGOs and public bodies."},{"q":"Who does GDPR apply to?","a":"Any organisation that offers services to or monitors EU residents","w":["Only EU-headquartered firms","Only EU citizens' employers","Only tech firms"],"x":"It reaches organisations inside and outside the EU."}],"cards":[{"f":"Six dimensions of AI sovereignty","b":"Data, infrastructure, regulatory, resilience and security, economic, culture and language."},{"f":"NCIIPC","b":"India's body for critical information infrastructure protection."},{"f":"RTO / RPO","b":"Recovery time and recovery point objectives: security and resilience measures."},{"f":"Personal data (GDPR)","b":"Anything identifying a living person directly or indirectly: name, IP, device ID, biometrics, vehicle number."},{"f":"Processing (GDPR)","b":"Collecting, storing, using, sharing and erasing personal data."},{"f":"Contracts vs enforcement","b":"Written clauses are not enough; demand audit trails and proof."},{"f":"Privacy as foundation","b":"A strong data privacy regime is the foundation of an effective AI governance regime."}]},{"id":"c27","num":27,"module":"privacy","title":"GDPR in Practice","subtitle":"Controllers and Processors, Special Data, Consent and Data Subject Rights, Fines and Compliance Checklist","date":"4 October 2026","faculty":"Faculty: Programme Co-Director (strategy and governance)","workshop":false,"hook":"GDPR is the seminal privacy law that most countries, including India's DPDP Act, broadly follow. It builds trust by making organisations collect only the minimum data, for a clear purpose, with real consent, and by holding controllers and their processors accountable through rights, contracts, audits and heavy fines.","sections":[{"h":"GDPR: scope and purpose","blocks":[{"p":"Adopted in 2016 and in force from <strong>25 May 2018</strong>, GDPR governs how organisations inside and outside the EU handle the personal data of EU <strong>residents</strong> (not just citizens), including any firm that monitors their behaviour. The UK still follows it after Brexit."},{"p":"India&#x27;s <strong>DPDP Act 2023</strong>, with rules published in 2025 and implementation due by 2027, is aligned broadly but is not identical. Where no law exists, follow the most restrictive standard."},{"p":"Data protection is about <strong>trust</strong>: people share data for a purpose and expect it not to be sold or misused. Collect the <strong>minimum data</strong> needed. Schools asking parents&#x27; salaries or lucky draws collecting phone numbers fail this test, unless a clear purpose such as a subsidy justifies it."}]},{"h":"Controllers, processors and owners","blocks":[{"p":"The <strong>controller</strong> decides why and how data is collected and used, and remains accountable even for an employee&#x27;s mistake. The <strong>processor</strong>, such as an IT services firm working for a ministry, acts on the controller&#x27;s instructions and has direct but narrower legal duties."},{"p":"<strong>Processing</strong> covers almost everything: collecting, recording, structuring, storing, altering, retrieving, using, sharing, combining, erasing. Participants debated data owner versus controller; one compared sharing data to letting a house: the tenant uses it but never owns it."},{"img":"assets/figures/c27-1.png","title":"WHO DOES WHAT UNDER GDPR","alt":"Data subject Controller Contract Processor Authority The person the data Decides why and how Written terms bind Processes on the Supervises, is about; gives data is collected and the processor controller's investigates and consent used instructions fines"}]},{"h":"Retention, contracts and special categories","blocks":[{"p":"Retention follows the statute for each data type: tax and financial records may need 10 years against a 7-year privacy default. Banks often keep more, but must justify it. Revoking consent is <strong>prospective</strong>, and data is then kept only as the law requires."},{"p":"Put processor terms <strong>in writing</strong>. Does retention run from contract start or from collection? Processing of <strong>special categories</strong> (race, ethnicity, political or religious beliefs, union membership, genetic, biometric and health data, sex life) is prohibited without strict conditions."}]},{"h":"Rights, consent and fines","blocks":[{"p":"Consent must be <strong>clear, affirmative and granular</strong>; withdrawal must be easy; a child under 16 needs a parent&#x27;s consent. Notices must be in plain language at collection. Subject access requests must be answered <strong>within one</strong> <strong>month</strong>, and erasure requires knowing everywhere the data sits."},{"p":"Breaches are not criminal offences but bring <strong>administrative fines</strong> from the supervisory authority (the ICO in the UK). Authorities weigh cooperation, self-reporting, data type and past record. Multiple violations in one processing operation are fined once, for the most severe."},{"img":"assets/figures/c27-2.png","title":"TWO TIERS OF GDPR ADMINISTRATIVE FINES","alt":"Up to EUR 10m or 2% of turnover Up to EUR 20m or 4% of turnover Controller or processor obligations breached Data subject rights infringed Negligence in safeguards and records Basic processing principles broken vs Whichever amount is higher Whichever amount is higher"}]},{"h":"Making GDPR work in your organisation","blocks":[{"p":"Run a <strong>data flow audit</strong>: what is collected, where it is stored, why, who owns it and how long it is kept. Check international transfers to cloud regions and whether the destination country is safe."},{"p":"Keep audit trails, a data inventory and <strong>data protection impact assessments</strong>; de-identify archives; build privacy by design. Insert <strong>right-to-audit</strong> clauses in cloud contracts, including for your regulator. Personal data used to train AI models must follow the same rules."},{"img":"assets/figures/c27-3.png","title":"GDPR COMPLIANCE CHECKLIST","alt":"Data flow audit Right-to-audit clauses Clear privacy notice GDPR Audit trails and DPIA Granular consent and withdrawal readiness Safe international transfers Access requests within a month Erasure and traceability"}]}],"takeaways":["GDPR protects EU residents wherever the organisation is based, and most national laws, including India's DPDP Act, broadly follow it.","Collect the minimum data needed, for a clear purpose that the person understands.","The controller stays accountable; processors must be bound by detailed written contracts.","Retention depends on the statute for each data type; revoking consent applies from then on.","Special categories such as health, biometric and religious data need the strictest care.","Consent must be affirmative, granular and easy to withdraw; answer access requests within a month.","Fines reach EUR 20 million or 4% of global turnover, whichever is higher.","Audit data flows, keep trails and impact assessments, and secure a right to audit your cloud vendors."],"quote":"Get the minimum data required to process the request. Don't ask for more.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-27.mp4","videoSecs":530,"notes":"media/notes/class-27-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"When did GDPR come into force?","a":"25 May 2018","w":["1 January 2016","1 April 2020","2 December 2027"],"x":"Adopted in 2016, it protects EU residents, not just citizens."},{"q":"Who decides why and how personal data is collected and used?","a":"The controller","w":["The processor","The data subject","The supervisory authority"],"x":"The controller stays accountable, even for an employee's mistake."},{"q":"What is the maximum higher-tier GDPR fine?","a":"EUR 20 million or 4% of global turnover, whichever is higher","w":["EUR 10 million or 2%, whichever is lower","EUR 1 million flat","10% of profit"],"x":"Lower tier: EUR 10m or 2%. Rights infringements and broken principles hit the higher tier."},{"q":"How quickly must a subject access request be answered?","a":"Within one month","w":["Within 72 hours","Within one year","Whenever convenient"],"x":"Erasure requires knowing everywhere the data sits."},{"q":"A child under what age needs a parent's consent under GDPR?","a":"16","w":["13","18","21"],"x":"Consent must be clear, affirmative and granular, and easy to withdraw."},{"q":"Which is a 'special category' of personal data?","a":"Health and biometric data","w":["Email address","Job title","Company name"],"x":"Also race, ethnicity, political or religious beliefs, union membership, genetic data and sex life."}],"cards":[{"f":"Processor","b":"Acts on the controller's instructions, with direct but narrower legal duties; must be bound by written contract."},{"f":"Data minimisation","b":"Collect only the minimum data needed for a clear purpose."},{"f":"Revoking consent","b":"Applies from then on (prospective); data is then kept only as the law requires."},{"f":"Fines for multiple violations","b":"In one processing operation, fined once, for the most severe."},{"f":"DPIA","b":"Data protection impact assessment."},{"f":"Right-to-audit clause","b":"Lets you (and your regulator) audit your cloud vendors."},{"f":"Data flow audit","b":"What is collected, where stored, why, who owns it, how long it's kept."}]},{"id":"c28","num":28,"module":"privacy","title":"India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act","subtitle":"Scope, Consent and Legitimate Use, Fiduciaries and Consent Managers, Rights, Breaches and Penalties","date":"11 October 2026","faculty":"Faculty: Programme Co-Director (strategy and governance)","workshop":false,"hook":"India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023, with rules notified in 2025 and compliance due by 2027, follows the spirit of GDPR in an Indian setting. It is an enterprise-wide risk, not an IT project: it demands lawful purpose, free and specific consent, strong safeguards, fast breach reporting and a cultural shift in how organisations treat personal data.","sections":[{"h":"Why DPDP, and why now","blocks":[{"p":"The <strong>Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023</strong> (MeitY) has notified rules and a compliance window of a little over a year. GDPR was studied in depth because it is the mature model DPDP follows, and global firms face both."},{"p":"It is a <strong>civil, not criminal</strong>, regime, but it is an enterprise-wide risk with financial and reputational impact. The faculty warned that many organisations are &#x27;sitting on a time bomb&#x27; without the discipline or culture it needs."},{"p":"Cross-border transfers are allowed except to a <strong>negative list</strong> of countries the government will notify. Exemptions cover crime prevention and investigation, courts and tribunals, and certain state functions."}]},{"h":"What counts as digital personal data","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>Personal data</strong> is any data about an individual who is identifiable by or in relation to it: not just a name, but a car, mobile or card number, IP address, or two or three fields combined. Physical records being digitised also fall in scope."},{"p":"Participants debated product and service preferences: with a small enough dataset almost any field can pinpoint a person, which is why <strong>anonymisation and pseudonymisation</strong> matter."},{"p":"A <strong>personal data breach</strong> is any unauthorised processing or accidental disclosure that compromises confidentiality, integrity or availability."},{"img":"assets/figures/c28-1.png","title":"THE DPDP ECOSYSTEM","alt":"Data principal Consent manager Data fiduciary Data processor Data Protection Board The individual; Registered platform Decides purpose and Processes on the Hears complaints and parent for a child to give, review, means; appoints DPO fiduciary's behalf imposes penalties withdraw consent"}]},{"h":"Consent, legitimate use and purpose","blocks":[{"p":"Processing is lawful only with <strong>consent or a legitimate use</strong>. An HR team asking blood group for factory emergencies may be legitimate; asking income or number of children may not. You must be able to prove legitimacy."},{"p":"The Act does not ban collecting data; a bank needs identity data. It governs <strong>purpose, processing and</strong> <strong>controls</strong>. Participants raised years of unwanted loan calls despite DND, religion data for diversity, and costly platform changes to honour consent choices."}]},{"h":"Fiduciaries, consent managers and rights","blocks":[{"p":"The <strong>data fiduciary</strong> appoints a data protection officer and may use processors. Registered <strong>consent managers</strong> let people give, review and withdraw consent; data auditors check the life cycle. Firms choose product-level or universal consent; cookie consent needs a fair, unbiased banner."},{"p":"Data principals have rights to <strong>information, correction and erasure, grievance redressal and nomination</strong>, and duties too: no impersonation, no false or frivolous complaints. Children&#x27;s data needs verifiable parental consent, with no tracking or targeted ads."},{"img":"assets/figures/c28-2.png","title":"VALID CONSENT UNDER DPDP","alt":"1Free 2Specific 3Informed Not forced or bundled with unrelated Tied to a stated purpose, product by Clear notice in plain language services product 4Unambiguous 5Withdrawable 6Or legitimate use A clear affirmative action As easy to withdraw as to give e.g. emergencies, legal duties, state benefits"}]},{"h":"Breaches, grievances and penalties","blocks":[{"p":"Report a breach to the Board within <strong>72 hours</strong> (or an approved extension), with cause, mitigation, who caused it, prevention steps and proof that affected people were told. Reasonable security safeguards must cover data at rest and in transit, networks, apps and people."},{"p":"Grievances go first to the fiduciary, then the <strong>Data Protection Board</strong>, then TDSAT and finally the Supreme Court. Penalties reach <strong>Rs 250 crore</strong> per breach and the Board weighs voluntary disclosure. Many boards still have not appointed a DPO."},{"img":"assets/figures/c28-3.png","title":"PENALTIES UNDER THE DPDP ACT","alt":"Data fiduciaries Data principals Security safeguards failure: up to Rs 250 crore Duty breaches: up to Rs 10,000 Breach not notified: up to Rs 200 crore No impersonation vs Children's data duties: up to Rs 200 crore No false or frivolous complaints Significant fiduciary duties: up to Rs 150 crore Give authentic information Other breaches: up to Rs 50 crore"}]}],"takeaways":["DPDP compliance is due within about a year; treat as an enterprise risk and a cultural change, not an task.","it Personal data is anything that can identify a person, IT alone or combined with other data.","Process only for a lawful purpose, with free, specific, informed consent or a provable legitimate use.","Collect what the business genuinely needs and be ready to justify every field.","Appoint a DPO, consider consent managers and data auditors, and design for withdrawal of consent.","Children's data needs parental consent; no tracking or targeted advertising.","Report breaches to the Board within 72 hours with full account.","a Penalties reach Rs 250 crore per breach; voluntary disclosure and cooperation count."],"quote":"This is not IT. This is enterprise wide.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-28.mp4","videoSecs":497,"notes":"media/notes/class-28-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"India's DPDP Act is what kind of regime?","a":"Civil, not criminal, but an enterprise-wide risk","w":["Criminal, with prison terms","Voluntary guidelines only","An IT department policy"],"x":"'This is not IT. This is enterprise wide.'"},{"q":"Within how long must a data breach be reported to the Data Protection Board?","a":"72 hours (or an approved extension)","w":["30 days","One year","Only if customers complain"],"x":"Include cause, mitigation, who caused it, prevention and proof affected people were told."},{"q":"What is the maximum penalty for failing to keep reasonable security safeguards?","a":"Up to Rs 250 crore","w":["Up to Rs 10,000","Up to Rs 50 crore","Up to Rs 1,000 crore"],"x":"Breach not notified or children's data duties: up to Rs 200 crore each."},{"q":"Which is NOT a requirement for valid consent under DPDP?","a":"Bundled with unrelated services","w":["Free","Specific","Withdrawable"],"x":"Consent must be free, specific, informed, unambiguous and withdrawable."},{"q":"Who gives, reviews and withdraws consent on a registered platform?","a":"A consent manager","w":["A data processor","The Data Protection Board","TDSAT"],"x":"The data fiduciary appoints a DPO and may use processors."},{"q":"Cross-border transfers under DPDP are...","a":"Allowed except to a negative list of countries the government notifies","w":["Completely banned","Allowed only to the EU","Allowed only with court approval"],"x":"Exemptions cover crime prevention, courts and certain state functions."}],"cards":[{"f":"Data principal","b":"The individual the data is about (a parent for a child)."},{"f":"Data fiduciary","b":"Decides purpose and means of processing; appoints a DPO."},{"f":"Legitimate use","b":"Lawful processing without consent, e.g. emergencies, legal duties, state benefits."},{"f":"Grievance path","b":"Fiduciary, then Data Protection Board, then TDSAT, then Supreme Court."},{"f":"Children's data","b":"Verifiable parental consent; no tracking or targeted ads."},{"f":"Duties of data principals","b":"No impersonation, no false or frivolous complaints; penalty up to Rs 10,000."},{"f":"DPDP timeline","b":"Act 2023, rules notified 2025, compliance due by 2027."}]},{"id":"c29","num":29,"module":"governance","title":"AI Strategy and Organisational AI Governance","subtitle":"Use Cases and ROI, Responsible AI, AI Safety Institutes and the Hourglass Governance Model","date":"18 October 2026","faculty":"Faculty: Programme Co-Director (strategy and governance)","workshop":false,"hook":"AI is like dynamite: it can build or destroy, depending on intent and control. An AI strategy decides where AI creates value and how it will be measured, while AI governance, owned by the board and executive team, makes sure it stays ethical, legal and accountable from design to retirement.","sections":[{"h":"Why AI needs both strategy and governance","blocks":[{"p":"Like Nobel&#x27;s dynamite, AI can do great good or harm. It is transforming drug discovery, clinical care and science, but <strong>safety benchmarks lag and AI incidents are rising sharply</strong>. Investment is concentrated in the US, whose pull on global talent is weakening."},{"p":"Adoption is spreading at historic speed, often through free tools. Participants noted paid conversion stays low because of cost, while banks restrict tools."},{"p":"Vendors retiring models within weeks worried participants: established firms give two to three years&#x27; notice, newer AI labs much less. Build <strong>deprecation into the life cycle</strong> and your contracts."}]},{"h":"Crafting an AI strategy","blocks":[{"p":"An AI strategy says how AI will support business goals: which functions (customer, manufacturing, supply chain, fraud, employees), enterprise-wide or functional, and why. Look <strong>360, even 720 degrees</strong>: strategy to operations, regulatory, risk, ethical and societal."},{"p":"Start with an <strong>AI policy</strong>; then pick use cases, infrastructure, cost, benefits and risks. Organisations begin with low-risk use cases that will not fail. Define the ROI measure up front."},{"p":"Participants shared results: <strong>30% less effort</strong> in loan documentation, AI support agents, and physics-based AI cutting engine inspection reports from eight days to ten minutes. Publish such wins as papers or white papers."},{"img":"assets/figures/c29-1.png","title":"BUILDING AN AI STRATEGY","alt":"AI policy Objectives Use cases Business case Risks and ethics Principles and Insight, efficiency, Enterprise, Infrastructure, cost, Bias, transparency, guardrails first supply chain, talent, functional or benefits, ROI regulation, society customers geographic; low-risk measures"}]},{"h":"Responsible AI and safety institutes","blocks":[{"p":"Leading firms publish <strong>responsible AI standards</strong> and require ethics committee clearance before production. Empathy, bias control, transparency and accountability are core: if a customer asks in court why an application was denied, you must explain it."},{"p":"UK, US, Singapore and India have set up <strong>AI safety institutes</strong> for model evaluation, risk research and standards. India&#x27;s, under the IndiaAI Mission, follows a hub-and-spoke model with academia, industry and startups."},{"img":"assets/figures/c29-2.png","title":"RESPONSIBLE AI PRINCIPLES LEADERS PUBLISH","alt":"1Empathy 2Bias control 3Transparency Weigh societal impact, not just tech Examine training data for skew and Explain the logic behind outcomes and money human bias 4Accountability 5Ethics committees 6Safety institutes Named owners answerable for results Clearance before production UK, US, Singapore, India test and set standards"}]},{"h":"AI governance and the hourglass model","blocks":[{"p":"<strong>AI governance</strong> is a set of responsibilities and practices exercised by the board and executive management to set direction, ensure objectives are met, manage risk and use resources responsibly."},{"p":"The <strong>hourglass model</strong> (University of Turku) turns ethical principles and laws into operational processes across three layers: the <strong>environment</strong> (laws, standards, stakeholders), the <strong>organisation</strong> (mission, strategy, values, capabilities) and the <strong>AI system</strong>, with <strong>67 tasks</strong> mapped to the OECD life cycle."},{"p":"Keep an <strong>AI system and algorithm repository</strong>, like an asset register, covering in-house and procured models and agents, each numbered and owned."},{"img":"assets/figures/c29-3.png","title":"THE HOURGLASS MODEL OF ORGANISATIONAL AI GOVERNANCE","alt":"Environmental layer: laws, standards, stakeholders Hourglass 67 tasks mapped to OECD life Organisational layer: strategy, cycle stagesmodel values, capabilities AI system layer: life-cycle governance tasks"}]},{"h":"Governing the life cycle and accountability","blocks":[{"p":"At design, register the system, assign an owner, document use case, constraints and architecture. Set <strong>pre-</strong> <strong>deployment metrics</strong> aligned to risk tolerance; lab and production differ, so test on your own test bed, not just a vendor&#x27;s synthetic data."},{"p":"In operation, use version control and roll back when metrics breach. <strong>Accountability stays with the CDAIO</strong>; responsibility sits with developers. One measure of accuracy is the share of outputs a human approves unchanged. Writing requirements for AI will differ from traditional RFPs."},{"img":"assets/figures/c29-4.png","title":"GOVERNANCE TASKS ACROSS THE AI SYSTEM LIFE CYCLE","alt":"Plan and design Data and model Verify and Deploy Operate and validate monitor Register system, Algorithm repository, Pre-deployment Owner signs off Version control; roll owner, use case, data quality, risks metrics, own test bed impacts and targets back on breach architecture"}]}],"takeaways":["AI is dual-use: strong intent, controls and governance decide whether it helps or harms.","Set an AI policy first, then choose use cases by value, risk and readiness, starting with low-risk pilots.","Define ROI measures and risks up front; publish proven results to build credibility.","Plan for model deprecation in contracts and the life cycle.","Publish responsible AI principles and require ethics clearance before production.","Use the hourglass model: environment, organisation and AI system layers, with tasks across the life cycle.","Keep a repository of every AI system, algorithm and agent, each with an owner.","Accountability rests with the CDAIO; gate every move from lab to production with metrics and human review."],"quote":"Accountability always lies with the CDAIO; responsibility lies with the developer.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-29.mp4","videoSecs":534,"notes":"media/notes/class-29-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"Why is AI compared to dynamite?","a":"It can build or destroy depending on intent and control","w":["It is expensive","It was invented by Nobel","It only works once"],"x":"Strong intent, controls and governance decide whether it helps or harms."},{"q":"What should come first when crafting an AI strategy?","a":"An AI policy","w":["Buying GPUs","Hiring a large data team","Launching a moonshot"],"x":"Then objectives, use cases, business case, risks and ethics."},{"q":"What does the hourglass model of AI governance include?","a":"Environment, organisation and AI system layers, with 67 tasks mapped to the life cycle","w":["Only an ethics committee","A single risk register","Three lines of defence"],"x":"Developed at the University of Turku."},{"q":"Who holds accountability vs responsibility for an AI system?","a":"Accountability with the CDAIO; responsibility with developers","w":["Both with the vendor","Accountability with developers; responsibility with the CDAIO","Both with the board only"],"x":"'Accountability always lies with the CDAIO.'"},{"q":"How should firms handle vendors retiring models within weeks?","a":"Build deprecation into the life cycle and contracts","w":["Ignore it","Only use one vendor forever","Avoid AI entirely"],"x":"Established firms give 2-3 years' notice; newer labs much less."},{"q":"One measure of AI accuracy suggested in class is...","a":"The share of outputs a human approves unchanged","w":["Number of tokens generated","Model size","Response length"],"x":"Pre-deployment metrics should align to risk tolerance."}],"cards":[{"f":"AI governance definition","b":"Responsibilities and practices of the board and executives to set direction, meet objectives, manage risk and use resources responsibly."},{"f":"AI system repository","b":"Like an asset register: every in-house and procured model and agent, numbered and owned."},{"f":"AI safety institutes","b":"UK, US, Singapore and India; India's follows a hub-and-spoke model."},{"f":"Low-risk first","b":"Organisations begin with low-risk use cases that will not fail."},{"f":"Own test bed","b":"Lab and production differ, so test on your own data, not just vendor synthetic data."},{"f":"Roll back","b":"Use version control and roll back when metrics breach."},{"f":"Responsible AI essentials","b":"Empathy, bias control, transparency, accountability, ethics committee clearance."}]},{"id":"c30","num":30,"module":"governance","title":"India AI Mission and the EU AI Act","subtitle":"Adoption Approach, Chief AI Officer Role, India's Legal Landscape and Risk-Based AI Regulation","date":"25 October 2026","faculty":"Faculty: Programme Co-Director (strategy and governance)","workshop":false,"hook":"There is no silver bullet for AI adoption: start experimenting, fail early and build trust. India is laying national foundations through the IndiaAI Mission and existing laws, while the EU AI Act, the first comprehensive AI law, regulates AI by risk and reaches any firm whose AI is used in the Union.","sections":[{"h":"How organisations should approach AI now","blocks":[{"p":"Look at <strong>internal</strong> uses (productivity, skills, infrastructure protection), <strong>external</strong> uses (customers, regulatory affairs, insights) and, later, <strong>sovereign</strong> AI. There is no silver bullet: <strong>start experimenting and</strong> <strong>fail early</strong>, beginning internally where risk is lower."},{"p":"Human <strong>in</strong> the loop is expected today; human <strong>on</strong> the loop is the next step towards autonomous agents. Leaders need a fair grasp of every moving part: data, infrastructure, governance, business and risk. Participants warned the 67-task governance model could slow agility. The faculty advised scaling governance with maturity: one adopter prioritises culture and comfort first, adding metrics as AI scales."}]},{"h":"The rise of the Chief AI Officer","blocks":[{"p":"A large Indian bank has advertised for a <strong>Chief AI Officer</strong>, a first for the country, and other banks are following. It is not a deep-tech role but one that works across functions and experts."},{"p":"India may soon draft its own AI Act. Expect <strong>transparency, explainability, contestability and</strong> <strong>accountability</strong> to become legal requirements."}]},{"h":"India's national AI foundations","blocks":[{"p":"The <strong>IndiaAI Mission</strong> builds compute as a service, shared datasets, Indian foundation models, applications, skills and safe, trusted AI. Organisations face the same choices: LLM or SLM, build or buy, internal or customer-facing."},{"p":"Treat AI as an <strong>investment</strong>, not a narrow cost-benefit sum. If one or two of every hundred trained staff find a breakthrough use case, it pays. Participants described month-long AI and cyber training drives. The <strong>RBI&#x27;s seven sutras</strong> put trust first: without trust, innovation and adoption stagnate."},{"img":"assets/figures/c30-1.png","title":"PILLARS OF THE INDIAAI MISSION","alt":"1Compute 2Datasets 3Foundation models Scalable GPU capacity offered as a Shared, including synthetic, data for Indian LLMs and SLMs on Indian service experiments languages 4Applications 5Skills and startups 6Safe and trusted AI AI for critical sectors and public Capacity building, talent and funding Governance, risk and security good frameworks"}]},{"h":"India's legal landscape for AI","blocks":[{"p":"No AI-specific law yet, but many apply: <strong>DPDP</strong> governs the data and input stage, the Consumer Protection Act covers misleading &#x27;AI-enabled&#x27; claims and AI-driven harm, plus the IT Act, BNS, Telecommunications Act and <strong>sector regulators</strong>. Map laws by sector and geography."},{"p":"Ministries such as MeitY, Home and External Affairs and DoT shape AI governance. Organisations can mirror this with steering committees and AI advisory groups. Protecting IP and ideas from cloning is a growing worry."},{"img":"assets/figures/c30-2.png","title":"INDIAN LAWS ALREADY TOUCHING AI","alt":"IT Act Sector regulators: RBI, SEBI, DPDP Act (data and input) IRDAI AI in India Telecommunications Act Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita Consumer Protection Act"}]},{"h":"The EU AI Act","blocks":[{"p":"The <strong>first comprehensive legal framework for AI</strong>, phased in, aims to build trust and give innovators legal certainty. It applies to providers, deployers, importers and distributors <strong>inside or outside the EU</strong> whenever AI output is used in the Union."},{"p":"Prohibited practices include social scoring and certain biometric identification and profiling. <strong>High-risk</strong> systems face strict duties from <strong>2 December 2027</strong>; general-purpose AI rules took effect in August 2025. Classifying and owning every AI system matters because the owner must understand its risk."},{"img":"assets/figures/c30-3.png","title":"EU AI ACT: FOUR RISK LEVELS","alt":"Minimal Limited High Unacceptable No new rules, e.g. games, Transparency: disclose AI Strict duties before Banned, e.g. social scoring spam filters and AI content entering the market"}]}],"takeaways":["There is no silver bullet: start experimenting, fail early, and begin with lower-risk internal use cases.","Move from human in the loop towards human on the loop only as trust and controls mature.","Scale governance with maturity so it does not kill agility.","Chief AI Officer roles are emerging in India; they are cross-functional, not purely technical.","The IndiaAI Mission builds compute, data, models, skills and trusted AI; trust underpins adoption.","Existing Indian laws, from DPDP to consumer protection, already apply to AI.","The EU AI Act regulates by risk and reaches any firm whose AI output is used in the EU.","High-risk AI needs risk management, quality data, logging, documentation and human oversight."],"quote":"Without trust, innovation and adoption will stagnate.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-30.mp4","videoSecs":515,"notes":"media/notes/class-30-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"What is the next step after 'human in the loop'?","a":"Human on the loop","w":["Human out of the loop immediately","No humans at all","Human in the cloud"],"x":"Move towards autonomy only as trust and controls mature."},{"q":"Which is an 'unacceptable risk' under the EU AI Act?","a":"Social scoring","w":["Spam filters","Video game AI","Chatbots that disclose they are AI"],"x":"Unacceptable risk practices are banned."},{"q":"Who does the EU AI Act apply to?","a":"Providers, deployers, importers and distributors whenever AI output is used in the EU","w":["Only EU-based AI startups","Only government agencies","Only general-purpose model makers"],"x":"It has extraterritorial reach like GDPR."},{"q":"Which Indian law covers misleading 'AI-enabled' claims?","a":"The Consumer Protection Act","w":["The Telecommunications Act","The DPDP Act","None"],"x":"DPDP governs the data and input stage; the IT Act and BNS also apply."},{"q":"When do strict duties for high-risk AI systems apply under the EU AI Act?","a":"From 2 December 2027","w":["They already applied in 2018","August 2030","They are voluntary"],"x":"General-purpose AI rules took effect in August 2025."},{"q":"What do the RBI's seven sutras put first?","a":"Trust","w":["Speed","Profit","Automation"],"x":"'Without trust, innovation and adoption will stagnate.'"}],"cards":[{"f":"EU AI Act risk levels","b":"Minimal, limited (transparency), high (strict duties), unacceptable (banned)."},{"f":"IndiaAI Mission pillars","b":"Compute, datasets, foundation models, applications, skills and startups, safe and trusted AI."},{"f":"No silver bullet","b":"Start experimenting, fail early, begin with lower-risk internal use cases."},{"f":"Scale governance with maturity","b":"Heavy governance too early can kill agility; add metrics as AI scales."},{"f":"AI as investment","b":"If 1-2 of every 100 trained staff find a breakthrough use case, training pays."},{"f":"Chief AI Officer in India","b":"A large Indian bank advertised the first CAIO role; it's cross-functional, not deep-tech."},{"f":"Likely future legal requirements","b":"Transparency, explainability, contestability and accountability."}]},{"id":"c31","num":31,"module":"governance","title":"Building a Scalable Enterprise Data Strategy","subtitle":"Defence and Offence, Data as a Product, Federated Governance, Metadata and Data Quality","date":"1 November 2026","faculty":"Faculty: Guest faculty (data science and analytics leader)","workshop":false,"hook":"Companies do not lack data; they have too much data that is untrusted, unknown and unused. A scalable data strategy turns data into a trusted, reusable asset by balancing defence and offence, naming owners, governing what matters, and measuring quality at the source, because AI runs on trust and there is no trustworthy AI without trusted data.","sections":[{"h":"Why data strategy matters now","blocks":[{"p":"The problem is not too little data; it is <strong>untrusted, unknown and unused</strong> data. Poor quality and unclear ownership act as a tax on every decision. A data strategy makes data a <strong>trusted, reusable asset</strong> so the organisation decides on evidence, not gut."},{"p":"Participants noted healthcare struggles to capture data from clinicians and biomedical devices, while e-commerce captures every click and then hunts for insight, inverting the funnel."},{"img":"assets/figures/c31-1.png","title":"THE TWO JOBS OF A DATA STRATEGY","alt":"Defence: protect and comply Offence: create value Security, privacy and regulation Insights and faster decisions Single source of truth Customer and product analytics Quality and lineage controlsvs Self-service for business users Reduces risk Grows revenue and efficiency"}]},{"h":"Six pillars and how to build the strategy","blocks":[{"p":"Decide <strong>which data is worth capturing</strong>: if storage is cheap, capture it; otherwise stage it by value. Decide platform and vendor risk too: a cloud outage in October took down a UK fintech running on a single provider."},{"p":"Culture and literacy decide use: dashboards built for one executive often die within months. Start by <strong>listening</strong> to the C-suite on revenue, risk and efficiency, assess current data use, then prove value with a small, high-ROI MVP and scale."},{"img":"assets/figures/c31-2.png","title":"SIX PILLARS OF A DATA STRATEGY","alt":"1Value 2Ownership 3Governance Which data is worth capturing, at Who owns, stewards and runs each data Policies, access, privacy, metadata what cost? set? 4Quality 5Platform 6Culture and literacy Fit for use, measured at source Architecture, vendors, single or Can people actually use it? multi-cloud"}]},{"h":"Data as a product","blocks":[{"p":"Treat data like a product with a <strong>product owner</strong> accountable for value and risk, a <strong>data steward</strong> for meaning, rules and quality, and a <strong>custodian</strong> who keeps pipelines running. Good data products are discoverable, trustworthy and reusable."},{"p":"Ownership changes behaviour: owners listen to users and keep improving. Participants debated whether one chief data officer should own all data; the faculty favoured <strong>one umbrella</strong> with domain owners beneath, to avoid silos and too many cooks."},{"img":"assets/figures/c31-3.png","title":"DATA AS A PRODUCT: ROLES AND QUALITIES","alt":"Owner: accountable for value and risk Steward: meaning, rules, Reusable quality Data product Custodian: pipelines and Trustworthy platform Discoverable"}]},{"h":"Federated governance and metadata","blocks":[{"p":"A <strong>federated model</strong> works best: a central core sets standards and guards a single source of truth, while domains own and productise their data under those rules, with regular check-ins. It scales as the company grows."},{"p":"<strong>Metadata</strong> is data about data, like a library card catalogue: where data came from, what it means, who owns it. Photos carry location metadata, which is why celebrities strip it before posting. Policies say what to do with data, such as masking PII; metadata describes it."},{"img":"assets/figures/c31-4.png","title":"FROM LISTENING TO SCALE","alt":"Listen Assess Prioritise MVP Scale C-suite goals: Data types, culture, High-ROI, short-cycle e.g. mortgage Extend to other revenue, risk, workflows use case underwriting lines; literacy grows efficiency assistant"}]},{"h":"Quality, and why AI depends on it","blocks":[{"p":"Data must be <strong>fit for use and measured at the source</strong>. Leading firms keep a certified layer with quality scores, lineage and self-serve discovery, as at Netflix and HDFC Bank&#x27;s single view of the customer. <strong>Data strategy is now AI strategy.</strong> No trusted data, no trustworthy AI. One client wanted AI, but an audit showed data was missing or incomplete, so the first step was fixing capture. Put strategy before technology and govern the data that matters."},{"img":"assets/figures/c31-5.png","title":"DIMENSIONS OF DATA QUALITY","alt":"1Accuracy 2Completeness 3Consistency Reflects reality No missing values that matter Same answer across systems Timeliness Uniqueness 456Validity Available when needed, even real time No duplicates Fits defined formats and rules"}]}],"takeaways":["The problem is not too little data but data that is untrusted, unknown and unused.","Balance defence (protect and comply) with offence (create value).","Start by listening to business goals, then prove value with a small, high-ROI MVP.","Treat data as a product with a named owner, steward and custodian.","Use federated governance: central standards, domain ownership, one source of truth.","Use metadata to make data findable, understandable and safe to use.","Measure quality at the source across accuracy, completeness, consistency, timeliness and uniqueness.","Data strategy is now AI strategy: no trusted data, no trustworthy AI."],"quote":"No trusted data, no trustworthy AI.","quoteBy":"","video":"media/videos/class-31.mp4","videoSecs":482,"notes":"media/notes/class-31-summary.pdf","readMins":3,"quiz":[{"q":"What is the core data problem most companies face?","a":"Too much data that is untrusted, unknown and unused","w":["Too little data","Too few dashboards","Too many data scientists"],"x":"Poor quality and unclear ownership act as a tax on every decision."},{"q":"Data defence vs offence: which is offence?","a":"Creating value through insights and faster decisions","w":["Security and privacy","Regulatory compliance","Lineage controls"],"x":"A data strategy balances both."},{"q":"In 'data as a product', who keeps pipelines running?","a":"The custodian","w":["The owner","The steward","The auditor"],"x":"Owner: value and risk; steward: meaning, rules and quality."},{"q":"Which governance model did the class favour for data?","a":"Federated: central standards, domain ownership","w":["Fully centralised in IT","Every team for itself","Outsourced to vendors"],"x":"It scales as the company grows, with one source of truth."},{"q":"What is metadata?","a":"Data about data, like a library card catalogue","w":["Encrypted data","Deleted data","Data stored in the cloud"],"x":"Where data came from, what it means and who owns it."},{"q":"Which is a dimension of data quality?","a":"Timeliness","w":["Popularity","Volume","Colour"],"x":"Others: accuracy, completeness, consistency, uniqueness, validity."}],"cards":[{"f":"Six pillars of data strategy","b":"Value, ownership, governance, quality, platform, culture and literacy."},{"f":"Good data products are...","b":"Discoverable, trustworthy and reusable."},{"f":"Policies vs metadata","b":"Policies say what to do with data (e.g. mask PII); metadata describes it."},{"f":"Where to measure quality","b":"At the source."},{"f":"Path from listening to scale","b":"Listen, assess, prioritise, MVP, scale."},{"f":"Certified data layer","b":"Quality scores, lineage and self-serve discovery (Netflix, HDFC Bank)."},{"f":"'No trusted data, no trustworthy AI'","b":"Data strategy is now AI strategy."}]}]};
